      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>milw.ft" Mc DOUAI, L'S PATENT SELF- it AISIN FIA)t . THITAST</head>
        <p>milw.ft" Mc DOUAI, L'S PATENT SELF- it AISIN FIA)t . THITAST</p>
        <p>IJADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIB,</p>
        <p>The lightness of a tulle or chiffon or gauze head. gear is in itself a very strong recommendation, and, in addition, the plastic character of such a toque gives it an advantage over those of heavier materials. A swirl of chiffon arranged on a net foundation can be pinned on at any angle, pressed in here, curved out there, and otherwise suited to the style of the wearer. A velvet hat is often so heavy that, however carefully it may be adjusted, the slightest touch throws it out of gear, and the crookedness of it throws an air of jauntiness about the wearer which is wholly destructive of dignity. The flower toque is to be as fashionable as ever this spring, and one or two have already been seen in the fashionable promenades. Very often the crown is composed of the foliage of the flower that covers the brim; or the entire brim is made of rose stems .with small green leaves and half-opened buds, while full-blown roses form the crown.</p>
        <p>Whet the trimmings bestowed upon a dress are very nevs and lovely they immediately lift it above the ordinary toilette. The most effective used at this moment are tassels. Not too many appear on a frock, but just enough to emphasise the effect tc be produced. Sailor collars are finished in front wfth a couple of big tassels of gold thread and silk falling from a passamenterie medallion to match ; string tinted lace revers are all over tufted with tiny ball tassels depended from little cords, boleros are edged with them in clusters, and always in their company appear very handsome buttons, among which are vastly fashionable wooden discs well padded, and then covered with velvet or cloth rimmed with gold, steel, or silver beads. Al] the gold that is used this winter is noticed to be making a miniature effect instead of a bold one, and takes the form of needle-run threads in lace, delicate embroideries on buttons, belts of tissue and tassels. Silver is being much less used than steel, but I hear that pearl.passementerie is about to become very popular.</p>
        <p>An excellent dress is in nut-brown tweed, strapped and stitched on both skirt and bodice. The latter is coat shape, fitting closely at the back, and finished with a turndown collar, ove: which falls the collar of the waistcoat in figured panne. The Bishop sleeves are caught in at the wrists with a strap of the cloth buttoned over. This gown would look very well in cloth witn rows of brown and gold braid wherever the strap. pings are shown, and finished round the hem with stitchings to match. A wide fur collar and cuffs can be used to make a very smart bolero. In tabac-brown cloth and brown fur, with small copper buttons, and the cloth tabs stitched with brown silk, it is very smart ; or black cloth with grey or white fur would be very attractive; ox sable-marmot or mink would be very stylish on brown or black, with steel buttens and cascades of ivory or lace.</p>
        <p>Tulle toques, are very like gossr er, and are tucked with patient care to make them still more ethereally light and bewitching than when merely swathed. A black one that has tucks stitched with white silk and a wreath of white jessamine on the edge of the brim looks charming. Leaf wreaths are still seen, and flowers like ranunculus and snowdrops whisper of spring.</p>
        <p>Spots appear on many of the new materials for dress and millinery. There is quite a rage for \Lem, especially for light spots on dark. When they were in last they were dark on light, such as black on white or pink or scarlet. A grey gown has the turn-down collar in dark grey velvet spotted with white, the material of the gown being strapped all round thc. edge. The mousquetair 9 uffi match the collar. A very pronounced shade af reddish brown is coming in. It ;s a russet with much yellow-brown in its composition. Brunettes would rejoice in it. Some of it is peppered closely over with white spots, which have the effect of making it very becoming. A gown of this stuff is made with a double skirt and a deep-basqued coat, 30 that the effect is as of three flounces of equal depth. In velvet this is a good colour, but blonde or a faded woman should never be seen within yarils of it.</p>
        <p>Your loving, JBZINIZ.</p>
        <p>Cardinal Ciasca died the other evening from heart disease at Rome. A 304ni10 cycle race, in which none of the riders will weigh less than 16 stone, has been arranged in Paris for May 8. Throughout the whole of January the wireless telegraph apparatus on the Ostend boats worked on one day only.</p>
