      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>MOST NUTRITIOUS.</head>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
        <p>P PS'S</p>
        <p>GRATEFUL-COMFORTNG.</p>
        <p>COCOA</p>
        <p>ZREAKFAST--SUPPEF't</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Many leading women in Society will make a point of entertaining this season, and Londonderry House,which is one of the largest and best appointed mansions in London, will be the scene of many brilliant gatherings. The Marchioness of Londonderry id giving a grand reception there on February 19th, and later on the nails and dinner parties at the mansion in Parklane, will be amongst the leading events of the Coronation year. The Duchess of Devonshire, and the Duchess of Sutherland, will also give some splendid entertainments, and the Countess of Warwick assisted by her daughter, Lady Marjorie Greville, will he amongst the most beautiful and distinguished of London hostesses this season. Lord Crewe has just acquired Wharncliffe House for a town residence. It is one of the finest and most noble of London's historic mansions, standing in ice own grounds and Lord Crewe is said to have given over £90,000 for the property. It is not far from Lord ROSE- bery's hence in Berkeley Square, and, in all probability, Wharncliffe House. with Lady Crewe as its mistress, will be the scene of many brilliant entertainments, both of a social and political nature, connected with the fertuues of the Liberal Party. The late Mr Sidney Cooper's pictures alwaysattracted a geod deal of attention at the Royal Academy, both on account of their merit, and the fact that this dietin- Relished artist had been a regular exhibitor there since 1840. Mr Cooper was within a year and a half of being a century old, and yet, such was his wonderful sight, that be was able to work in his painting room almost to the last, and his pictures at the Academy, at the last two exhibitions, all fetched ingh prices, Mr Cooper's boyhood was one of comparative poverty and hardship, and, until getting on towa•ds middle age, his life was a constant struggle with adverse fortune, be often being reduced to painting theatre scenery, or carriage wheele, for a living. Finally be came out as one of our best painters of landscapes and animals, and altogether Mr Cooper exhibited nearly 300 pictures at the Royal Academy, during his long and distinguished career as an artist. It has often'been observed that those children with a natural aptitude for drawing are clever in other ways, and there is a reason for this, as drawing teaches one to think, and thus develoPea the mind. The experiment is being tried in POMO schools in Aieerica, of teaching nothing but drawing during the e'ementary stages of education, and, it is stated that the children afterwards matstsr ether studies, such as reading and writing,ete., much mere readily and efficiently than those who begin with the usual. couree of instruction. Many familiar with the subject of elementary education will not be surprised at this result, and a similar experiment might well be tried at some of our schools. VfiThe marriage of Miss Alice Grosvenor, with Mr Ivor Churchill Guret, was almost the last in the rush of Society weddings before Lent, and now wedding bells will be hushed until after Easter. The Hon Ivor Guest, is, of course, the eldest on and heir of Lord Wimberne, whilst his bride is the second daughter of Lord Ebury, and a cousin of the Duke of Westminster. Nearly all the leading families in Society were represented at the wedding, which was a very praty one,end both bride and bridegroom received a very large number of wedding presents. Many of the fashionable weddings this year leave been carried out on an exceedingly elaborate and costly scale. There has been a sort of rivalsai, in such matters as the decoration of the church, the rateeption, and above all, in the show of wedding presents. On some occasions the display has been of a rather ostentatious character, arid at one wedding after another efforts appear to have been made to &amp;Aim a record as regards the number and veins, of wedding gifts. This has led to a protest in society circles, against the practice of giving presents, which when carried to extremes becomes a tax and burden upon people. The Bing appears to share this opinion, einem at one or two fashionable weddings, His Majesty instead of sending some article of jewellery, has merely sent a gift of flowers. With the commencement of Lent, the usual period of stagnation between the seasons sets in, when it is diffi- cult to speak with confidence of the forthcoming mcdee. The winter gaieties are over but the dressmakers and milliners all pretend that it is too early yet to say what particular styles the coming season will bring forth. One says pretends, because the leading business houses settle all these matters beforehand, but they do not give out the information until the proper time arrives. Last year rrapy fabrics ran threugh spring, summer, autumn, and winter, and though they may perhaps he somewhat less in favour, as the spring and snmmer advencee, yet there is little doubt that they will be well worn. Amazingly strong are the majority of these I crany materials for all their delicate aspect, and, gener- ally speaking, very soft and becoming. Fancy silk tassels, hanging from rosettes of stitched glace, edged with a fancy (salon, are in great demand for evening dresses built of crepy fahrics, as also are flowere in raised, delicately-tinted chiffon, showing stems and foliage in ribbon -embroidery. Belts for evening gowns are usually of material in folds, cut on Isla.. which when nulled down to suit the wearer, can be fastened with jewelled pine. 'Quite a novelty in the millinery world is chrysanthe- mum straw, which has the aepearance of the petals of the Japanese verietr, massed together. Hats and toques of this straw are teine lavishly trimmed with flowers and tulle, and many show a drapery of fare at the back, which falls over the hair. The entire flower toque is still to tee fore, wallflowers, anemones, primulas, and roses being in especial request for them. Rarely have the flower manufacturer' enjoyed such a busy and sure- ly prosperous time. For evening gowns trails and garnitures are lavishly need, while the small wreath of leaves, or leaves and buds, are being worn by all fashionable girte, and many smart married women. The reappearance of flowered silk waistcoats helps to serve two greed ends—the revival of brocaded fabrics, and the employment of skilled embroidery hands. Then they afford an excellent opportunity for the exhibition of a choice at of buttons. Very little of such a waist- csat is seen, save when the coat is thrown back, but this fact in no wise seems to be taken into consideration, by the matters or wearers. It is whispered that with more genial weather coats will'disaPpear in favour of barqued bodices, but whether this is to be SO remains a mystery at present. In "The World and Winstow " (Hodder and Stoursh- ton, 64) we have a work of fietion that may well he elmeed among the most successful, and delightful of the past season, and Miss Edith Henri( t`a Fowler's latest circle of readers will be fervently hoping that her forth- coming marriage will in no way interfere with her literary career. Here and there, in thin, Mies E H Fowler's latest work, one is reminded of the manner of her perhaps mere widely-known sister Miss Ellen Thernycreft Fowler. The marriage of both sisters, so rrrcour says, is to take ylice en the same day. MA DOE</p>
        <p>AREPRESENTATIVE INSTANCE.—The experience of a Preston lady publistei in another coiumn of his issue is a fair sample of similar cases in all parts of till country, which are coming t o light daily, where remarkable cures have been effected by ths use of D, Williams' pink pills for pale people. It is on efficial record that these pills have cured paralysis, loccmotor ataxy, rheumatism, and sciatica ; also all diseases arising from impnverishment of the blood, scrofula, rioketa, chronic erysipelas, consumption of the boweP and lungs, ar Demi*, pale and sallow cemplexion,general muscular wesalyeas, lops of arpetite, pplpitaticns, pains in the back, nervous headache and neuralgia, earls" decay, all forms of female woakness, and laretetia. These pills are a tonic, not a purgative ; they are genuine only with the full name. Dr Williams' Pink Pills for Palo People, and are arid ho chemisti and by Dr Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn Viadnct. London, E.C., at 24 9d a box, or Fix boxes for 13s 91. rink Ole Fold Tante or from glass jails arc not D, Williams' Pink Pills ; accept them only in the pink closed wrapper as above dercribed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>RHODESIA.</head>
        <p>RHODESIA.</p>
        <p>The most striking feature of the past week has been the growing favour of Rhodesian shares, and there has been a rise in the price of nearly everything in that market. The surcnssful flotation of the Charter T 1135 opens a new chapter in the history of Rhodesia'A development, as it will supply the necessary Working Capital for developing many valuable properties already owned by English Companies, among which the Austen (Owen) Development Syndicate, Limited, is second t' none, taking into con sideratinn the fact of the smallness of its capital, which is only £50,000, in £1 fully raid up sharer, and that it owns two farms of 6,000 acree each and 507 claims in the best known and most important gold-mining districts of Ithodeeia, including Sebakwe, Selukwe, rtanombie, and Gwelc. Jut before the war, Austen "Gweb) shares stood at double thr ir present price, and were expected by those in the know to go much higher, the reports received from the Manager at the mines being at a highly favour- able character. On one set of 27 claims, known as the Scouts' Reef property, upon the development of which the Company has already spent nearly £14.000, thr quartz ill No 3 Shaft, at a depth of 85 feet, has given an average assay of 4ezr. 63 wt. of gold per too, the average assay of 1,817 feet rtrike of reef showing 18 dwts 20gre line gold per ton. Many of the other claim, contain ancient workings, and are close to some of the beet proved gold mines in Rholeaia. From the xbov e, it can be readily seen that these shares are well worth the attention ef tte Speculative investor.</p>
        <p>Salmon fishing opened on Thursday on Ihe Don and the Dee. and the other Scottish rivere. The beginning, however. was a poor one owing to the ice-bound cot^ diction of many of the rivers. The lower and faster flowing reache3 are not frezen, but conditions are not favourable fir fishing. The coast stations had fairly good hauls, ut net fishing on the rivers yiel led little result% Salmon generally are reported very plentiful The price was 23 61 to 2i 7d per lb.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>IHE EXAMINER FWD FEW:UARY 14 1002</head>
        <p>IHE EXAMINER FWD FEW:UARY 14 1002</p>
        <p>A PLUCKY POLICEMAN'S ADVENTURE. Dived into Liquid Poison. To Rescue a Suicide. A story true in very detail, and as exciting and sensa- tional as any ever written in novel form, has just been told to a representative of the London "Sun by an ex-police officer of the Metropolitan force. His tame is Alexander Robert Heffernan, ot 53, Archel-road, F age of 36, finds himself a poullbeaem p, : a etshiornert, bravery from the the early holder of a certificato for Royal Humane Society. He is a most extraordinary man, as the following details of his career will show. Said he to an interviewer:— quite a soldier's family, sisters is the wife of a I ‘m. We —the Useoff g—lderiecta'nsdaarueghter and each of my soldier, I was in the North Staffs Infantry Regimen t—lestter known as "the 64th " for twelve yeare, and went through the second Zulu campaign, during which, on one occasion. Cetewayo's son, with an enor- mous army of gigantic black warriors, made is run. It was a good ob we did too, for we ore fearfully out- _ombered, and had we not retreated, .._ .6 would not have been one of us left alive. However, in the end we came out all right ; and in IE9I I came home from Africa, and joined the Metropolitan police force. " I was always a good athlete. They made me one of the T Division Metropolitan Police Cricket Team the very year I joined. I bowled in every match, usually taking the largest number of wickets, and I made the record number of top scores. The District Railway Cricket Team also used to get me to play for them. At Stamford Bridge Athlete grounds I won a number of prizes as an all-round athlete in various dabs that hold their meeting there, and won so many events at the police sports that I was eventually handicapped cut of existence so as to speak, in order to give others a chencs. I was made to give 24 yards in the 100 yards sprint, and 1360 in the mils. " As a swimmer I woo many competitions, heating all comers in the City Police swimming events. In con- eequence of my successes I was asked to compete against the two crack swimmers of the Police of all England—Drake and Bryare, of the Manchester Force. I did so, and I beat Brous, Drake however, was a little too good for me. fie was a masterpiece ; and I believe is still the crack swimmer of the English police. By beating Bryars I gained the positicn of second best." "And how did you come to have to be pensioned off?" queried the reporter. " At'," replied the ex-con- etable. "It was all through that affair in the Regent's Canal. It was on the afternoon of February 26th, 1897. I was on duty, when I was told that a man had jumped into the Regent's Canal. Rushing to the place I threw off my overcoat, and dived in to rescue him. The water at that point is made very poisonous by the refuse from the chemical works and the gasworke in the district draining into it, and the chem,cals were fizzing and epluttering iv the water all around us as we strugg:Oo, I was struggling in the poisonous water for twenty micutcP, The Man cursed roe terrible i and contiwareo, to try and free himself in order to jurbto back into the water, even when I aet him oa the bank. It was some time before I oculd get my wet clothes ehanged, and I caught a terrible chill. But that was not the worst of it. The poison had got Into my system, and the 'tuff which killed the man I had rescued—for be died a few weeks after from poisonieg—nearly killed me too ! I began by being unable to digest my food, or even to keep it on my stomach. As soon as I bad taken a meal I vomited it all back again. Of course I was placed on the sick list, and the police doctor told no* that the poison in the water, which I had swallowed Mad completely destroyed the coating of my stomach. The police wrre very kind to me, and sent me down to the Convalescent Home at Brighton twice. After the second visit I had to go back to my awn home very bad indeed. I was so weak that when I attempted to walk I went blind, and had to cling to anything near me to prevent me from failing. I hod fearful night-sweats, and would often be seized with a kind of cramp, which seemed to be in my very bones. I could not retain ford er nouriehmer t, except in minute quantities—just enough to keep me alive. "I next went into Brompton Hospital, and there they declared it impossible to satisfactorily diagnose my ail- ments. For two years I remained in the police force, suffering in this way, and during the two years I was only able to put in three months deity ! Seeing that I was practically done for, in January, 1299, I was Riven a speciel pension, and retired from the force. (The rol- lowing is a copy of Pension certificate )</p>
        <p>5904</p>
        <p>)( I ' I nui there</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>EnoTint is good company for itself, at any rate. It sometimes happens that the reigning beauty is the cause of stormy times in her own house. ARTIST: Do you like things done in oil?" Misa New Corner: "Oh, yes, I adore sardines." ANSwF,RED.—Tom : "After all, what are kisses good for ? " Kitty (demurely) : "Their face value." CHILLING.—He: "I give myself to you.?' She: "Well, there's no use being so solemn over such a small present." "So they lock up the jury every night, do they?" " Yes • otherwiSe they might break loose and kill some o the lawyers." EDITOR: "Did you write these jokes yourself ? " Would-be Contributor: "Yes, sir." Editor: "You must be older than you look." THE CALLER: "I would like to see something in the way of a check." The Tailor: "Excuse me, are you a customer or a debt collector?" His ANSWER.—LittIe Clarence (a thoughtful lad( : "All things come to him who waits, don't they, pa ? " Mr. Callipers: "Yes—all things not worth waiting for." No OFFENCE MEANT.—Ctletemer : "I want a ' note-book—something that I can carry in my pocket to jot down my ideas in." Clerk: "Oh, you want something very small, then ? " PLEASING QUALITIES IN MAll.—" Which suitor are you going to accept, Clarissa?" "I can't decide, ma, which I like best. Harry is so timid, and Jack is so persistent." SHE: "Julie and Joe are engaged, but they have decided to keep their engagement a secret; Julie told me so." He: ‘• Yes, I know it, Joe told me." NEWRICH : "I want my daughter to be the most accomplished and well-bred young woman in the city." Lady Principal: "Are you willing to be separated from her?" CRAwFoRD: "You seem to have had a hard time at the races to-day." Crabshaw : "That is noth. ing to the time I'll have when I get home. The money I lost belonged to my wife. "I CAN'T understand how you can sell your brooms cheaper than I. I steal the handles and bristles and all I've got to do is to make 'em." Why I steal my brooms ready made." CAUSE FOR ALARIL—Doctor: "You say you think something is the matter with your wife ? " Anxious Husband: "I'm sure of it. She hasn't had the baby photographed for thirteen days." WHAT HE THOUGHT.—"I have pleaded the cause of my sex," said the New Woman, "in season and out of season." "You have," said her husband, emphatically; "especially out of season 1" JUST THE DIFFERENCE.—First Artist "An artist has as much right to sib upon the critics as the critics have to sit upon the artist." Second Artist : "Of course ; but the critics get paid for it and the artist doesn't." NATURALLY.—" Our treasury," said the chief financial secretary, "is suffering from an overplus of drafts." "No wonder," exclaimed the Chinese Emperor, with considerable petulance; "just think of all the open doors." NOTHING exasperates a woman who has been shading her eyes from the gaslight with her hand all the evening so much as to find that after all she has left her best diamond ring on the wash- stand.</p>
        <p>THE HEIRESS: "Am I the only girl in the whole wide world you love?" He: "No, dear ; but you are tho only girl I know who could afford to marry me." MR. DUMPSEY (who catches Johnny amongst his mother's fresh tarts) : "Look here, Johnny ! What are you up to?" Johnny (indistinctly): "Up to the ninth, pa; but they're awfully small." LITTLE JAMIE MARSHALL was observed poring over a book belonging to his mother, on the man- agement of children. "What are you doing with a book like that, Jamie?" asked Mrs. Marshall. "I want to see if I'm properly brought up, ma," replied the precocious youth. MIGHT BE WORSE.—"I say," said Hewitt to Blewitt, "you don't seem to growl about domestic matters as much as you used to do." "No," quoth Blewitt; "ever since I read that the King of Siam had six hundred wives I have been quite con- tent with my lot." Young CANDID (at the amateur threatricals): "Did you ever hear such horribly discordant, ear- splitting—" Old Proudfoot : " Sir-r 1 That's my eldest daughter, and—" Young Candid: "I repeat, sir, such ear-splitting clatter as the idiots behind us are making? Why, I can't hear a word of the song." " MY wife has a saving disposition," said Hicks. "When we got our upright piano she made a red plush cover for it so that the rosewood wouldn't get scratched. Then she covered that with a sorb of linen duster arrangement so as to save the plush. I tell you women have great minds I" ON DANGEROUS GROUND.—FIick: "Call him a musician ! Why, he doesn't know the difference between a nocturne and a symphony," Flack: "You don't mean it?" And they hurry to get away from one another. Each is terribly afraid that the other will ask: "By the way, what is the difference ? "</p>
        <p>HEARTLESS.—" I have here," said the caller, who entered the sanctum with three bows and a wave of his hand, "a few little poems that I jotted down in leisure moments, just for my own amnsement." The editor looked them over. "Wish our readers were as easily amused as you are," he snapped. "Good-day." " MY wife is a lecturer and I am an entertainer," said Nobbs. "Indeed. I knew your wife ap- peared in public, but I did not know that you ever did." "Oh, I don't ! I stay at home and enter- tain the baby." PEDAGOGUE (to history class): "Who is the Prince of Wales's mother?" Smart Boy: "Llan- dudno, sir." Pedagogne (angrily) : "Llandudno, air 1 What do you mean, sir?" "Please, sir, the guide-book says Llandudno is the Queen of Wales, and I thought the Queen of Wales would be the Prince of Wales's mother." STUMPS: "How well you're looking this mornin', Boone." Boone : " Yes—l never looked better in my life. I'm lookin' for a man who owes me £5."</p>
        <p>FAMILY FIEND: "I congratulate you, my dear sir, on the marriage of your daughter. I see you are gradually getting all the girls off your hands." Old Goldbranch : "Off my hands—yes; but the worst of it is I have to keep all their husbands on their feet." SAVED Jusr IN TlME.—Barber (running his hands through customer's hair): "Your head, sir, is quite "-- Customer (irritably : " You gave it a shampoo yourself two days ago." Barber recovering quickly): "Is quite a remarkably well- shaped head, sir." LADY OF THE HOUSE: "I should think a big, strong fellow like you would rather work than go round begging." Sam: I would if it wasn't for one thing." Lady: "What's that?" Sam: "I ain't got no fambly meself, and I'm afraid if I went to work I might crowd out some poor chap wot has little ones to support." "POOR Jarley—the boys played a great trick on him," said Bigelow. "Jarley went shooting for quail, but all he bagged was a rabbit. The boys stole the rabbit and filled the bag up with toast, and when he got home, he told Mrs. Jarley that that was all he got, emptying the contents of the bag on the floor. She'll never believe that Jarley was sober." "What did she say ? " "Noth- ing, except that she thought he must) have done his shooting in a restaurant." BIG BROTHER: "I should like to know what you have been flirting with that lunatic Saphead for?" Pretty Sister (indignantly): "I haven't." "Yes, you have. He told a friend that you stood before him ten minutes as if entranced, and you looked straight into his eyes as if you could read his very soul, and he said if ever ardent admira- tion shone in a human face it did in yours." "The idiot ! I was looking at my own reflection in his eye-glasses." EXCITED INDIVIDUAL: "See here, MT. Bangs, you're a scoundred of the first water. When I bought that horse I supposed I was getting a good sound animal, but he's spavined and blind and gob the staggers. Now, I want to know wnat you're going to do about it?" Bangs: "Something ought to be done, that's a fact." Excited Indi- vidual : " Well, I should say there ought." Bangs : " Well, I'll give you the name of a good veterinary surgeon; it's a shame to have the horse unfran in thsah</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha soap is a beautiful business,- because it makes .whole families happy and prosperous-- all that use it—comparatively.</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha soap Peninsular House Monument London E C</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>Metropolitan ipotice.</head>
        <p>Metropolitan ipotice.</p>
        <p>THIS is to certify that Alexander Robert .( Heffernan, K or Bow Division, joined the Metro- politan Police, as Constable, on the 25th day of 6 ji January, 1802, and resigned his situation on the .; 6 I,Btli day of January, kiil, having being granted .' Special Pension of 5.:24 H. 3d. per annum. The $, ~ above Pension is granted on account of injuries f; I received by the Constable in tie execution of ! his duty. His conduct was generally good. ; The Pension will be paid to the Pensioner ) r (by the Receiver for the Metropolitan Police Die- J ; trict) in accordance with the rea stations of the i( Secretary of State, and it cannot be assigned as ' security for a loan of money. t ' Given under my Hand, and Sealt 1 of the :Metropolitan Police. j a S Creswell Wells, Superintendent. Assistant Commissioner 1 A. C. Howard, of Police of the Metropolis. i</p>
        <p>SEAL.</p>
        <p>NSW SCOTLAND YARD, 30th day of January, 1899.</p>
        <p>I continued to puffer as badly ss ever, and went to the Seaford Hospital, in Sussex. They gave me even ; attention, but after a month's treatment I same back worse than ever. No matter how tempting and het); the food which was prepared, I vomited all I took. ano the very sight of food made me eck ! I became reducer in weight to just over 10 stone, and I was as weak as e kitten. The vomiting, the pain across the arnsoach,ane in the back, the night sweats and the cramping sense- tions continued unabated until September in at year. "In that month I tried tome of Chas: For 'Cs Beans. A few doses made me eotnewhow feel a kai, !iv( her, no I went in for a course, in order to give them a fair trial, By the time I had taken a few boxes I was a different man. The vomiting bad stopped, arm the dull,aching pain at the pit of the aiomach had gOne Every day I got Stronger abd stronger, The Ctamp and the night-3weats stopped, the species and Bashi. before my eyes disappeared, I became able to walk withote needing support, and at Melt I enjoyed sound, refreeh ing sleep: To cut a long story short, I am now quit, cured. The effects of the poison have been driven ow of my system by the Bear s, and my stomach and diges- tive organs seem to have been re-made. I have eat n se well that I have gathea twenty-one pounCe in weigh and am still adding to it. For the that time for mall; five years, on Christmae Day last I was able to eat sec Enjoy a good Uhristmas dinner. " Two mouths age I was able to restart work ac attendant in a picture gallery. For three weeks before Christmas I was at work !here Item haif-past eigh, o'cicck in the morning to hslf-past eleven at pliCt t Saturdays inc:uded and I did not feel fatigued wtth it. "In nay case Bile Beans seem to have woshed miracle. Publish ray statement by all means, aed let as many people as poseib'e know that, amonget al; 11-, advertised remedies, there is one which dots • ct deper d for its sale on mere written advertisemente, tut upo living men and women wham 1 has saved 'corn tqi grave. My case is one instance of i's power, and ti ; medicine is Chat. Forde's Beane." _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>Snarl is the won eritil story which Mr Eleffersiili The great lessons to te learned from ir are these —Don% ccmpare Bile Beaca with ordinary ir edietne They are entirely different and superior. Don't t biz k that, tccause ordinary preac4 iptions and remedies hay- failed to cure a crtaia case, that bile Steers will I:l9Cf F- Narilv fail don's think tnat any case is ti o bad far Bile Beans to tench.</p>
