        <p>The Bile an Manufacturinz Co., will send yet, free sample box et 131:e 11.?,t1 if vol.] forwerd ti coupon to their Ceutral Die tributing Der ot,Grerk- FREE SAMPLE GRUPO"! strect, Leos, (Yorks.) along with full name arc] BILE BEANS. addrEss rcd a pency Warrington Examiner, strmp to pay return Feb. 15, 1902 postage.</p>
        <p>Fourteen tons of narcissus arrived at Prrzanre or Tneedzy afternoon firm Scilly, bring the large s corsignment ef flowers for the sm,sorr. It is reported from Canton that an immense fire has occurred in nisi western suburbs of that torn. Betwoo, four and five hundred shops have been destroyed. The Nneral took place at atoneyglass, Antrim, ci Tursday,of Henry M'Cleakey ,who Med or Stind,iy ,ag: 107. At the last general election he va'ked to the pa to record his vote. About 601 t by 20ft of the Great Northern platform at ratielaF, London, ard several trucks were clew top d on Tuesday niht by fire. It originatod ai the prsmises of a coal merchant, and burnsd out about 50 till, at ern!. Tuesday was a record day In the fashionable weddir g world, no fewer that ten functions having taken plre, to the afternoon, mortly at London Westend churches. The reason is le-nt, during which period society weddings are not solemnised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>[ALL Rulers REszairED.]</head>
        <p>[ALL Rulers REszairED.]</p>
        <p>THE TRAINING OF ALICE LOVEDAY</p>
        <p>EY MARGARET WATSON.</p>
        <p>PAST 11.--CIIAPTER 111.-CONTINUED.</p>
        <p>When Alice reached homethe forget-me-note were hanging limp and wilted. She put them in a glass of water on the dresser. In a little while each leaf stiffened, the stems grew straight, and the blue flowers looked up at her with cheerful eyes. But she looked back at them with her dark eyes clouded with sarrow—not with uncertainty. It did not occur to her that she could desert her post for the sake of making a happy life for herself. It would not turn out a happy one if she did—that she was very sure of. In the evening, sitting moodily in Mrs. Giffken's cottage, Clack told her of his brilliant prospects, and pf Alice's refusal to share them. They were alone, for the thildren had gone to bed, and Giffkens had just stepped down to the 'public,' as he did occasion- ally, in spite of his wife's protests. Eben sat beside the door in his working clothes, no coat on, his shirt sleeves rolled up to the elbows, his hands hanging down over his knees, his fair face, with a little downy whisker, most dismal. " She can't think much about me if she don't like me no better nor her father," he remarked, after an interval of silence.</p>
        <p>" Likin' ain't everything," retorted Mrs. Giff- kens. "Alice 'ave got something else to think about besides what she likes, and if I was a man I wouldn't want a woman as did jest what she thought she liked best, and didn't consider first where her duty was. Most gals 'ud ha' liked to idle and play about when they hadn't got no mother over 'ern to see as they didn't, and if she'd ha' done that you'd never ha' thought nothin' of her, and now you're all for her doin' what she likes, that is if what she likes is to go off with you, and leave her father and the children to do how they may." Eben stared, open-mouthed, at this view of the question.</p>
        <p>"I'd take Nelly and willin'," he said atjast, when he had gathered his wits together to see the strong points of his own case again, "and I'd bring my money home reg'lar. I ain't one to go to public, and that you knows, Mrs. Giflkens."</p>
        <p>" Loveday'd never part with Welly," she returned decisively. "So you can make up your mind to do without Alice, for she'd never leave her ; she's fon- der of that child, I do believe, than if she was her own."</p>
        <p>Couldn't 3 ou say a word for me, Mrs. Giffkens She thinks a lot o' you, and you could promise as you'd _see after 'em—'</p>
        <p>" Hark at him! sets there and asts me with my fam'ly to see after Loveday and his two ;" but see- ing Eben's misery she relented a little, and added, "Well, I'll see what she says, but mind you, 'don't think as anythink I can say'll make a ha'porth Aillerenc4 one way or t'other. Of course, it's a gosi Wee for the gal, I'm not saying authing agaist hat, but we can't always take the chance; ag coine our way. If her poor mother hadn't ha' died she mitha' bin a school-teacher by now, and earning her sixty pound a year, and I don't know as that mightn't ha' bin better than marryin' you," with a humourous glance at the dejected figure. " Marry- in's a casualty thing, and you never knows how the childern may turn out neither." But Mrs. Giffkens went across to Alice in the morning to say the best she could for Eben. She found her with swollen eyelids and white cheeks. It was Saturday morning, and in thinking over the necessary purchases of the week it had sud- denly occurred to her how dull it would be in all the years to come to have no Eben to raeether and wheel the perambulator home. Mrs. Giffken saw the sign., of distress.</p>
        <p>"Well, Alice gal," she said. " You ha' thought better on't, I expect, and nobody can't blame you; for you ha' done a good part by 'em this four years, and it stands to reason as a gal must think of her- self." "You don't think as I'd leave 'em, Mrs. Giffkens,' replied Alice, turning away to choke down a sob. "Well, I don't know why you shouldn't. I'd do for 'em what I could, and Loveday'd have to get a woman in to clean up and wash." ' '</p>
        <p>"But that'd never be like home to 'en' without someone here constant to see to their victuals and their mending. I did think praps he might ha' waited till Consty was a bit bigger and could look after things, and then if father'd let me take Nelly —I. couldn't leave Nelly not whatever."</p>
        <p>"Have you spoke th your father?" 'No. He said as he couldn't wait more than a year, and that ain't no use. I couldn't leave 'em to get on as they could." "Well you can't expect a man to wait. He'll get took up -with some one as soon as he goes down there, you see if he don't. They don't like waiting, not even for their victuals, they must have 'em, cooked or uncooked. I tells Giffkens he'd rather have sixpence to-day nor wait for a shillin' to-mor- row. They're all alike for not waitin'. Then you can't make up your mind to take him ?" "No. I couldn't leave them. I don't deny as I skould ha' liked it well enough if it could 'ave been, and me to keep Nelly, but I couldn't leave them, Dirs. Giftlens, and you couldn't in my place." "Well, no, I don't know as I could, and we can't expect to 'ave things as we'd like 'ell in this world. But I promised Eben as I'd, speak. Shall I tell hum? or will you give him your answer?" "I'll give him ray answer myself, if he'll come along the road to-night as I comes back from shop."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>CILAITEIt</head>
        <p>CILAITEIt</p>
        <p>It was between six and seven in the evening that Eben and Alice met, just outside the little town. . Nelly, who had walked in, was ensconced in the perambulator to ride home,, and built round with loaves of bread and groceries. At her feet, tied. up in a white cloth, was a bit of meat for Sunday's dinner. Consty held on to the handle of the per- ambulator, and thought sho helped to push. It was a lovely evening% • The tqcY gt thQ MiF°ll as a pale translucent green. The sun was gettlng *itheut a cloud. The waking of a little breeze made Alice stoop and draw a shawl up over Icelly'o knees. Eben came up with a miserable, sulky expression on his face. His blue eyes were dark with vexa- tion and disappointment. He did not take the perambulator as usual, but turned to walk beside Alice with his hands in his pockets. They walked on irt silence for a time, Alice finding the perambulator heavy and speech difficult.</p>
        <p>At last Eben spoke: "Have you made up your mind, Alice, as you'll throw me over ?" I don't want to do it,Eben," she answered, with a catch in her breath, "but I can't help it. I couldn't leave 'em, they are so little, and father ain't any use in the 'ouse. He wants waitin' on himself, and they couldn't get anyone else to do for 'em—not as I've done it."</p>
        <p>Here Consty interrupted : " What does he want you to go away for, Alice ?" she asked, and Nelly screwed herself round to look up in Alice's face and say: "Ally not go away. Naughty Eben to want Ally to go away." "Ally wouldn't leave Nelly, not whatever. Don't you see as I can't leave 'em, Eben ? I promised mother as I'd look after 'em, and you can't think as I ought to break my promise." But the appeal was vain. Eben felt his own in- juries too keenly to see her side. "Well," he said sulkily, "I don't see but you hsiro Itok care on 'em enough. They ain't babies new, &amp;ad. I said as I'd wait _another _twelvemonth." _ . _</p>
        <p>"'What's a twelvemonth?" crief,Alk; Conety'd be but seven, and Nelly five. I colddn't leave 'em any better then than I could now." , " It's cruel 'and on a chap, Alice," he said, and his voice was husky. "I mit stay on 'ere for ever and get no more nor eleven shillin'. 'Taint a chance as I can miss, and I ha' thought about you all this two year—ever since the time I saw you settin' under that lime tree with Nelly. I liked the looks of you then, though you did walk off so proud, and I ha' over thought different, and now as us mit be married and be as comformable as comformable, now you sny you won't have me, and it's cm:l 'and on a chap." "It ain't my. fault, Eben," returned Alice, the tears raining down her cheeks, "and I e,h.an't never like any one else as much as I do you. "Then why won't you have me, Alice ?" " I promised ntother, Eben—l couldn't leave them." _ _ _</p>
        <p>"It's cruel 'ard on a chap," repeated Eben Clack, and he turned away fronl,her without even a "go night." Alice pushed on home, choking back her sobs, and found her father sitting there,. waiting for his sup- per. Ho saw her trouble with slow surprise. "What's the matter, gal P" he asked her. f‘ lichen Clack's going away," she answered. "Well, what's that to cry about What's Elm Clack to you P" "He wanted me to go with him." "To go with him ! A gal like you! What** Lo You mean?"</p>
        <p>4' I'm grownup, father—l'm eighteen—he wanted us to get married." Eighteen be you? Law ! I never thought as you was that age." He was silent for a time, ruminating, then hc said: "You ain't goin' to ?" "No, father, I ain't. I wouldn't leave the chil- dern and you, not whatever." "That's all right then," he responded with a re- lieved air. In a little over a week Eben Clack was gone, and gone without even saying good-bye to Alice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CHAPTER V.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER V.</p>
        <p>One afternoon, six months after Eben Clack had left the place, Alice called in at Mrs. Giffkens' on her way home from the town to leave a few parcels she had brought for her.</p>
        <p>It was na much after four, but the November darkness was closing in. Alice's and Consty's boots showed how muddy the roads were, the perambulator had been heavy and Alice sat down to rest a min- ute.</p>
        <p>"What was the bacon this week ?" asked Mrs. Giffkens, taking out a worn leather purse. " 'Twas only fourpence ha'penny—ain't that cheap ?" replied Alice, and then she went on, "While I was in Stevenson's gettin' that and the cheese Mrs. Thornton's cook came in, you lmow, Sarah Limpus, she ain't been there long, and won't be / should think."</p>
        <p>"Well, I have heard say as they can't quite make her out."</p>
        <p>"She do seem queer. She come up to me and begun sayin' what a pretty child Nelly was, and how nice and clean they looked, and then she said she should think 'twas bad for me bein' at 'ome, and not learnin' how to make my livin' nor nothin' ; and she should think I should do Well in gentlemen's service, 'cause I was tall and nice looking. I can't think what she was after."</p>
        <p>"She ain't lookin' after your father, is she think you ?" "After father !" Alice's jaw dropped, and she stared horrified at Mrs. Giffkens.</p>
        <p>"Well, gal, I don't want to say nothin' to upset you, but I did 'ear as Mrs. Davill saw her, Sunday was a week ago. Your father was goin' up the road past the Allotments—you know he often do go that way of a Sunday afternoon—and she come athert the field from Thornton's and jest met him at the top of the 'ill by the school ; and Mrs. Davill she see him stop and. speak a minute and then they went on together, and out o' sight over the top of the 'ill. They Can't come back together, that Ido know, for I see him come back myself and More wa'nt no one with him then."</p>
        <p>"But—oh dear! oh dear! Father'd never think of havin' her, would he Mrs. Ginens ? Why, she goes to public. I see her myself, 'cause I'd forgot the tape as you wanted and.had to turn back for it, and jest as I passed the " Crown" I 'appened to look in, and there she was a-standin' at the bar and had a glass of something in front of her; and when she see me she went as red, and looked as angry; and then she come out after me and said as she'd felt fainty like, and thought she must ha' dropped, and a friend of hers had persuaded her to go in and have a drop of something. She made it a rewle never to go into no publics, she said. But I didn't see no friend with her—no woman, however—there was some men in there. I shouldn't think as Father'd 'ave anything to do with her if she wanted it ever so.' to be hopes not, but men ain't got mucli knowledge. And do you know, child, I ha' heerd something else this afternoon." "What's that then ?" Alice looked up with fresh fear. Anything was possible if this other horrible fear was. _</p>
        <p>" Eben Clack's got married." Mrs. Giffkens watched Alice with strong sym- pathy, and saw her shrink a little, as from a blow, and her face flush. _ . .</p>
        <p>"He hasn't been long about it," she said, in a hurt tone. "No. That's what I said. And she must ha' bin pretty ready. To get married like that in six months or less. Why she can't know much about him, nor he about her. However, it's to be hopes as they'll get on all right." "Yes, it's to be hopes so. But I must get in or tea won't be ready by the time father comes home. Good night. Come along, Consty," and Alice went out, stumbling over the well-known threshold. "Mind the step, my dear !" exclaimed Mrs. Gift- kens, and when the door was shut she turned to the fire, for want of a better audience and said aloud: " Men do beat all I That there Loveclay'll go and marry a woman as'll drink him out of 'ouse and 'orae, and that fool of a Clack 'axe bin and got mar- ried to some silly gal, jest when he might ha' had Alice, as there isn't her equal for stidclyness and thoughtfulness. t' Alice went home and made the fire up, and set tea. Then she found, in the farthest corner of her own drawer, in the family chest, a little parcel of dry sticks and leaves, that had once been blue water forget-me-nots growing in the brook by the hay- field. This she dropped into the hottest part of the fire. .</p>
        <p>" He might ha' waited the year," she said.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER VI.</p>
        <p>Day by day the trouble that was coming upon Alice grew and took shape. Nov her father had been seen to walk home with Sarah Limpus in the dusk of a Sunday afternoon. Now he had been seen to treat her to a glass of beer at the bar of " The Crown " Inn, Nam he and his team met her in passing through the town . and one day Mrs. Thornton's housemaid, meeting Alice, asked if her father was generally at home at eight o'clock in the evening, because Sarah Limpus always put a shawl over her head and went out as soon as she had sent the sweets up, and didn't come in again for an hour. Alice had to acknowledge hopelessly that her father had taken to strolling out every evening be- tween eight and nine..</p>
        <p>" Well, I'm sorry for you, Alice," said the girl, who was an old school-fellow. "She puts it about as she've got a lot of moody, saved up, but I don't believe it. She's too fond o'- gin to ha' saved much money, and you can tell your father I said so. She hates me now, so as she couldn't hate me no worser, .I,lenldn't stay with her, only I know Missid ptt; with her long, and she knows it too. She've been found fault with pretty sharp lately, I can tell you." Th3n Hrs. Gifikens tackled the man himself :</p>
        <p>fa that right then, Master Loveday, a orni le thinkin' o' gettin' married f" she asked him one eveni-,17, as be was passing home from work. v‘ Who says as I be ?" lie answered sulkily. " Ain't it r'ght then ?" " That ain't your business Mrs. Giffkena."</p>
        <p>" Not my bu;iness ! and lha' looked after them ehildern o' yourn as if they'd bin my own, and this I will say, Loveday, if you marries that there Sally Liurais you'll regret it to the longest day you live. " Well, what is there agenstth'eoman ? She kin in good service for years." " Years, he have, I'll go bail! but as for good, 'tain't a good 'once as'd'keep tier long. Mrs. Thorn- ton can't stand her ways, and that I'do know, and to think of putting sech a woman over Alice and the el:ildern—a woman as goes to public and drinks gin liko water.! There I I should think that your poor vife would turn in her grave at the thoughts of it." " Goes to public? Who nays as she goes to public ?" " Well, don't you know it your own self ?". Lovedity grew embarrassed, and shifted from one foot to the other. " If Toll call having a glass by chance .—,sPoin' to he muttered. " And there's poor Alice," Mrs. Giffkems went or., "as have been more'n a mother to they childern— what'll she do ?" " Well, I don't say as I be thinkin' o' gettin' Marr:cd, but I if was 'twouldn't be any wondeb as I should want a 'oornan as'd stay and look after me, not a gal as'll want to get married and leave me to do for myself." "Well, you know as how she give Clack the go- by, as decent a chap as you'd find, and she that fond of him, jest because she wouldn't leave you and the ohildern." • " Oh, yes ; she give Clack the go-by, but she mightn't do that .to the next one, and then where should I be? Not as I'm sayin' as I be thinkin' o' gettin' married.' " There's Consty gettin' a big gal." Consty. aint' forgot how oncomformable it was with Alice at fust, though you true very good, Mrs. Gifficens, and I'm not a-denyin' it, but there, what can a little gal know about making a ma,ll comformable ? Alice 'ave learnt to be very tidy and 'ancly, I don't say as she ain't, but I don't want to be left with no one but Consty to do for me. Mind I chin't say as I am agoin' to get married. I only , says as it 'ouldn't be onreasonable, and if I vas to, a respectable 'oonaan as 'as bin in service and startedsavad t o a mb-ailtk w a 0 a,ray Saved a bit o' money is the sort, as'cl.sitit me," and h tie), ?" called Mrs. Giffkens after " She ha' saved that in a gin bottle. You act her to show you her Saiin's bank book." But Loveday would not turn or answer. He went en -without a word. Alice, too, had made' up her mind at last to have it out with her father- •She had been serewintr tue</p>
        <p>her courage all day, through alternate Eta of herand cold, and though she saw be was out of temper when he came in, she felt that she must go through with it that night, whatever it cost her. She waited until he had had his tea and the chil- dren were in bed; then she came and stood in the glow of the fire, her hands clasped before her, her forehead resting slightly against the high mantel- piece. Her father sat opposite in his elbow chair, smok- ing, and looking very glum. Now the time had come her lips grew dry, and the words she had prepared went from her. She stood there, looking down at him, and trying for fit words to begin, until he looked up impatiently and said: " Well, gal, what be standin there for? Ain't you got nothin' to do ?" Then her words came fast enough. _ _</p>
        <p>"Father, pray don't marry that Sally Limpus. She's a horrid, bad, drinkin' woman, and if you lmow'd half the things they say about her you wouldn't go nigh her.' "You can jest 'old your tongue about that, Alice. If you goes round listenin' to all the silly tales all the women tells, you can 'ear plenty. I knows as much about Sarah Limpus as you do, and more too, and I says as she's a respectable woman as has lived in good service all her life. I don't say as I means to marry her, but I do say as if Ido I've got a right; to, and neither you nor Mrs. Giffkens neither shan't stop me." "But, father, don't I make you comfortable ? ana don't I take care of the children P"</p>
        <p>"I don't say as you don't. But you mayn't be here alias. You mit take it into your 'ead to get married, and then where should I be ? or the chil- dern either?"</p>
        <p>" Oh, father !" Alice knelt down before him, and laid her hands on his knees. "I'd promise! I'd promise true and faithful, as I'd never get mar- ried, but stay and look after you and the children all my life. I did send Eben away 'cause I wouldn't leave you—you knows I did—and I'd promise, father."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>(To bs Concluded.)</head>
        <p>(To bs Concluded.)</p>
        <p>A WIDELY DISCUSSED QUESTION Great Interest Aroused in Preston.</p>
        <p>diacusiion which has been aroused in the mills L and workshops of Preston by the rather remark- 'able experience of a Preston Woman has now penetrated into most choirs in the town. A ratter strange feature of the case is that the feature which has caused most interest was developed by seems of a step taken in the face of strong prejudice on the part of the lady's hus- band. Mrs. Elizabeth Burgess, of 3, Osborne-strest Preston, the lady in question, explained all the facts to a Press representative who called on " I am only sorry," said Mrs. Burgers, who iS twenty-oeyen years of age, " I did not take this step leaner, for I now fecl quite a new woman, and after seven rears' miarry I have been quite restored to health"</p>
        <p>"Your case," observed the rspertcr, " seems unusu— al,y intorestine, and your experience, if recorded, would no doubt prove of value to thousands of others." " Well, out of ratitude for the immense benefit I have received, I shall be pleased to give it. I have been a sufferer from at ninth for seven years accompanied by indigestion and a fainting and d'zzy feeling. I had several doctors attending Ille, but mush of their medi- cine gave me euele pale that I could not continue taking "MO Ocelots' medicine no effecrat all ?' " Yes, it had some elight effect at first, but it after- wards became impotent, and the pains continued. Ono medicine in particular I dared not touch. I almost dreaded maneals, fcr the indigestion that followed, and the pain was sometimes so excruciating that it made me powerless to hold anything in my hand. Although I bad just had a meal, a fainting, sinking feeling soon followed, and I had always to have biscuits to chew. My nerves became affected, and the slightest monied used to be most distracting. Owing to the dizzy feeling I hardly dared go out alone. and what with shortness of breath, I could not join my friends in mountain climbing when on a visit to my home in iNalFs," "You would feel thst life was not worth living ? " "Y"s, I had an idea I could not live long. I was pale with pracically no colour in my lips. People used to say, "You do look ill ; why don'c you go to the dec. ter ? " As a matter of fact, I was being treated by doctors, and had triad their medicines and other remedies, but all to no purpose. They also used to tell me 'You stay indoors too much,' but I hardly dated go out," "Your present appearance does not betray any signs of such long suffering ?" interrogated the reporter. "No, I am qui'e well and strong now." "Tiwhat do you attribute this remarkable re- covery ? " "I can only attribute it to Dr. W Nam' pink pills for pale people." " What induced you to try this remedy, with regare to which I have lately beard from private sources some splendid testimonials ? " " Well, I had read about them in the newepapere, and heard that a relative bad tried them once, and that one box did her good, although she did not suffer in the same way as Inv eel. I certainly did not believe in them, as I thnught they were only like many other pills. But as r last resort I decided to give them a trial. My husband, who thought VVP had wasted enough in useless experiments, thought that I was wasting further time." ..venen did you first feel to have derived any bene— fit ?" _ . - . _</p>
        <p>"After the use of the second box the pain had gone, and after that I found I did not require to take the same number of pills as prescribed in the instructions.. I continued using thern,until no* I have got the seventh box, and I feel really strong now. If I walked up a hill my heart used to patpitate, but now I can go up a hill or upstairs with perfect ease." "'Has the indigestion been removed ?" " Oh, yes, and lam delighted to say I have no pain whatever." "Ss the you now have perfect faith in the ourative powers of Dr Wilms' pink pills, and you are prepared to give this public: testimony ?" " Certainly. I Clll9 only explain to inquirers am I have explained to yea, and that is the truth. I have recom- mended them to many since, and to those who have. inligestion I 9ay Take Dr Williams' pink pills ; they cured ma,' My sistlr suffered from anm nia, though not as acutely as myself, end the pill cured her." Mrs Starves's authenticated story is surely convinc, ing proof to Preston people that Dr Williams' pink pills, pwseqii all the virtues that are claimed for th em.</p>
        <p>Local Oddfellowship Presentations at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Is connection with the Admiral Nelson Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddfellows, presentations of PP 6 11/Ps jewels were undertaken on Monday evening at the Pelican Hotel. to BrOs F Webster. P P 0 M. P J Whittle, P P 0 M, and Frederick J Eamon, PPO M. There was a good attendance of members, over whom Bro F !Barnes, N G, presided and amongst others prosent were Bros John Eamon, Prov C S and Lolge See; A Perry, VG, George Perry, PP GM ; J Norbury, PPGM;M Knowles, PPGM; Ellis Clive, Prov Dap G ; William Holbrpok, P G; Thomas Healing, PG; A Smithson,, PH ; Edward Powell, P ; W Ilabborlev, 61.; Barnes, trustee, .k.c In asking the Prov C _ to make the presentations on behalt of the lodge, the G referred to the somewhat unique position of the lodge, in presenting to three of its worthy members jewels of this character. In making the presentations, the Prov C S referred to the services which had been rendered to the " Admiral Nelson" Lodge, aud the Warrington district gener- ally, by the three brethren to whom he was about to present these handsome jewels. It was, he said, also an honour that no member of any lodge in the Warrington District had ever had before, and in asking these particular brethren to accept the res- pective jewel,, he hoped that they would continue to devote their be9t energies to the furtherance of . blyackno on wltrild.egmed . The jewels bore suitable in— Odd fellowship in Warrington in the future as they had been bad done in the past. Bleb of the recipients conferredsuit- li ectiptions testifying to the offices which the res- pective three brethren had filled. honourtbe which During the even• - ing, various songs and recitations were rendered by different members. It may be added that the Pro. am., Bro. Thos. S. Steel had consented to make the presentations, but he was unavoidably ab:ent, th rough business engagements.</p>
      </div>
