      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>PRINCE HENRY OF PRUSSIA THE FIRST GERMAN PRINCE TO PAY AN OFFICIAL VISIT TO THE UNITED STATE</head>
        <p>PRINCE HENRY OF PRUSSIA THE FIRST GERMAN PRINCE TO PAY AN OFFICIAL VISIT TO THE UNITED STATE</p>
        <p>The hand of friendship recently extended by the Kaiser has been eagerly grasped by the United States, and Prince Henry of Prussia on his arrival at New York will receive a character- istically cordial American welcome. For weeks past our cousins on the other side of the Atlantic have been planning a reception worthy of such an important occasion and of a visitor so dis- tinguished, and, what between the Government, the millionaires, the public bodies, and the people, Prince Henry is like to be overwhelmed by their generous hospitality. When, on board the German Emperor's magnificent yacht Hohenzollern, he enters New York harbour, he will be received with a salute of twenty-one guns equal to a Presidential salute, and once on shore he will be taken in hand by President Roosevelt and other leading men of the Republic, and enter- tained during the whole period of his stay on a most elaborate scale. The object of the Kaiser in inviting President goosevelt's daughter to christen his new yacht, and in arranging that his brother should be present to do honour to the occasion, is not far to seek. It is obviously to the best interests of Germany that she should be on good terms with the United States in view of the Venezuelan trouble, and for many other excellent reasons, and the resourceful Emperor has, with his usual astuteness, hit upon a method of Bemiring the desired friendliness, the success of which is assured from the fact that it appeals direct to elle sympathies of the American people, whose love for a prince is becoming proverbial, and who are devoted to their President and his family. This is the first time that a German prince has paid an official visit to the United States, but if warmth</p>
        <p>of welcome counts for anything it is not likely to be the last. By the way, the launching of the Kaiser's yacht is not the only nor the most impor- tant ceremony which Miss Roosevelt will grace with her presence this year, as it is understood that she will accompany the Hon. Whitelaw Reid to England for the Coronation of King Edward. Mr. Thomas Sidney Cooper, the world-famous Royal Academician who died a few days ago, was in his ninety-ninth year. .• Lle wielded the brush almost from the cradle to the death-bed. For sixty-seen years without a break he exhibited paintings at the Royal Academy, a record which has been achiev- ed by no other British artist. It was to landscape and 'animal . painting that he devoted the main efforts of his life, and the chief artistic merits of his paintings we re their simplicity and truth to nature; the excellence of his animals, in whioh,, during his best years, he • approached, and probably equalled, the best Dutch masters who painted in the lame style ; the beauty of his technique, the .perfect balance of his compositions, with the just appreciation of their relative portions ; and lastly, the exquisite glow and atmosphere with which his pictures are filled. Of his best work the nation possesses seven examples. He was a man of deep religious feeling, and of ,warm heart and kindly -disposition, earnest and dogged and even rugged in his perseverance, and, moreover, a true artist -whose great merits everyone long agd'recognised. De Wet is indeed a marvel of elusiveness and resourcefulness. Once more he has slipped between the meshes a Lord Kitchener's net, and that When it seemed impossible for him to escape. The trap which had been set for him appeared as perfect as human skill could devise, but "the best laid schemes o' mice and men gang oft agley," as Bobbie Burns has informed us, and the great Boer strategist is still at large, though •vtigtus nearly three hundred of his men, seven hundred horses, and a large herd of cattle. Thils is the heaviest blow he has yet sustained, and at the same time it ranks as the greatest success achieved by Lord Kitchener since he took over the chief command in South Africa from Lord Roberts. Although Lord Kitchener's -elaborate scheme failed so far as the capture of the guerilla leader himself, it has ren- dered De Wet incapable of doing much mischief for some considerable time to come. His force has</p>
        <p>THE LATE I.llt. T. SIDNEY</p>
        <p>COOPER, R.A.</p>
        <p>THE VETERAN ARTIST.</p>
        <p>/THE GREAT BLOCKHOUSE SYSTEM IN SOUT</p>
        <p>AFRICA.</p>
        <p>THE LINES OF 11/ACla/or:ES AEt DENOTZD EY DOTS. ' been completely broken up and all his guns are gone, and moreover he has been driven into a part of the country where it is less easy for him to • practise his elusive tactics than in the north. This success is about the, best illustration we have</p>
        <p>yet had of how the blockhouses can be effectively</p>
        <p>iurned, to account in dealing with the scatterea _Cgutraandoes of the enemy. The blockhouse system</p>
        <p>December, 1900, and subsequently extended undet his command, has now grown to immense dimensions. The lines of miniature forts, which have so effectually protected the railways, now measure over 3,500 miles in length, and there are about three blockhouses to every mile. The bloek- house system not only safeguards the lines of communication, but, by a network of cross lines, hampers the enemy's movements to a paralysing ex- tent. Though bands may occasionally dash through the cordons, as in the cate of De Wet, the lines gradually force them into corners where they are pbliged to surrender. The map shosts the lines of blockhouses already constructed, the forts being indicated by black dots on a thin line. Where' a thicker line is seen running parallel to the string of blockhouses, that represents the rail way. This map will enable the reader to form a bettfr idea of Lord Kitchener's Brobdingnagian net than -...olumns of description:</p>
        <p>After a long absence from town Mr. Fred Terry and Miss Julia'Neilson have returned to the Globe Theatre with a highly' sensational and decidedly tedious political melodrama, which proved a dis- appointment on the first night, as a " stalls " piece is expected from them rather than a " gallery ' one.</p>
        <p>WHO PLAY THE PRINCIPAL PARTS IN "THE HEEL OF ACHILLES," AT THE GLOBE.</p>
        <p>.The Heel of Achilles as it is called, is the Indian frontier, the weak spot of the British Empire, and around this Mr. Louis N.. Parker and Mr. Boyle 'Laurence have woven a sensa- tional Russian intrigue. The anthers all through their piece have ascribed all the bizarre -situations to fate, but upon analysis it becomes evident that they are rather due to the foolish- ness of the foolish and a total lack of the commonest precautions on the part of the characters. To begin with, when Adam Bartenieff swears a solemn oath to do for the betrayer of his daughter, Prince Vladimir, the gentle- man in question and the villain of the play, instead of dropping such an undesirable adjunct as a man thirsting for his blood, persuades him to return to Russia with him and keeps him handy to bob up like a dem ex machind in the last act to murder him, save the self-sacrificing heroine, and generally untangle the threads that the authors have pur- , posely tangled in the weaving. This is one sample of "fate," and goes to prove how insincere and un- convincing the whole play is. Mr. Fred Terry and Miss Neilson work hard and consoientiously, but have no chance of great achievements. Now practically every West End theatre is full, and the critics can again take a breathing space after the recent hard work.</p>
        <p>The twenty-sixth annual Rugby match between England and Ireland, which was played at Leicester on Saturday, was particularly interesting from the fact that, while the wearers of the Red Rose were fighting hard to avoid a retention of the wooden spoon, the Irishmen were strongly fancied in certain circles on the western side of J. DANIELL, (ENGLAND).</p>
