      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>3. FELTON, (IRELAND).</head>
        <p>3. FELTON, (IRELAND).</p>
        <p>IttTißT INTERNATTON AL FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>TIL RIVAL 'CAPT A INS</p>
        <p>IN -THE ENGIJ.ND V. IRELAND MATCH. St. George,E, Channel as likely winners of the championship. The weather was wretched, but that did not prevent some twelve thousand followers of the Rugby code from turning out, and tboy were rewarded by a grand game. The English pack, with Daniell a:s ever the most encouraging of skippers, played magnificenty from start to finish, and the representatives of the Emerald Isle also pet in some clever work, especially the-lacks, who,e tackling was a conspicuous feature of the maLeh. Fulton; the Irish captain, was in grand form at full back, and apart from the one mistake which led to England's second try he played a splendid . game, and was quite a host in him- self. He was kept very busy, and his. kick- ing and fielding were both judicious and skilful. The victory of the Red Rose over the Shamrock gives "Wales an incr*ed chance of gaining the Triple Crown, for bath England and Scotland have been defeated, and on the form dig-, played on Saturday the Welshmen would certainly have,triumphed over the plucky, but not cohesive, Hibernians. In Association circles immense interest was 'taken in the Southampton, v. Liverpool tie, which was won by the former by;</p>
