      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>LOCAL RECHABITISM.</head>
        <p>LOCAL RECHABITISM.</p>
        <p>rwelve Months' Progress in War- rington and District.</p>
        <p>Though snow was falling steadily and a piercing win had the .ff&lt;ct ot compelling man) people to seek the comfort of their fire-tmae,., this mu' not in the les'st damp the enthusiasm of those members of the Order ot ftechsbites wno had been tulo off to represent the Vi arrington tents at the annisa meeting of the district at bolborne on Saturday afternoon, and whose only means of .acces, to tire Tillage was by making the journey in a 'tars. To say tbe least of it, [die p.ospects of a drive through the country were not inviting, but neverthelisss, the score or mere of Rechabites upon whom the duties of representa- tion devolved, turned up in full force and determined to make the best of mattere. it was shortly before two o'clock when a start wet, made, • and the streets. of Warrington were soon left behind. -Progress along the country lanes was rendered exceedingly difficult by the snow which lay inches deep, and several times did the horses come to a standstill. Then it was that some of the more buoyant spirits of the party descended from the vehicle and beguiled the time by indulging in the wholesome recreation of snowballing. At times the game waxed fast and turious,and we have it on authority that a certain robust official proved a particularly deadly' shot," ,s Awn° of his oppou eats found out to their discomfiture. Golborne was at last reached, and the party wended their way to the Liberal Club AsSembly Room, where the meet- ing was to be held. Additional interest was lent to the proceedings by the fact that the High Chief Ruler, Brother H Wells Smith, of Sheffield, presided, It ig now necessary to state that the Warn')gton district of the Order of Rechabites einbrales tents from the following places —W arrington, Padgate, Stockton Heath, : Latchford. Peuketh, Sarlestown, GLlborne, Ashton- in-Makerfield, Orrell. Bryan. and • Gars wood. Altogether sixteen tents. were representel at Satur- day's meeting including seveafrom Warrington and outskirts. It may be stated that the Rechabites can boast of a following of over seven hundred adults in Warrington alone. As the High Chief Ruler hal not arrived,the first part of the meeting was presicidd over by. Brother. William Baxendale (of Brynn),District Chief Ruler. The Rechabites have a distinctly business-like method of conducting their meetings and any dis- cussion was brief and to the point. Those brethren who represented tents were busily engaged in tak- ing note, in order to be able to present a report of the, proceedings to the nAmbers. Later in the I afternoon, the High Chief Ruler nut in an appear- ance and bad a cordial reception on taking the chair. _ _</p>
        <p>1- The progress of the Order in the District during the put year was briefly reported upon by the Secretary (Ben Thomas Winstanley, of Ashton) who Wormed the meeting that the total teembership was 1092 as against 1011 laat year. The total funda in the hands of the District (including the amounts in charge ef the various tents) totalled £3278 16+ 4d, as compared with £3016 70 44 last year. During the year there had been a total gain on the sick fund of £144 10J 31 and on the funeral fund of £193 lOs 10d. the iotere t obtained during the year on the total capital was 31 per sten% It will be seen from . this report that the district is in a very II ,urishing state.,</p>
        <p>_ The report wail adopted. - Some Interesting details relating to the development of the Juvenile District were then presented by the superintendent (Brother W Wright, Warriorton). He reported that the district WA9 instituted in 1891 with a total membershiptif 94 and funds:amountiag to £4 9 21. At the present time the number of members w,e- 512, being an increase of 117 on last year and the funds amounted to £153 14s 7d as compared with £l3B 19A 90 last year, Four funeral claims had been paid amount- ing t 3 £24 10s. Seventeen juveniles had been transferred to the various adult tents in the district during the year. In conclusion Brother Wright said the juvenile work was very hopeful indeed and there were encouraging signs on every hand. The High Chief Ruler delivered a Phort speech wt reference to the juvenile work. He said it was a grand thing to know that 4,000 juvenile member, were every year finding their way int.) toe adult rank. No other organization in the country could boast of that. Re was pleased to hear of the success of the work in the War- rington District It was it.lportanc that children should nc tbe sent into the womli hanlicapped and efforts for their well-being should be made early in their lives.</p>
        <p>The report was approved. The election of officers was proceeded with. Brother William Baxendale was re-3lected District Chief Ruler and Brot'oer J Huddleston (Earlestown) wan elected deputy D R Brother D Byrom tendered his resig- nation frnn the office of Past Chief Ru'er, and Br t'ier James Home (Garswood) was elected in his place. Other elections were as follows :—Guardian, Brother J Hill, (Ashton) ; Levite, Brother B Walker. (Latchford) . Juvenile Superintendeat, Bro W Wright (Warrington) TrusteeS, Brethers Thomas Ball, James Shaw, and; Edward Walkden ; TrAsarer, Brother T Massey (War- rington); Secretary, Brother Thomas Winstanley (Ashton) ; Auditors, Brothers Warburton and Clarke. A letter from the Warrington District Nursing Associa- tion appealing for support, was referred to the Execu- tive. Other business of minor importance having been transacted, an adjournment was made to the Wesleyan ,School where an excellent tea was sinned, and needless to say, the repast was ki•enly enjoyed. A very successful public meeting and eoncert after- wards took place at the Liberal Club. The Rev J Sutcliffe (Congregational Minister) presided • and deliver.d a brief addrrsa, in the course of which he mentioned that one reason why the annual meeting had been held at Golborne was because the local tent had just attained • membership of one hundred. A capital programme of quartettes, genes and recitations was submitted by a concert party from Wigan, all the artistes acquitting themselves admirably. During the evening The High Chief Ruler gave an intern e address. He nointed out that the Order of RecheLites had 300,000 members and the Rechabi'•em were a select number of people who had since 1835 been trying to prove that people could live without iatexinating drinks. Some time ago the Etatistics of the ,)fder were com- pared with those of the Foresters and Odd fellows and the gentleman who investigated them had reported that taking the average age of the Rechabite to be twenty years and !comparing him with the average Forester or oid fell o w of the same age and taking his experience throughout life, he found that the Rechabite lived on an average five years and eight months longer than the members of the Oddfeliows and Foresters Societies He could not come to any other conalusichk than that the total abstin- ence proclivities of the members were res- ponsible for this extra period of life. It was also ascertained that the Rechabites had foutteen weeks lees sickness during their life than the Gidfellows and Foresters. Continuing, Mr Wells Smith pointed out that if the Rechabite• lived five years and eight menths</p>
        <p>longfr than the 0.31 fellows and ifcresters, Che Society received contributions for that period, an the interest was also increased. The Friendly Societies of this coun- try bed saved only forty millions of mon-y during the fifty to eigtry years they had been in existence, and vet in three months these level-headed Englishmen spent just as much money in int( xicating drinks as the friendly societies had saved in all those yeare. It was and sad regrettable fact, and then they talked about German competition and other things. The matter was entirely in their own hands. Was 'there anyone present who ever knew of a Recbsbite being in I a Workhouse? He had asked many audiences that question, and was a Guardian f.,r the city of Sheffield, , but had never heard of a Rechabite being in the Work- house. (Applause.) Referring to the drink evil he asked them to remember that it was not a matter of Liberal and Tory in politics, it was a matter of the Common wealch. the national politics. What did the people say who sat for three and a half years and gathered evidence as to the drink traffic and all its ramifications, the majority of whom were not teetotalers ? They said tt We are convinced that there is a large amount of superfluous drinking existing and hardly any sacrifice is sufficient to be made to rid the country of this national degradation." The Rechabites existed for the purpose of ridding the nation of the greatest curse that ever afflicted this or any other nation. Referring to the Juvenile Section he said they were making more progress than any other Society in this respect. They had opened 189 branches last year alone and for fourteen years had registered more branches than nearly all the other organizations put together. (Applause.) - Other musical items having been rendered the singing of the National Anthem concluded an enjoyable gathering.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>EDUCATIONAL MATI ERS</head>
        <p>EDUCATIONAL MATI ERS</p>
        <p>Discussed by the Warrington Trades</p>
        <p>Council.</p>
        <p>Educational matters occupied considerable attention at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council which was held in the Druids' Hall Sankey-street, unTuesday evening. Mr J Edwardson pre- sided over a good attendance. The SECRETARY (Mr F Stott), who represented the Council at the annual meeting of the Lancashire Federa- tion of Trades Councils, submitted a report of the pro- ceedings at that meeting. Be referred to the discussion which took place with respect to the education question in the course of which he mentioned the fact that the Warriogton Corporation were intending to promote a Bill to confer upon them the powers of a School Board. This gave rise to some discussion and many of the delegates considered that there would a danger if a com- mittee was fcrmed from the Town Council to deal with all phases of education, that the members would most probably begin tollplace some outside persons on the committee. . The delegates were afraid that the men who would be "co-opted "on the committee would not be the meta win/ were most able to deal with the educa- tion question, but who bad probably the greatest influ- ence over the members of the Town Council. After considerable discussion a resolution was moved by Me Redfern, of Rochdale, to the effect that there ought to be an educational authority for a stated area to deal with the elementary and technical education, and the members should be elected by popular vote. " Co-opted!" members -should ,not be allowed to sit on , the committee nor .41 should a com- mittee of the Town Council, who had been elected not to deal with education but with the ordinary work of the Town Council, be formed for educational purposes. The Committee should be electea by popular vote for educational purposes only. This resolution was carried by a large majority. —The Secretary also referred to other matters which were discussed by the Federation and a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to him for the report on the proposition of Mr Podmore, s conded by Mr Greeley. The Council decided that if necessity arose, a special meeting should be held to consider the Government Education Bill and to instruct the Secretary as to how to vote in the matter at the forthcoming meeting of the Federation. During the evening one of the delegates called atten- tion to a circular:which has been issued by the School Attendance Committee of the Corporation embodying a nutnber of the byelaws recently adopted. One clause stated that" the maximum penalty is twenty shillings for every half-lily's non-attendance." Mr Portuoes pointed out that the School Attendance Committee were simply complying with the law of the land in issuing the byelaws. Mr. PitesTos said the clause respecting the maximum penalty wee a piece of bluff sent out by the School Attendance Committee and the workingmen had no need ' to take any notice of it whatever. To his mind, when the Government stipulated that workingmen could not ' control their own children and they (the Government) were going to take it into their hands and compel the workingmen to do with their children what they (the Government) said they must do, a great deal of alter- ation was necessary in the E 'illation Act. Ho was not putting down education, but he maintained that if children were to be compelled to go to school they should be entirely educated at the expense of the Government who sent them. That part of the Educa- tion Act was entirely wrong and when the reactionary measure was b:ouelat forward it was to be hoped that the all-wiso G mernment which was goine to force chil- dren to attend school would also find the means of ^ providing the money. Mr:Poomonis:esid it was Ve law that a man wati liatle to the penalty it he kept his child at borne without a good reason. The intenders was that children should attend school on every possible occasion. He did not see where the bluff came in and he should like Mr Plineton to explain. Mr PLIN•TON said the Act did not state at all as to what reasons a child ceuld be kept at home for. According to the figures published it allowed a child to be absent One hundred times during the year, and if the child did not exceed that it would not be punished so far as the exemption certifieate was concerned. They some- times allowed the authorities to have to much of their own way. The statement with regard to the penalty was misleading to workingmen. Mr PODMORE : They are liable to the fine if they keep children from school without a proper excuse. Mr Plinston : But they are not liable to ?..0.3 for every offence.</p>
        <p>--- -- Mr 7oDat-Inn That is the maximum penalty. Mr•PuNsrox : It is a boeev. Continuing he said he bad met w:th members of the working class who feared tat a 20i fine would be imposed for every half day the child was ab;ent. He said that this was bluff and nothing but bluff when the child could be absent one hundred times during the year and they could not enforce the penalty, It was the duty of that Council to look after the interests of the uneducated working- men of the town who were bemg treated very wrongly and harshly by the School Attendance Committee. After further discussion the matter dropped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>PENKETH</head>
        <p>PENKETH</p>
        <p>THE FATAL TRAP ACCIDENT.—An inquest was ducted by Mr. Brighouie, county coroner, at the Red Lion Hotel, Penketh, on Monday, into the circumstarces attending the death of John Fisher, farmer, who resided at Woodcroft Farm, and who died from the result of a trap accdient on Tuesday evening, February 4th. It appears that deceased and a man named Cooke were driving in a governess car in the direction of Sankey and when near the School House, Great Sankey, the vehicle collided with a lurry which was in charge of a man named George Rigby. of Sr. Helens. Both occupants of the governess car were thrown cut and the deceased, who was seriously injured, was carried into the School House, where Mrs Clarke, the wife of the schoolmaster, attended to him. He was afterwards removed home and attended by Dr Murray. Mr Browne solicitor, was present on behalf of Mr Cooke. The Coroner, at the outset, asked Rigby if he was represented by a solicitor, and on receiving an answer in the negative, spoke of the importance o`. the case and the seriousness of Rigby's position. after hearing one witness he said he thought it was advisable to adjourn the inquiry so that Rigby could 1)3 legally represented. The jury agreed, and the inquest was adjourned to Monday next.</p>
