        <p>Small-pox and Re-Vaccination.—Action by the Lymm Urban Council. At a special meeting of the Lymm Urban District Council held on Tuesday, the Chairman (Mr Dew- burst) said he had consulted Dr Lunt (the Medical Officer of Health) with regard to the advisabiliv of re-vaccination among the inhabitants of the dis- trict. He need not point out the advantages of re-vaccination, and in view of the present epidemic of small-pox in London, which appeared to be spreading to different parts of the country. Dr Gant was of opinion that they should issue large posters in the district cilling attention to the Janger of the disease,and advising people to be re-vaccinated. Dr Lunt, as public vaccinator, would be prepared to attend people at their homes and also at his surgery. The calf lymph used would be supplied by the Local Government Board. --lhe Medical Officer suggested that members of the Council should set an example to other people by being re.vaccinated themselves from the Chair- man down. The Chairman quite agreed. He said he was re-vaccinated eight years ago, and was again vaccinated the other day. If the Council expected their employes to be re-vaccinated they should set them the example.—Two or three members ex- pressed their personal objection to be re-vaccinated. The Medical Officer, replying to one member, said that sanitation had very little to do with smallpox.</p>
        <p>If they had an outbreak it would not matter whether their sanitary arrangementi were perfect or not, the dieesse would spread. That had been proved conclusively in London.—On the motion of the Chairman, seconded by Mr. Groves, it was de- ciced to issue the posters.</p>
