      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the meeting of this society on Sunday. Mr William Beswick presided and ' delivered a brief and interesting address. The speaker for the afternoon was Mr Beecher Smith, of ManchestE r. Giving out the text " Him that overcometh will I make a pillar in the temple of my God, and he shall go no more out," and, addressing the meeting on " pillars," the speaker called attention to the Prentioe's Pillar in Roeslyn Chace!, and to atVitl6'd Polar in Strasburg Cathedral. The story of the former, he said, showed the evil of given way to envy. After the pattern of Sivina's Pillar our lives ought to be fashioned—first the four Evangelists, nem four angels, and at the top, 4 figure of Christ. 'Mr W H Wallinst in gave an effecive rendering of tho solo " The HolF Temple " which was greatly appreciated by the audiPnce. The service was els) taken part in by the Rev J Yonge,air William Lowe and Mr Farrar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Wycliffe Church.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Church.</p>
        <p>Special sermons were preached at this church on Sunday by the R-v JAIn Yonge. At the morning ser- vice the subject of his discourse was "The shadow Of Peter," based upoa the text "They brought forth the sick into the streets and laid them on beds and couches, that at the least the shadow of Peter passing by might overshadow some of them," Ants 5 , 15v. The preacher expounded in an able mariner how a man's Shadow had an unconscious lull'', nce upon others and bow it either helped or hindered others. Ought it not to become a serious matter sad concern t us, he asked, to think that our livt s are al wa:ys ratting their shadow - upon the lives of our fellows? Such a fact ought to make for the seriousness of personal character. He asked them to remember that their lives did cast their shadows and in places where they least imagined. They did not fully realise how intensely real and close was their fellowship with their fellows. In the evening the rev gentleman preached a sermon to young men. The subject was taken from I Corinthians, 15 chapter, part of the 34th verse " Some have not the knowledge of God. I speak tbis to your shame." lie remarked that he was very glad to see that so many young nvn had respot ded to the invitation to be present. Ile should not like the young women to imagine that because the sermon had been specially chosen for young men, it bad nu interest and no importance for them. It would affect a great many others besides young men and young women if their consciences were active and responsive. lathe course of his remarks, the preacher said the Christian beldever and especi-lly the Christian young believer ought to be very careful of the literature he read. He did not wish te be misunderstood. It was no desire of his to discourage reverent inquiry into new fields of truth. The apostle Paul said " Prove all things." But while they di I that they must recollect what the apostle added " Hold fast that which is good." A thing was not false because it was old, no more than was a thing true because it was new There was a tendency smonest young inquiries to prefer the new rather than the old very largely because it was new His (the preacher's) word of council would be " fast that which is good even though it be old." He would advise them not to let modern eceptiem sap any of their beliefs in the everlasting gospel nor would he advise them t s accept even the best modem criticism as in any sense final and conclusive. Above all, let them cling firmly to the personal faith in God, and faith in Christ.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>The Rev C H Hocken preached at the above place of worship on Sunday morning. In the evening the RPv. Theodore Bishop preached to a large congregation from the words "A world of Sin, a worldwide Remedy." Mr Edward Pownall rendered in an excellent manner the solo "Honour and Arms," which was much appreciated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>MANCHESTER SHIP CANAL COMPANY LIMITED.</head>
        <p>MANCHESTER SHIP CANAL COMPANY LIMITED.</p>
        <p>The report of the Manchester Ship Canal Company for the half-year ended December 31st last shows that the Ship Canal revenue roceints were £163,008 and that the earienrtiture was £102,980, leaving a credit hatance of £60,028 The profirs of the Bridgewater department were £11,189. With the addition of £5,379 for bankers and general interest, the total profit on the working for the half-year was £76,596. The weight of toll paying merchandise which oa.sed over the Ship Canal during the half-year was 1,551,244 tons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>CURING THE DEAF, AND HOW IT</head>
        <p>CURING THE DEAF, AND HOW IT</p>
        <p>IS DONE.</p>
        <p>The January issue of the " Supplement to the Journal for the Deaf,"contains a description of some remarkable Cures of Deafness effected by the Drouet Treatment, and also many intsresting comments by the editor of this Otological Review, pointing out the fact that this method of treating Ear Diseases and Loss of Hearing has been successful in the most obstinate and complica- ted cases, as well as in many instances where the ail- ment had previously received the attention of several spfcialists without deriving benefit. These who desire to obtain full particulars of the Drouet Treatment can obtain the Journal and Supplement free by post on application to the Secretary, Drouet Institute, Regents' Park-road, London, N. VV. One of the cases reported is that of Miss E. Landon, who suffered from deafness, noises in the bead, and dis- charges from the ears for tiftee years. Miss London communicates the result of the Diouet treatment as follows :</p>
        <p>Swallowfisld, R.adfrie. Barks., Dec. 5:13,1901.. .</p>
        <p>Dear Sirs.—l write to say there has been no discharge since the 207 h November. The noises have ceased, also the hearing so much improVA that I should not think any further treatment necessary.—l remain, faithfully yours, E. LONDON. A. further communication, under date of December 12th, declares that the restoreion of the hearing and the cur 3 01 the ear trouble is complet,. Those who wish advice as to the application of the Drouet treatment for any special case should write for a Report Farm, which enables anyone suffering from deaf- ness, or any ear, nose and throat disease to submit their case to the Consulting Physician. This form is sent with the Journal and Supplement, and advice by correspondence is always given free of charge. All applications should be addressed to the Secretary, Regent's Park-road, London, N.W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Young People's Society.</p>
        <p>An intereeting paper on "The Position of Woman in 1801 and 1901' was read at the meeting of the above-mentioned society on Monday evening. Miss Porritt wss the essayist and in contrasting the ' position of woman in the two periods under dis- cussion, she was able to show that the woman of to-day occupied a superior position to that of her sister of a century ago, Whereas the woman of 100 years ago received a meagre education and was taught to think herself inferior to her brothers and that her one great aim in life was to get married, the wooaan of to-day was proving herself ,intellectually, the equal of man and by entering upon a professional career she secured aq independence which was undreamt of 100 years ago. Not only had the position of woman improved in the upper and middle classes, but those in the lower clases who were employed in factories worked under better conditions owing to the beneficial changes which had been brought about by the Factory Acts and other Parliamencary rarisures. An interesting discussion followed, taken part in by the Chairman (Mr R Wyllie), tkir George Wallington, Mr Thomas, Mr Gauld, Mr J Hawthorn and Mr C Eccles. The essayist replied and the meeting concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>NOTES ON UAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON UAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>Two more blank football days —blank so far as Warrington is concerned —have to be recorded this week Saturday should have seen the Warrington team at Huddersfield, but the elements could scarcely have been worse, so the fixture was postpened. On Tuesday the already over-due return engagement with Hull was down for decision at Wilderspool, but frost and snow had again combined to make the condition of things totally unfit for football. It is three weeks since the Warrington team was in action, and they ought, there- fore, to be very fresh and energetic when hostilities are once more resumed.</p>
        <p>Very few clubs succeeded in bringing off their matches on either day, but among the notable achieve— ments may be mentioned the successes of Halifax at Bradford, Swinton against Hull, and Runcorn against Brighouse on Saturday, while on Tuesday Runcorn stopped Halifax's long run of successes by making a pointless draw at Hanson-lane. Oldham got an easy couple of points at Brighouse, while Salford did a very smart thing in beating Bradford at Park-avenue by goal to nil.</p>
        <p>Tho Northern League table up to date is as follows : For Alvainst. _</p>
        <p>P WL 1) ... fa T YTS ... G T P PTS. Broughton Rangers 23 ...19 3 1...39 61-261...20 19— 97...39 Halifax *Swinton 22 —ll 8 3...2i 27-131...26 31-145-25 21 ...14 7 0...22 45-179...20 15— 85...24 Bradford 22 •..1! 10 1...26 40-174...21 32-138...23 Hull *Salford *Runcorn 23 —.ll 8 1...24 32-144-15 2)— 90...21 Oldham *Warrington *siutudet 4 2l . 1011 0..23 24-132...32 37-176...18 Leigh Huddersfield Bat,ey 'l9 412 3...12 17— 75...26 36-160 ..18 Brighouse Rangers 22 ... 2 19 1...18 9 63...46 67-293 5 *Swinton, have had four, and Salford. Hunslet Warrington, and Runcorn, two points each deducted for breach of the ules.</p>
        <p>30-</p>
        <p>There has not been much change in the positions of clubs, but it is worth while pointing out that Halifax have climbed up from a place in the last three to second from the top. How they have done it I don't know, for I have not seen a team this season which looked less likely to have such a run of successes than Halifax. They have excellent forwards, but the backs ' only stem an average lot, as an attacking force. Still one must admit that there is more than average merit about a team which jumps over the heads of competi- tors in this surprising fashion.</p>
        <p>It was hard luck that Shrove Tuesday should prow, to be ouch a failure from the footballers point of view, for from time immemorial it has been a great day for the national game, and there were a great many fixtures arranged. Only a few could be carried out, and it looks long odds on the end of the season being sadly congested, especially for those clubs which well up in the cup ties. Warrington have already important matches postponed, two of them at home, SO that in any case the monetary loss to the club will be, considerable.</p>
