      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>EXAMINEE,.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINEE,.</p>
        <p>FEBRUARY 14TR 1902.</p>
        <p>The errors of the Useless Wei-Hai-Wei. present Government touch so closely the national reputation that it is hardly possible to expose them without damaging the latter. This is the case with Wei-Hai-Wei. All through modern European history runs like a golden thread the theory of the balance of power. Our own Government is supposed to guide its policy in the Far East On this line. When Russia occu- pied Port Arthur, and our ships of war scuttled at the instance of Lord SALISBURY for fear of exciting the Bear, we were sold that diplomacy would adjust our apparent loss; and then, with a magnificent flow of language and use of every rhetorical device, Mr. Gosoitex announced the ac- quirement on lease of Wei-Hai-Wei. There were members of Parliament inclined to be sceptical as to the value of the new possession; but they were told they were anti-Imperialists,and if they had the proper enthusiasm of loyal subjects they would acclaim this restoration of the balance of pow et% A littlo later and we heard that the soldiers were playing cricket at Wei-Hai-Wei, and then we knew it was thoroughly Anglicised. The scheme of fortification, however, halted. Two years passed, and last evening it became necessary for Lord ONSLOW to break the information in the House of Peers that Wei-Hai-Wei was useless as a naval basis or a military fortress, and that the one Chinese regiment now stationed was to be disbanded and replaced by a few Chinese police. But, he added, apparently quite unconscious of the anti-climax to the earlier vision, the place is healthy, and it can be utilised as a sanatorium for Hong Kong and Stianghai. Any more grotesque ending to the dream of a display of England's greatness, which was intended to hold in check Russia, to be a moral object lesson to the Chinese, and to inspire the Japanese with respect for England, can hardly be imagined. There are to gather the diseased, the wasted,and decrepit of our Colonists in the Far East, not often to be seen, it is true but to be a bye-word of Gteat Britain's vaunting, of the assertion of her power. The sea along the shore will be a sort of Pool of Bethesda, but with- out any spiritual moving of the water—a remote second-rate watering-place,whose visitors, if they suggest anything, will suggest the degeneratio n of our race. But to talk of this humiliation at any length is to touch the self-respect of the nation, and so probably it will be silently borne. The important point, however, is whether it is prudent to retain an indefensible property, which can be so easily taken by Russia at any time of clashing of interests. The policy of the present Govern- ment seems to be to fix up our military forces in South Africa and then to d:splay tempt- ingly a withered arm, or, to speak accurately, an arm in regard to which all hopes are withered, in the Far East.</p>
        <p>The House of Commons The Art of Army Contracting. can never, by any sort of circumstances, be the most appropriate place for discussing army eon- contracts. There is a danger, to judge from the temper shown in the questions addressed to Lord STANLEY, that the lessons of the remount scandal in regard to the purchase of horses in Austria and Hungary will be lost in the desire to hit some- body. That was shown by the vehemence with which the Nationalists pressed Mr BRODRICE for uformation as to the Court of Inquiry to which iGeneral TRUMAN is anxious to submit himself,and itiaeir concentration of feeling on Captain HAETT- GAN. After all, this eagerness to see if any partic- ular persons can be made "examples " is rather short-sighted. The prime responsibility must rest with the War Office, as represented in the House of Commons by Mr BRODRICK ; and the much more serious:question is whether .the arrangements</p>
        <p>for providing remounts as a whole are worthy or not of the eonlidence of the House. Mr BALFouR.</p>
        <p>- has declined to grant a Committee of Inquiry on Remounts at the preseat time, because, he says,it would dislocate the service by bringing home officers to be witnesses from all parts of the world, and taking them from their duties at home. That ground ior postponement may be good enough as affecting an examination of past transactions, but it is no reason for refusing a limited inquiry as to the methods of the present, which would not call men from all quarters of the globe. Just as Hr. BRODRICE called in a body of business men, com- mercial magnates, era practical members of Par- liament to advise him on the financial arrange- ments at the War Office, so he would do well to take counsel as to War Office contracts. The investigations would, of course, be private, and only the conclusions disclosed. We are glad to see that the " Spectator" takes a serious view of this matter, snd holds strongly to the opinion we held at the time of the remount debate—as to the danger 3 of the larger affair of concern being obscured by the smaller. The waste of money in the Austria-Hungarian purchases was bad enough, but have we any assurance after such a disclosure that our Governmenr is now buying in the best markets according to the shrewdest methods? The rumours which come from Ireland and Canada are disquieting. They may be entirely baseless, but, one muddle having been exposed, it is important that the War Office should be ready to relieve anxiety in other directions, and, above all, be able to show that it would be equal to an emergency on another occasion.</p>
        <p>Indirectly the remount discloses are having a bearing en the meat contract controversy. Again, the same tendency to exaggerate the personal element and overlook the much more importan principle involved is apparent. Mr. BERGL, who has held contracts for the supply of meat for Army depots in England to the satisfaction of the War Office, submitted the lowest tender for the colossal task of feeding our Army with meat in South Africa. It was quite natural that the War Office should wish him to be associated in this undertaking with some guarantor; as it went so much beyond any ordinary contract. What,how- ever, has set the heather ablaz3 is the report that Mr. RHODES, Mr. BEIT, and some others seem to be joined in the contract. There are all sorts of suggestions that this is " a job," and unworthy ejaculations come from some of the irresponsible Irish. Now, as a matter of fact, the associa- tion of Mr. RHODES and Mr. BEIT in any meat contract at the present time would be the best possible security for healthy meat, as the directors of the Chartered Company are parti cularly anxious to have the good opinion of the English people, which is far more important to them for some years to come than a largo profit gained at the cost of the grumbling of the British Army. A worse disservice to themselves they could not possibly, render. The experience of the War Office with Mr BERGL also encourages the belief that, in so far as he is associated with the contract, he will give satisfaction. But that is where the trouble comes. In the arrangements to which the War Office have assented, they have apparently opened the way for a trading in con- tracts. They have allowed an individual's con- tract to be transferred to a limited liability com- pany. It is no part of the real ground for com- plaint that both parties--the original contractor and the company—are satisfactory to the War Office. The mischief lies in the possibility of a man obtaining a contract, and selling it to a com- pany, and so getting a profit which is a severe loss to the State,because neither the actual contractor nor the country benefits. Nobody says that Mr Beam, has done this; but the Government are making it possible for other men. To that the attention of Parliament should be directed.</p>
        <p>THE delay in fixing which of the trans-isthmian routes shall be adopted for the America canal joining the Atlantic and the Pacific Oceans appears to be partly caused, at least, by the ad- dition of a third proposal to those the respective merits of which have so long been canvassed. Everyone knows about the Nicaraguan route, which Congress at first favoured; and of the Panama ronte, on which the ill-fated French com- pany has expended a large sum of money. The Darien route is less known, though, as a matter of fact, it was surveyed as far back as 1864, and its claims are rather revived than now first put for- forward. The proposal, however,has been brought into the range of practical politics lately by the powerful advocacy of Senator Hanna—Perhaps under Mr Roosevelt, as under Mr McKinley, the most influential man in American politics. The chief engineering difficulty would be to take the canal under a range of peaks of between one and two thousand feet, necessitating a tunnel two hundred feet or so high, one hundred and eighty feet broad, and three miles long. Thirty years ago this would have been an almost impossible feat. To-day engineers laugh at it. The Darien route is supposed also to have the advantage of cheapness as the ships could go through at the sea level throught. There would be no necessity for huge locks and retaining basins, highly dan- gerous in a country where earthquakes are fre- quent and often on a very largo scale. Lastly,the Darien route is by far the shortest, enabling a quick vessel to get through easily in three or four hours. Other considerations and the influence of interested parties may, perhaps, prevail; but on its merits the Darien proposal distinctly gains in favour.</p>
        <p>England has unquestionably lost a born diplo- matist in Lord Dufterin. A man of distinguished appearance, of unusal talents, he was trusted alike by the chiefs of both parties, and filled with the utmost dignity and credit some of the highest appointments available within the wide limits of the British Empire. Ile might have gone far in Parliamentary life, though it may. be doubted whether the loftiest seats of ambition were here open to him. Probably he himself felt his limita- tions as a party politican, and was not sorry, in 1872, to escape from Mr Gladstone's Cabinet. As a Whig of the older fashion, the tendency of the times was not to his liking, and he took U p cheerfully the work of the Governor- Generalship of Canada. :Lord Beaconsfield, however, first recognised his greatness as a diplomatist, and sent him as Ambassador to St Petersburg, a somewhat difficult post at a time when the London music-halls rang with the rhythms of bellicose and anti-Russian sentiment, and crowds were echoing the strains outside. On the turn in politics Mr Gladstone transferred him to Constantinople, and thence to India, where he established a policy traditions of which will last through the term of many a succeeding Viceroy. Of his work at Rome and at Paris little need be said ; nor is there room for a catalogue even of the honours which have been conferred upon him in the long career. In the chief Courts of Europe he was a persona gratissima, and his caution, ins tact, and his patience have smoothed over many an awkward place in our international relations. He leaves a name which, when the secrets of European diplomacy are revealed, will be found written conspicuously across the history of the latter part of the nineteenth century.</p>
        <p>-- It is not often that creditors in bankruptcy pro- ceedings have much cause for congratulation, but there are exceptions to every rule and such an exception has occurred in connection with the</p>
        <p>bankruptcy of John Garlick alias John Grant alias I John Brooks Bic. Five years ago the Warrington I creditors of this notorious individual found that: he had been arrested on serious charges connected with the conduct of his business which flourished with meteoric brilliancy for a very limited period in Orford-lane.- His assets realised very little and the creditors had the cold comfort of learning that they ;had been trading with an artful swindler. Much has happened since then in the eventful career of John Garlick. Escaping from Stafford Goal he once again entered into business," and under the alias of John Brooks he be- came the employer of one hundred work- men whom he engaged at box-making in Hull At length it became known that things were not what they seemed, and as it became whis- pered abroad that "John Brooks" was an escaped convict, he beat a hurried departure from the eastern seaport, leaving his " estate " to all and sundry. A paragraph in the newspaper caught the eye of Mr. Dibb, the official receiver for this district, and he immediately put in a claim far the "estate" on behalf of the Warrington creditors. This was extremely annoying to the Hull creditors. who had been bitten by the astute convict, but as he was an undischarged bankrupt, the Warrington creditors had by law a prior claim to the estate. 'This has now been realised and the Official Receiver hasithis week been authorised to distribute the proceeds. The result is that the Warring- ton creditors will not .only receive their accounts in full but interest on the same from the time of the bankruptcy. This is remark- -ably good news for the Warrington creditors but we can hardly conceive of the Hull creditors shar- ing in their happiness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Small-pot Insurance Policies may assuredly be reckoned as the lat,3sc novelty of the surprise ' ,order. They are said to be applied for and issued an. large numbers. Like insurances effected on ships and their cargoes, and certain other under- takings outside the accepted and established insur- ance system, they are made at Lloyd's. Would-be insurers against this particularly unpleasant" con- tingency have simply to consult a directory for some broker giving his address at Lloyd's who, on application, will supply the requisite information. It may be stated here, however, that the premium payable for one hundred pounds, to accrue to the insurer in the event of being smitten with small- poit,is half-a-crown for a vaccinated person,or three and fourpence if not vaccinated. No more eloquent testimony, perhaps, to the value of vaccination would be possible. When, as s pure matter of busi- ness, men are prepared to risk their money on an opinion.; it may be fairly assumed that they have at least the courage of their conviction. Actuarial business, founded upon patiently collected facts and statistics, obtained from the very beat sources, to the abso' ute exclu,ion of sentiment. is. nothing if not exact. Hence, it is left to be inferred that the chances of taking the dreaded infection bear, for the unvaccinated to the vaccinated, the propor- tion of 3s 4i to 2s 6d. Policies for larger sums are issued, and especial arrangements May be made in peculiar cases.</p>
        <p>"February Fill Dyke" has justified its character- istics hitherto in most parts of the provinces. lt came in toughly, and readers were duly reminded that meteorological statistics show some of the sharpest weather during the second month of the • year. The prophets predicted a dry February, however, *with a temperature rather below the average. The north-easterly winds and gales experienced during the first half of the month nave proved extremely trying, and must have been keenly felt by the poorly-clad, half-fed, and out-di work people among us. Reports from all parts of of tee country have a good deal of sameness abut them, and the Continent, also, appears to have been included io the cyclonic zone. Nor can it be said that we are apparently verging upon the fringe of the visitation as yet. It will be remembered that it was a late as the tenth of March some years ago that the great blizzard paralysed nearly the whole of the western counties. Considering the severity of the cold experienced, the marvel with some is that *We have not had either more snow or more skating, or both. Formerly. February brought a little. diversion in the shape of St Valentine's Day, bat yesterday the shop windows of the fancy stationers served only to remind 11A of how the glory of the good Saint Valentine has indeed faded. Ugly cari- catures of human nature, plain and highly roloured, are all that are left to recall a once hilarious or a happy time,</p>
        <p>Americans have a,habit of reminding us that we have no weather to boast of in England —only samples. But we do not forget that, by their fre- quent visits to 4is country, they themselves appear to find a chirm even in this kind of variety, and those of them who have been wintering on this side the" herring pond" this year must have dis- covered other things to twit us about, as regards I both the weather and its accompaniments: In many parts of the provinces, people area litt'e con- cerned about the prevalence of small-pox, which has for weeks past been so rife in London and evinced a tendency to spread. At the same time the influenza fiend is said to have laid hold. of as many members of Parliament as it did seven years ago, when the visitation was very severe, and, by holding both he Speaker and his Deputy in its grip, threatened last week to bring our legislative assembly and its business to a standstill. The cir- ctimatance that stnall•pox is so much in evidence just now, in certain parts of the country. would, perhaps, have occasioned less anxiety were it not for the fact that the epidemics 9f this disease are apt to occur in cycles. It seems as though, at a particular times the subtle infection seizes upon all whom it can reach, and then, having exhausted the suitable Material upon which it feeds, it disappears for a succession of years. The public are naturally interested in the pre- cautionary measures i\dopted by local authorities 'while newspapers are compelled occasionally to direct attention thereto, although it is hoped that the interest will in no case be allowed to develop in the direction of alarm, since there is high authority for the belief that fear of infec- tion disposes towards it more than anything else. The best thing that can be done is for the inhabit- ants to try to keep up their general health, and possess their souls in patience in full confidence that the sanitary authorities will discharge their duty in the matter with all diligencle and every .regard to the importance of any need that may arise.</p>
        <p>Building Regulations vary in different provincial disqicts, and in framing their bye-laws, some authorities effect originality uf purpose and de'3lme to be Roiled by those of greater experience. Accord- ing to P. "—rmineering," the regime observed in nine ,of our viikges out of ten is more stringent than are the conditions which prevail in London; a cir- cumstance which is attributed to the interference of anitary authorities, and the "model bye-laws A of the Local Government Board." Such regulations it is believed exercise a Prejudicial effect upon the welfare and progress of rural England generally, Where landlord and tenant e maybe equally desirous Of introducing improvements th hand of the No one, former is stayed by restrictions. nowadays would think of minimising the importance of sanitation i but it iS quite possible, through the display of too much zeal, or bre% little officiousnesii, to do moral harm than good, through the medium of these over-rigid regulations. The contemporary alluded to, for in- stance, points to the condemnation of old cottages ‘ as unfit for habitation where the building of new ones is only possible to the owner who happens to be a rich man. The result is that many cottages • disappear, never to rise again in the shape ofrnew ones, and thus the rural landscape loses much of its charm and picturesqueness, if nothing worse hap- pens, and the rural district is in this way more or lees denuded of its population- ,</p>
        <p>Sea-water for the Streets was a subject much dis- cussed by local authorities, in various parts of the provinces, and by the newspapers, some two years since. Corporate and other public bodies have,like Individuals, to live and learn, and the result of action taken, not two only but twenty years ago, by the Bournemouth municipal authority, brings this truth home rather forcibly. About the time mentioned, the governing body of Bournemouth Considered a water-scheme that would enable visi- lore and residents to have seawater baths in their respective residences would be an additional a•trac- Um to the town, and, at a cost of £20,000 pipes through which seawater could be pumped into the water towers and flow by gravitation through some</p>
        <p>thirty miles of pipes, were laid all over the town. But the Scheme has.since proved an utter failure. It appears that the suction pipes were frequently choked, and that the suppl) became irregular, with the result that the number of customers gradually dwindled to-very few: It was then suggested that the sea-water might be used for Street-watering purposes; and this for a time, was very acceptable —to coach-builders. But what was profitable to carriage-makers was the opposite to those who .owned the carriages, since the road dust, moistened with sea-water, was most injurious and destructive to the varnish with which , the vehicles were beautified in addition to which the water had a tendency to rot the roads. Under the circumstances it is not surprising to hear that ths Bournemouth Town Council have decided to discontinue the use of sea-water for laying the dust in the streets. But [the lesson taught is not for tiouruemouth alone; for many authorities, at Places far inland aS well as others nest.' the coast, had contemplated the nossi7 'bility of effecting local et:onomies by laying down maius and availing themselves of the use of siea- water, both for watering the streets and for baths in the houses. • It is quite Dos 4itile; of course, that the last word has yet to be said upon the subject. Only one side, indeed, appears to have been heard, and it can scat cely be believed that the other will give in so quietly and completely Without offering some kind of explanation. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>CH ARAM ER SKETCH. The Valetudinarian Bachelor.</head>
        <p>A CH ARAM ER SKETCH. The Valetudinarian Bachelor.</p>
        <p>By W H. lathwood</p>
        <p>Mr Gaskell sneezed thrice and stopped. He leaned against a gaslamp for pupport and a cold perspiration broke over him. He drew in a deep breath and ex- perienced a slight difficulty in doing so. He tried an- other and felt a stitch in his side. He fancied that there</p>
        <p>was a degree of feverishness, for'his hands were hot; but his brow was cold enough—so were his ears, and this was a good sign ! "Is it pleurisy or only bilious- ness ?" thought Mr Gaskell. "Is it inflammation of the diaphragmlor wind F" He ruminated eagerly upon the proceedings of the last few hours in order to ascertain how far he could have contracted pleurisy, The night was cold and damp, just the night for diseases on the chest. " Hang it,' thought Mr Gaskell, "what a beastly climate this is in spite of Count de Mirabel's panegyrics! " Mr Gaskell remembered too, that he had held two minutes conversation in Tottenham Court-road with a friend, and had felt a slight chill. "I wish he'd been to the deuce first," thought Mr Gaskell. Then his mind went out in search of a few facts in fat/our of biliousness. "That stuffed pork might have done it," he thought "Beer, too, never does agree with me and then that port." 'He had only had about seven glasses altogether, but it might be , Again Mr Gaskell drew in his breath. The pain in his side had increased. "It's pleurisy sure enough" he muttered, and perspired even more freely. It was certainly at his right side and might be attributed to liver, but this slight ray of hope was immediately quenched in the general gloom of his morbid imagina- tion. As he stood under the lamp post with his hand- kerchief to his mouth he died, in his own mind, a pain- ful and lingering death in a hot stuffy room, with the gaslight turned low—took a pathetic farewell of his invalid sister—saw himself buried by individuals who were too well fed upon the occasion to be decently indifferent—heard Jones, in good health, say to Dodson in better—" Have you heard of poor Gaskell's death ?" —had already composed his own epitaph -and was pathetically considering the funeral wreath in the coUntry Church-yard and the schoolchildren innocently prigging flowers therefrom, when— " Keb sir ?" said a hoarse voice.</p>
        <p>" Cab, yes, and drive like blazes," exclaimed. Mr Gaskell from his handkerchief, making fo: the door, through hich he slid. "Rite yer are sir! wot address sir," said the cabby 'opening the roof. " Terrace, Floxton—Shut down that roof " still from the handkerchief.</p>
        <p>" Wot sir ?" said the cabby: " Hamit " exclaimed Gaskell lapsing into German in hii excitement—" Elgin Terrace, I say, drive on, and shut out that cursed draught ! ' " Rite yer are, sir" yelled the cabby, shutting it out. Then he soliloquised, " Wot's hup wiv the 'ealthy lookin ole buffer'—he muttered,as he beneficently lashed his jaded steed to help a fellow-creature in distress— " Wotever's hup wiv him ? " Ouse afire—missis bunked—or wot ?" Then a thought seemed to strike him. ." Ad a tooth out "he muttered philosophically or wy the henkycher ?" Then he gave his undivided attention with the butt- end of his whip, to the jaded steed as is the manner of men when they work for a fare ! They went on quickly. Mr Gaskell could hardly make out the streets. He still sat with his handkerchief to his mouth. He wondered whether the cab was damp and instinccively tucked his coat tails under him. lie saw the shops fly past, also the mud. He heard the butt end of the whip and mentally resolved to reward that cabby for his kind exertions. At the same time however—but with no reference to his own case—he began to wonder how cabmen could possibly stand the sorts of weather they had to en- counter. This led him to the subject of cab-horses. He fancied the conditioit of this latter must be, of neces- sity, even worse than that of cabman. Poor noble brutes : -he thought incidentally, bred for the service pf man -which is only another way of saying that their existence is infernal, for no service could be harder. Poor noble brutes (it is the horses. Mr Gaskell meant) so swift, so generous so docile! What animal is so hardly dealt with? The little lamb is 'permitted to enjoy itself for a season before it gets its throat cut? The gentle cow enjoys a life of repose and so does the gentle bull! The cock's life is ideal from an Ottoman point of view. The dog has nothing whatever to complain of except occasion- ally the cudgel of Bill Sykes which he can evade if he a knowing enough—or a tin can which he can't if it gets tied to 'his tail ! But the horse—the noble horse ! Cabined or stabled, cribbed, confined,—but not too much of it ; worked from morning till night—and often times until next morning; hungry, cold, suffering, until he sometimes, and always dumbly, drops down dead ; lashed, kicked, poorly fed, emasculated ; and, at length inevitably knackered ! What hell of exist- ence more horrible ? Mr. Gaskell—wholwas a Theos- ophist—hazily began to believe, as he considered the subject, that the horse must be a re-incarnation of spirits who have been very naughty in some former dispensation and who, in the equine shape, are work- ing off " bad Karma " which is Hottentot for bad deeds. He also began to believe that no better mode of working off "bad Karma" could possibly have been devised, than to be made a four-legged creature remarkable for beauty and strength, docility and grace: forced to rush a great city twenty hours out of the twenty-four ' • to have a four-wheeler fastened behind ;—and upon the four-wheeler, ye gods, a cabby! The birds of the air get shot or snared ana are soon done for! So also with the rabbits and things of that sort. Otherwise their lives are pleasant. The fox is only run to death once in his life unless he a foolish enough to:escape, when the thing is repeated. With the exception of this trifling and very temporary incon- venience he enjoys himself. The savage beast, the ' poisonous snake, the bird of prey, the noxious insect, all are simply destroyed wheu they can be got at, before which event they have a very pleasant time of it. But the horse -so hard is his life since he has been taken under man's protection -the horse, God wa, is even worse off than a London shirt-maker ! Mysteries of existence are always humorous when you take an out- side view of them. For instance, what !could be more musing than the reflection that one living creature of most harmless disposition is compelled to rush broken- winded, half-dead, and with a four-wheeler behind him, through the bitter wet night, and another sits in- side the same four-wheeler comfortable and warm and amusing imself with a pleurisy-stitch! The latter reflection was his own for Mr. Gaskell had just been roused by a twitch in his side which inv. mediately diverted his thoughts to more serious matters. At the same time he heard the butt-end of the whip again administered—a cabby always does this: when about to stop—and they drove up with a jerk. Mr. Gaskell alighted. He still held the handker- chief to his mouth. He had the fare ready. "Fare, and a shilling extra for speed, cabman," said Mr. Gaskell through the handkerchief. " Thenky guvnor," said Jehu with a smiling face, Ithenky and good-nite ! ' "A word, however, before you go" said Mr Cashel' severely, and with a look at the dejected horse, i" The application of that whip sir—the way you have treated that animal on the road is something abominable. I have a very good mind to take your number and have You reported sir ! " Z.Jehu still stood on the pavement His mouth and eyes opened wide with astonishment. He gazed first at the horse—then at Mr Gaskell, then at the shilling extra for speed. After a moment's consideration, he ' gazed at Mr Gaskell again as one gazes at a lunatic and kept his eyes fixed, whilst he deliberately and silently mounted the box, settled the horse cloth about his loins, took up the reins and turned the cab round. Then he said quietly _ Look ere guvnor,if it's my number yer wants tyke my tip and get it. Go darn to Sandringham ter-nite wivout fail. 'ls R'yal Ighness is a pussonal friend o' mine and so is tom Welcher is 'eadgroom. If so be, as it's not too late ver may, get in wiv 'ard knockin.' If so be as the prince is not retired yer may see 'im. Wen yer see im, tyking of corse, the percortion of menshnuf bizness o' state, the Prince may give yer a letter o' hinterduction ter Tom. Now Tom knows my number, and w'en yer gets it, w'ich may be a leetle diffykult, cum back agen—get yet reportin' done—put , yer gums in a sling, and then 'ang yerself for a hepple- faced ole mug I With these words rapidly uttered Seim again admini. ' gated the butt-end of the whip, and floundered sway ' The indignant Gaskell, suddenly re minded of ()wish as rapidly disappeared into the house</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN WESLEYAN CIRCUIT.</p>
        <p>The total amount raised in the circuit in aid of Foreign Missions for 1901 was £284 15s 2d. The amount for 1900 was .2255 103 31 snowing an in- crease of .£29 4s lid. The income for 1901 is the highest which has been reached since 1892. LIFEBOAT RESCUE OF A RUNCORN CREW.. .</p>
        <p>The Ikinsey lifeboat Mary Isabella was launched in response to signals of distress from a schooner in the North Bay at 9.30 p.m. on Saturday. After a smart launch the lifeboat reached the vessel, which was the Ellesmere, of Runcorn, Captain Jones, bound for Liverpool with potatoes. The crew of three bands landed after the lifeboat had stood by for two hours. The vessel was left in a dangerous position close to the slurs,. A RUNCORN DIAN'S ADVENTURES AT SOUTHPORT.</p>
        <p>At the Southport Police Court, on Friday, firnest Worthington, a Runcorn man' was committed for two months for robbery with violence. The pre- vious Monday an old man, named John Orford, a St Helens farmer,travelled to Southport,and at Rain ford Junction the prisoner, who was at that time unknown to Orford, joined the train. They got into conversation, and found they were both bound for a bowling match at the New Inn, Southport. They drove to the place from Southport Station in a cab, and in the afternoon were returning in the same way when Worthington snatched Ortord'a watch frem his pocket, and, holding him back rifled his pockets, stealing 12s. When the cab stopped he sprang out, handed the cabman 2s and ran away. Re was arrested the same evening by Dat ctive-Sergeant Howard at Southport Station Prisoner pleaded guilts, and asked for another chance. • _</p>
        <p>I.IOOD TEMPLAR CONVENTION S.W. LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>The half yearly`meeting was held at Widnes on Saturday last presided over by Bro J Lindon C.C.T., St Helens. A good representation of members was present from Earlestawn, Warrington, Ashton, St Helens and Stubshaw Cross. The reports of the various lodges showed that active work was being carried out all over the area. Distinguished visitors Bro G B Walker,D Treasure, Sister Duncan P.D.V.T, and Sister A Hughes all of Liverpool, were received in the usual ways. The election If offiehrs resulted in the following being elected to office, 0.0 T. Bro J Lindon; C. Conn. Bro G Clarke, Earlestown ; GB. Superintendent Bro GB Turner, St Helens; Sister Mrs E Woods, Earlestown ; C.V.T. Sister Mrs Hatton, Earlestown ; C Sec. Bro Smith, Ashton; C Ilar.Bro J Speakman,Ashton;C Chap Bro J Lodge, Ashton. The next session will be held in March at Earlestown and in April at Leigh. Bro G B Walker and Sister A Duncan gave addresses thanks being:tendered to them for their presence. Tea followed,after which a public meeting was held. The Chairman was Bro James Lindon, Speaker Bro G B Walker, Bro Wilson of Earlestown also took , part in the programme. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this authority Was held on Tuesday iit,the Dutton Workhouse when Mr A R Norman J.P., presided.—There were also present Messrs T Wright, J Knowles, T Baxter, A H Talbot, J Spruce, E Clarke, G PAlling, I' Dariington, J Bolshaw, G F Ashton (oferk), and W E Iliugh (deputy clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>FRODSRAM AND DISTRICT RITANGELICAL -CHURCH COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>FRODSRAM AND DISTRICT RITANGELICAL -CHURCH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The secretary, Mr A E Rimmer, wrote under date February 6th notifying that the communication re the providing of preachers for services at Dutton, was read at their last Council meeting and that the thanks of the Council were due for th 3 appreciation of the services it had rendered. APPOINTMENT OF DELECIATES TO THE POOR LAW CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>The Chairman Moved that Messrs Pilling, Wright and the clerk be appointed to attend the Central Poor Law Conference to be held in the Guildhall, London, on the llth and lab March next. Mr Spruce seconded and the motion was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>AVP3INTMENT OF 6M</head>
        <p>AVP3INTMENT OF 6M</p>
        <p>On the motionO(MT-P7l-11n-g,s-ec- o-lid—ed by MrKnowles, Sarah Richardson was appointed cook at a; salary of £25 PP MIOUTR. APPOINTMENT OF BOYS' CARETAKER.</p>
        <p>It wag res -dyed on the recommendation of the House Committee that!: Mr S E Miatrim be appointed boys caretaker and general assistant at a salary of £25 per annum.</p>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>It was reported that the payments for out relief were as follows :—Runcorn £B5 ; Frcdsham, £25 ; Bud worth £27. It was further reported that the balance from the last meeting was £823 74 7d ; receipts £57 ctrques drawn that day £lB3 161 4d ; balance, £7l 11q 3. Pa,sed on the proposition of Mr Wright. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES. [BY A CORRESPONDENT 3</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES. [BY A CORRESPONDENT 3</p>
        <p>It is worthy of note that the Runcorn Rural Council have decided to petition the Local Govern- ment Board to forward s sufficient supply of calf lymph to the doctors practising in the Runcorn area. So far no small-pos cases have been reported in Runcorn but already, persons are getting re- vaccinated for fear the scourge travels Runcorn way, As it is the doctors have a difficulty in pro- curing the lymph from the necessary Authorities but it is to be hoped that the resolution of the Council will quicken the attention of the Local Government Board in the matter.</p>
        <p>The cycle club dinner, held on Weinesday, was, I in every respect, a complete success, owing to the I Indefatigable efforts Of Mr Booth. The dinner was ' served in first-rate style, while the concert was one of the best in the annals of the club. The speeches too, Were of a high order and in one an import- I ant pronouncement was made to the effect that the site for the cottage hospital would be definitely settled within the next three weeks. When this has been done the first step towards providing what should prove to be a boon and a blessing to suffering humanity will have been accomplished.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Brighouse Rangers have shown themselves to be the worst team in the Northern League, but weather conditions such as prevailed on Saturday are apt to place the worst on a level with the best, and it was very satisfactory that Runcorn won comfortably by, two tries to nothing. The two wingers—Flannagan and David Janes—were re- sponsible for the tries. David's return was hailed with delight, and for a first turn out, he gave a smart show. Plannagan once more justified, him- self and with luck should prove a great acquisition to the team. •</p>
        <p>"The most improved team in the,League" has been the phrase trumpeted of Halifax for some weeks past, and their performances have certainly given ground for the phrase. Bradford, Swinton and Leigh have each had to yield the palm with the best grace possible. It caused great surprise there- fore,in certain quarter when onguesday the Linnets checked the victorious career of the Yorkshiremen in their own hilly fastness. A pointless draw was the record at the finish. Ba 4 as was the weather many Euncornians accompanied the team.</p>
        <p>NOTHING NEW UNDER THE SUN? YES. THERE IS Mellor's New System of Treating Diseases of the Eve is absolutely unique. Thousands of Cures. Book IoItBII.—IdELLOR, Eye Specialist, 161, Oxford- street, Blanchester. (12).—Advt. ANTIQUII Wrought IRON and COPPHR WA If, for wedding presents, etc,—GABLAND, Bridge St,—Ad Vt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>rim EXAM IN ER FRI ).A V_ FEBRUARY 14, 1902.</head>
        <p>rim EXAM IN ER FRI ).A V_ FEBRUARY 14, 1902.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RU IAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this Council was hold on Tuesday at Dutten WrkhoiBe itr J Knowlas presided and there were *1 30 present Messrs T Wright, T Barter, H Talbot, J Spruce, E Clarke,G Pilling, T Darlington, J Bolshaw, Iloylarree, G F Ashton (clerk), W E Hough (assistant clerk), Vi Adams (medical officer of health), W Diggle and W Swinton (surveyors), and J Farrington (sanitary inspector.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>'SMALLPDX AND VACCINATION.</head>
        <p>'SMALLPDX AND VACCINATION.</p>
        <p>_ The Clerk to the County Council forwarded a circular with regard to small-pox and vaccination, and also a pamphlet issued by the County Medical Officer of Health giving instructions as to ,the means that should b 3 adopted to prevent the spread of the disease. The Clerk informed the Council Chit he had rent a copy of the pamphlet to the medical officer and asked him to report whether the terms of the pamphlet should be dessetnidated.by large posters or by distribut- ing the pamphlet. Dr Adams thought that posters would serve that pur- pose as well as being economical. It was pointed out by the clerk that the pamphlets only cost Si per 1,000 whereupon Dr Adams thought that pamphlets would serve the purpose better. Mr Talbot after further discussion moved that a ISullioient number of copies be obtained to give one to each house in the Runcorn Union.</p>
        <p>TO' ABOLISH THE CONSCIENCE CLAUSE. Mr Wright pointed out that in WO cases in which people had not been vaccinated 13(il died; in the same, number where all bad been vaccinated only six died ; and where, in the same number there had been re-vacci- natioaa not a single death had occurred. He would like to petition the Local Government Board to amend the Conscience Qlause, and would propose it, if necessaiy himself. Mr Clarke seconded. The Clerk pointed out that notice of motion would have to be given, as there were some members of the Council who might oppose it. Mr Wright said he would give notice of motion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>LOCAL GOVERNMENT BARD AND THE WETLY OF</head>
        <p>THE LOCAL GOVERNMENT BARD AND THE WETLY OF</p>
        <p>CALF LYMPH.</p>
        <p>Mr Roylance drew notice t] the fact that the Local Govaament Board were not supplying the medical men with sufficient calf lymph to carry out the vaccination of persons. To those situated in an infected area it was a serious matter. The Clerk pointed out that the Local Government Board would sent lymph if they possibly could but there was a limit to the supply. Mr. Roy lance moved that the Local Government Board be petitioned to supply their medical officers with lymph and the motion was carried atter furthur discussion.</p>
        <p>THE RUNCoRN WATER. The Clerk read a letter, dated 31st January, from Mr E Marshall, secretary to the Runcorn Urban District Council, furnishing particulars of the cnemical and and bacteriol igical analyses of water obtained from the County Analyst and from the Bacteriological Depart meet Thompson Yates Laborat3ries, Liverpool Univer sit; College respectively. From these analyses th Council would see that the water was free from sewag contaminatisn. The committee were very pleased t - be able to submit such satisfactory analesea to the Rural Council. It would relieve the minds of the authority fro n the anxiety which they evidently felt when they received the analyses of samples of water taken by their inspector.. As the Council would be aware great care is required to be exercised in the taking of samples of vie.ter` for the purpose of analysis. The Sanitary Inspector, when the letter had been read, said that such_care had been taken by him as an, man in Runcorn would take. He took the Samples for nalysis himself. Tke desk said that be had asked the .views of the rnodical officer on the letter And 49 would now report 'thereon. . 4.Dr Adams staid that the analysis Was satisfactory bu it was difficult to account for the great difference in the bacteriological results. The Rural Council expert showed 50,000 organisms per Cubic centimetre while the Urban Council showed:only 193 per cubic centimetre. After further discussion it was resolved, on the ad- vice of the medical officer that an effort should be made to get the same sample ef water taken in the presence of the different officials of the Council, to lo- afterwards sealed up and sent to their different experts far further analysis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>-THE MOORE itIIESTION.</head>
        <p>-THE MOORE itIIESTION.</p>
        <p>Mr C H Mason. soticitr to the Landon and North Western Rail way Company, wrote saying that as a great deal of tioae had been taken up with this swat question, and it appeared to have arieen, from an otni mon on the pars of the Ship Canal Company. be ha I advised. his • clients to. erect the stile and afterward. to discuss with the Ship Canal Company's salicitors th question of who was liable for the expense. THE THELWALL NUISANCE AGAIN. ' Siting Massey, Cuerden-terrace, wrote to the effect I that Pr Adams bad called to see him about his wife, and I did not seem pleased at his writing to the Council re the !nuisance. Dr Adams was -informed by the writer that I the stench came from the tannery br3ok. He refused to see his wife, and said it was very strange that he (the doctor) could not smell anything. He was only a work- in man aud did not want to vex the mediaal officer, but what remedy had a poor 'man against sickness, death and doctors bills which were caused by a vile stench? Dr Adams pointed out that the man's wife was not suffering from typhoid and that his letter was based entirely on a misunderstanding. If anyone was suffer- ing from illness in Ifhelwall it seemed,to be put down to the supposed nuisance. RAISING THE SLUICES AT LATCHFORD. Admiral Parry writing to Mr Ashton from Boscombe, Hants, forwarded a letter he had, received from Mr Colin Maclveri Warrington. This was to the effect that he believed that the Ship Canal Company were to raise the sluices at Latchford Locks 2 feet 9 inches for the ' Purpose of sending mcra water down the river to War- rington. This might or might not affect the drainage of ! Thelwall Parish, but he thought that it was a matter I that should be looked into by the Council. Be had heard thatithere were two more cases of illness in Weaste ' lane The admiral in his note observed that if the Ship I Canal Company did alter the sluices as proposed he expected it would dam up their stream, or at all events the cesspool that they had already erected at the South side of the month of the culvert. Was it a question for the Rural District Councilto take up or would he recom- mend them to take it up for that would only mean more delay. • The Sanitary Inspector said be was uncertain whether the proposed alterations would make any difference to Tbelwall or not. lie would inspect the place and report at the neat meeting.</p>
        <p>A NEW ROAD WANTED TO WESTON. Mr A T Smith, general manager to the Castner- Kellner Alkali Company, Limited, wrote under date 10th February, to Mr Ashten, the clerk: Dear Sir,—Referring to your visit here to-day with the Assessment Cmunittee we confirm conversation which has taken place on the subject of the total want of adequate road access to these works. As pointed out, we have been paying rates steadily now on a high assessment for seven years, and the place is no more accessible to-d4y than it was when we started, and It is difficult for me to understand what we pay rates for. To get to Runcorn Station from here, one has to travel about three, miles, through Weston village, or else run the risk of breaking one's legs going up Sandy-lane. We shall be glad if you will bring this matter forcibly before your Council at the earliest oppor- tunity to see if something cannot be done, either in the direction of making a new road, or putting Sandy-lane in something like respectable conditiqe for traffic. Mr. Wright, one of the delegates,seh that Sandy-road was in a deplorable state and sadly needed attention. The owners of the land were Major Orred and a Mr Bank es who were not at all willing to give any land on either side for the purpose of widening it. A portion also belonged to the Runcorn Urban Council. Mr. Baxter said he was present at the recent meeting of the delegates of the Cheshire County Council and the Urban Council with the view of taking the road over. It was of course, quite out of the question to make a County main road from the Runcorn railway station to Weston, as it could not be connected with any other main road. What was wanted was a new main road from the station to the works which would be a straight road. This could easily be done if the land owners would not be so stupid. Mr Evans was the stumbling block in the way. He seemed to take a special delight in being a sprag on the wheel of progress. He was sure the °minty Council would consider such a scheme I favourably by granting a liberal sum towards such a road. Weston was becoming a most important place owiug to the new works, the salt works, etc., and such a road would become a most important one. Mr Wright said that the general manager gave them the idea that in the advent of a new road being made the works would pay something towards it. It was resolved, on the proposition of Mr Roylance, that Messrs Wright, Pilling, Baxter, Guest, Chadwick and Talbot form a committee to consider the matter.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF DELEGATES. ORegolved that Messrs Moseley Williams and Talbot and the Clerk be appointed to attend the proceedings at the Sanitary Institute at Manchester, and that their expenses be paid.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S "ALEXANDRA "</p>
        <p>BISCUITS.</p>
        <p>TRODUCED';</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>"PICTURESQUE IRELAND.</head>
        <p>"PICTURESQUE IRELAND.</p>
        <p>Lecture at Earlestown.</p>
        <p>The second of the course of lectures promoted by the Newton-in-Makerfield Lectures Committee was given in the Town Hall, Earlestowo on Wednesday evening. Not- withstanding the inclemency of the weather the halt was Well filled. Dr Street presided and the lecturer was Mr Richard Kerr, F.G.S.,whose subject was" Picturesque Ireland." The Chairman said he had pleasure in introducing to them a gentleman,who.he was sure wou'd give them alt very great pleasure. Be would take them all from'this cold and snowy country of ours to the Emerald isle, and show them all the b3autiful geographical and historical portions of that country. Mr Kerr was well qualified to do that, he was an Irishman ty birth and they knew an Irishman would speak well of his own country. Tee Lecturer said when he got over one difficulty he I had, which was peculiar to all Irishmen, that wali ex-1 treme bashfulness, he hoped to be ante to speak dis- tinctly enough, s that they could all hear and uncler- stand him. They knew that Ireland was an island, and that an island was a piece of land surrounded by water, but that Was not the definition of an island in Spain in the days of Drake. The Spaniards taught their children that an island, was surrounded by Englishmen.. He was going to take them in imagination round the Coast of , Ireland and sometimes he would take teem inland. Be did not want them to fancy that Ireland was an unin- teresing country. Be was always trying to get English people to g 3 to Ireland for their holidays. 'Englishmen, generally when then bad any money to , spend went for their holidays to Feance, Germany and lup the Mediterranean. By all means, he 'would say, let 'them go, but see Ireland first. Afterwards they could go to the Continent, they could go to Jericho if they liked, but see Ireland. Irishmen were more nearly related to US teen the Coetinentai people. If they could educate the people of Ireland to be as free as the English- man. and not to be under the thraldom of anyone, and resist the ways of the agitator, but to be independent and to know the resourcefulness of their country, they would make Ireland whit it was '1,500 years ago. I nation's grestness'depeaded upon the education of ito people. Be believed if the late Queen had visited Ireland more 'frequently it would have done more for the country than all this Acts of Parliament. 1,500 years ago, Ireland was in a prosperous and enlightened condition, it was the seat of learning, and well-to-do E oglishinen used to send their sons to be educated in Ireland, because there were seminaries of learnicw and religious institutions estab- lished throughout the country. When St. Patrick landed in the country in the year 432, he found educa- tion established and the country prosperous.. The first missionaries who ever landed in Eogland to preach the gospel were missionaries from Ireland. In the year 593 the Dane e overran Ireland, killed the clergy and des- troyed their buildings of learning, and Ireland hart never recovered. Die relnainder of the lecture consisted of limelight illestrations of such ol Ices as Dublin, Bay of Killiney, Vale of rayons, Vale of Giendalough, Sir Walter Raleigh's house (Youghal), Blarney Castle, Londonderry, The C use way, Belfast, &amp; a:, 'plentifully graced with Irish bole and witticisms.— ['tanks to the Lecturer and Chairman terminated the moetin g.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>FATAL COLLISION WITH. A CYCLIST.</head>
        <p>FATAL COLLISION WITH. A CYCLIST.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternoon at the Frodsharto Police Court Mr J 0 Bate, county coroner, held an inqa-st touching the death of Mr William Sharp, farmer, who died from injuries received in collision with a cyclist o Monday. Mr Joseph Burton, Runcorn, appeared on be- half of Peter Earlam, the cycliet. Samuel Sherri, 8, Alvanley-terrace. Froasham, son the deceased, said his f eller was 70 years of age and resided at Bridge,Farm, Frodsham. Witness saw him ahout half an hour after the accident occurred.He stated to witness that be was badly hurt and did not think he would recover. He died on 'Monday at 155 without having recovered sufficiently to give any account of what had occurred. Dr William E Borten deposed that he visit id the deceased almost immediately after the accident. 'He was quite unconscious and was suffering from concussion of the brain and several external injuries. Arthur James Ashworth, Sandfields, Frodsharn, stated that on Friday morning at nine o'clock he was going down Bridge-lane Frodaham. He noticed a coal lorry moving up the hill by Dr Burton's house, which was two or three feet from the footpath. Be did not see it pull up. Alter passing the lorry be met aMr Brett and the deceased. Both were walking abreast of the lurry in the road on the far side of the lorry. After he had got: two or three yards oast he heard thud prior to which he heard the sound of a cyclist's bell several times. Wit- ness did not actually see the collision. When he turned round be saw the deceased partly in the gutter and partly in the road. Witness wen!: for the doctor. He also saw Peter Earlam lying on the floor alongside of him and the bicycle a few yards away. Abraham Brett, Newtown, Frodshani, a carter, said that on Friday morning he was in charge of the hone and lurry in Bridge-lane at the time mentioned. He saw the cyclist coming over the brow of the hill as he was going up. Witness intended to turn into Dr Burton's and had got three parts across the road prior to seeing the cyclist. Witness beckoned with his hand for the cyclist to go on the off side as he could not git back again. The cyclist accordingly turned the other way, and the witness stopped just as the cyclist passed. Be did not know anyone was behind his lurry, but beard the noise as soon as Earlana had passed. When be turned round he saw Earlam picking the deceased up He heard Barham ring his bleycle boll for him but not for anyone else. He would be riding at about six or seven miles an hour. He had seen cyclists riding faster. The Coroner : In your statement to the police you say that he was riding faster than he should have been. la that your opinion?</p>
        <p>Witness :Ye, sir. Coroner: Did you say at the time that Earlam was riding too fast. Witness : No Sir I The Coroner: Did you notice whether he ha:i on a free wheel, . Witness : Yes Sir. _ _ •</p>
        <p>- Francis Bratt, father of the last witness, deposed that he was with the deceased at the time in question. His son the last witness was in charge of the horse and cart. Mr Sharp spoke to him as he came out of his garden and they both walked up the road a bit Witness then left deceased and went to his lurry and left de- ceased walking in the road. He did not see the cyclist until he had passed the horse. He then turned round and saw Mr Sharp hetirate as to which way he should get out of the road of the bicycle. He saw the deceased struck right in the chest. The cyclist seemed to be going at a moderate speed. By Mr Burton; He did not move at all. He stood in the middle of the road. By the Foreman: There was sufficient room for the cyclist to have passed between Mr Sharp and the lurry or Mr Sharp and the footpath. Thomas Warrell, Bridge-lane, stated he was behind the last witneas's cart in charge of a trap. He noticed the lurry on the wrong side and saw the two men. He noticed the cyclist when half way down the brow. The cyclist tried to miss the cart and then ran in Mr Sharp. He was going at an ordinary pace. Peter Earlam, Main-street, Frodshatn, elected to give evidence and after corroborating the evidence of the previous witness swore that the deceased moved out of the road into the channel and then for some unaccount • able reason stepped back again when witness was within a yard of him. It was then too late to turn the cycle aside and he crashed into him. After a short deliberation the jury reCurnei a verdict of accidental death entirely exonerating the cyclist from all blame. Mr Burton, on behalf of the cyclist, expressed his deep regret at his client having been the unwitting cause of Mr Sharp's death,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>~---. THE HARRISON CHARITY CONCERT. [A r RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>~---. THE HARRISON CHARITY CONCERT. [A r RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>There was a crowded and appreciative audience at the Runcorn public ball oa Monday night on the occasion of the 9th annual concert in aid of James Harrison. 4, Wiyerralace, who, ten years ago met with an accident which rendered him incapable of earning his livelihood. The best instrumental and vocal local talent was brought to striking combination and the result was a concert, high class and refined, in the strictest sense of the word. Mr Taylor presided end briefly extended a warm wel- comeito those who were supporting the movement. The instrumental portion of the programme was left in the hands of the Runcorn Orchestral Society, who, under the conductorship of Mr Horace Handley rendered in fine (style Rossini's " Tancredi," Sullivan's " Gon- doliers" and Sousa' "El Capitan " the second com- position reciving the most meritorious treatment. Toe Runcorn Male Voice Choir conducted by Mr Yates splendidly rendered "Martyrs' of the Arena." " The Pilgrim's chorus" and " Spartan Heroes," being vociferously encored. The time and balance of the choir was all but perfect and with the addition of a few tenors has the making of one of the best choirs for miles round. Miss Phoebe Hum- phreys with the sweet songs " Eileen Alannah " and "Angus MacDonald" quits captivated the audience and in response to a well merited recall sang " Home sweet Home." MissE Clare was equally sticcessful and her voice was heard at its best in the " Sign of the cross" for which she was recalled. Mr W Efopwood, proved to be' in excellent voice as was evidenced by his magnificent rendering of Elatton's song "Revenge "and "Off to Philadelphia" for both of which he was encored. He might interpret the soul of a composition with less apparent straining, and when be can do this his musical success is assured. Mr W Shaw's musical qualities , again manifested themselveS both in solo and duet. In company with Miss Clare he gave a charming rendition of The Spider and the Fly." After hearing such a concert one cannot help wondering why a first class vocal society in Runcorn cannot make progress. Mr W Yates, the secretary, is to be complimented on the suc- cess of the entertainment and it is to be hoped that it wilt be as much a success financially as it was musically.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>1 FATALITY IN AN—IIr)NE.N-DRIZT—INCIIES—T AND VERDICT. ' On Thursday afternoon Mr 8 Brighouse, county oorouer, held an inquest at the Town Hall on the body of:Frederick Clare, 41 years of age, of 50, Queen-street, who was killed at the ironfoundry of Messrs Dagfish and Co., on Wednesday. Mr Graves, inspector of factories and workshops,was In attendance.—Evidence was given that on Wednesday forenoon Clare was superintending the placing of a repaired leather belt on one of the shafts and was standing on a ladder for that purpose. The custom was for the machinery to be slowed down when the belting was ready to go on,but betore that time was reached Clare in some way slipped and his arm was caught in the shafting. He was dragged round and dropped to the ground, death being instantaneous. Mr Graves said it would be better if • instructions were given for the machinery to be stopped on all occasions In future. Floe jury re:uraed a verdict of .6 Accidental death," and expressed the opinion that no one was to blame in the matter.</p>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON BIGAMY. CASE.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes, on Thurday, before Mr Justice Bucknill, a man named Wiiliatn Bromley (38), labourer, pleaded guilty to having, at Warrington, on December 29, 1900, bigamously married Ruth a Bleckensopp. Mr Ford, who prosecuted, said that the prisoner was first married in 1891 to a woman named Snell at a Wesieyin chapel in Newport. There were four children of the marriage, and subsequently the prisoner deserted them and his wife. He came to Warrington, where he went through the form of marri- age with the woman Bleultensopp, who had kept house for her mother. In the following April be deserted her, and she Was now left with a child. There was no sug- gesti n that she knew he had a wife living when she married him. —Die Lordship said the case was a bad one, and pas3ed seatencs of four veers' penal servitude.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RUNCORN CYCLE CLUB ANNUAL DINNER.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CYCLE CLUB ANNUAL DINNER.</p>
        <p>The annual Cycle Club dinner was held at Wilsons Eict3l on Wednesday evening when over eighty members and friends sat down to an excellent repast which had been served by Mr Vi'ildgoose, propriet)r. Aftrr the dinner had been done substantial justice to, the tables were cleared and tie after proceedings took the form of a concerc, during the coarse of which be annual priz3 distribution for regular attend- ance, etc., took place. Mr S Taylor, J.P. presided and he was supported by Messrs F.l Norman, .1 P., R Post:Lett, J.P., R H Pringle, .W B3otti (non. erc.) J Shaw and other local gentlemen.</p>
        <p>Mr 9 Taylor, in opening the proneolinge, said that it was with extreme pleasure that for the fourth time he was with them on the club's account. The club was now looked upon in Lancashire as well as Cheshire as being a most important one. During the last twelve months there had been an increase in the membership of over twenty. (Applause.) The total membership was now eighty-four witch was most satisfactory. He was truly sorry to find that the ladies had fallen off. Even the three they had last year had desertsi them. (Laughter.) He could not, however, think that Mr Booth was to blame with his pleasant countenance and genial affable manner. In order to start the ladies of Runcorn he would publicly ask Mr Norman and Mr Posnelt, to persuade their wives to j Mn. (Laughter and applause.) They were both cyclists (Laughter.) If he had a wife he would make her join. (Laughter.) lie was very gratified to find tha suacess which had met their efforts in rtipoing the cvale parade, the band contest and the other means of raising money. in the endeavour t establish a cottage hoopla' (Applause.) The club had done well indeed o raise the sum of £6O towards such a worthy object.. (Applause.) He hopeu the club would long continue to supp,rt the parade. Steady progress had been made during the twelve months. They then wanted £l,OOO and their Idea was to purchase a buil ling and turn it into a cottage hospital. On the advice of Mr Wilding it was decided that a secondhand building would not provide s suit able hospital. He had got out plans which had been gone carefully into. It would cost about £1,500 without the equipments. They had, however, got so far advanced that it was only a question of a site and within the next three waits that would be decided upon. (Applause). They would then have to get out the plans, the estimates, and set the °entracte. This year, owing to the ceronation, was a most important one and he was sure that Runcorn would rise to the occa- sion and not be behind any town in the Kingdom. He hoped their permanent object would be the building of a cAtage hospital and that they would raise the £2,000 There was a great deal of credit due to Mr Booth for what had been done. Be had no doubt that when the hospital was established the club would raise a sub- stantial amount towards the endowment of the hospital. After the customary loyal toasts had been honoured Mr F J Norman proposed the toast of the Runcorn Cycle Club. He said that all recreation shou4l be a means to something nobler and, better. Tills, he thought was the true meaning of Rudyard Kipling'a recent remarks en Athletes in general which had been so vigorously resented. They in Runcorn, at all events, could say that they were working for something noble in the shape of a cottage hospital. He congratulated the Run- corn Cycle Club in taking the part they had done and said they would never regret it. (Applause). Mr Booth the secretary, in replying, said that he was pleased to see the president and vice.presidents with them and extended a hearty weleome, on behalf of the club, to them. There were over 50 members who turned out in the runs, which was a moat satis factory number, Sir John T Brunner had written him enclosing his sub- scription and expressing his good wishes for the welfare of the club and the furtherance of the hospital scheme. (Applause.) Mr Congreve C.E. should have distributed the priz but it was annonnced that the Seeretary had received a , telegram from him stating his inability to be present. Mr Posnett however was present to distribute the prizes. Mr. Posnett, who' was received with applause said that he had always taken a deep interest in the cycle club and the Cottage hospital scheme. So long as Mr Taylor was the president he could safely say they would never have any difficulty with the club It was far better to wait a little longer and have something that they would be proud of, and that they would not get tired of in a few years. Phey wanted a site that would enable a man to get better and this be trusted they would shortly get. He was sorry Mr Coagreve could not be with thorn owing to his deep interest in the club and the cottage hospital scheme. Mr Posnett then handed Vote 1901 attendance medals to the following gentlemen :—Mesars W H Harrison, T Crawford, W Booth, G Whinyates, W Hied, W Les. During tha evening the following items were ren- dered :—Pianoforte selection. Mr Sidney Essto ; con- juring exhibition by Mr J Clare;humorous sour selected, Mr H V Sayers, encored ; song, " G )od-bye," Mr D 0 Parry, encored ; banjo and concertina solo, Messrs Waterworth and Shaw ; conjuring exhibition by Mr J Clare ; recitation, Mr 13 V Sayer* song, " Death of Nelson," Mr D 0 Parry ; song, " The Irishman in France," Mr Laudenburg ; banj selection. Mr J Water- worth: song, Mr Landenburg God save the King. Votes of thanks to the host and hostess and the Chair- man brought the meeting to a close.</p>
        <p>'Spectroscopic Work at Recent Solar eclipses." This was the subject ot an interesting lecture de- livered in the Masonic Hall, on Monday evening under the auspices of the Literary and Philoso- phical Society by Mr John Evershed, F.R.A.S. Mr. J D Buckton (president) presided and in the course of his remarks said that spectroscopic work brought to them greater wonders than anything else in astronomy. They not only were brought nearer to other bodies but were able to ascertain what those bodies were composed of and draw from that a great many conclusions.—Mr Evershed then de- livered his lecture, which was illustrated by a num- ber of lantern views shown by Mr H N Houghton. The lecturer said that an analysis of the sunlight by the spectroscope had enabled them to under- stand things which in pre-spectroscopic days would have been thought wildly impossible. It was now known precisely what elements were distributed over the sun's surface in the form of highly heated gases and the conditions of the pressure acd tem- perature under which they existed. Incidentally he mentioned that' the spectroscope had not given them very much information about sun-spots ; on the contrary it had rather misled them. Son-spots revealed a state of things inside the sun which apparently could not be explained in accordance with their present knowledge. The lecturer proceeding to deal with his subject proper, described his spectros- copic work at two recent solar eclipses. lie went with a party organized by the British Astronomical Association to Lapland in 1896 Cloudy weather prevailed on the day of the eclipse and the expedi- tion was fruitless. A party who went to Nova Scotia were more fortunate, and the lecturer was able to show a beautiful photograph of the sun flash obtained by Mr .Sh.ackleton. lie want with the expedition to India in 1898, and under favour- able conditions obtained some very valuable flash spectrum photographs..at the time of the total eclipse and at a point ot the re-appearance of the ' sun, He was not quite so fortunate in 1900, when he went .to Algeria, where, owing to a mistake of the English authorities, he selected a place which was barely inside the shadow area. The photographs he was able to take, however, were extremely in- teresting and valuable, and amply rewarded him for the great trouble he had taken over the work. Mr Carruthers proposed a vote of thanks to Mr Evershed. Mr Ruddock seconded, and said that the results of Mr Evershed's work had been of great value to the Royal Society and the Astronomical Society,and had upset a good deal that other astro- nomers had hitherto thought correct. The vote was heartily carried, and Mr Evershed returned thanks.</p>
