      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>A WARRINGTON WOMAN _ABANDONS HER CHILDREN-HOW</p>
        <p>BEUEF MoalLY IS SPENT. at the Warrington Pollee Court on Saturday, before Mr Pirchall (in the chair), and Mr R T Yalu) ough Kate Kelly, Ros—yard,a married woman,the wife of a soldier now serving in 7muth Africa, was charged with neglecting her two chil- dren, aged tea years and three ye tr ree•pectirely.in a planner likely to emse then. unnecessary suffering and injury to he Ala.—Mr Crompton (Messrs Browne and Crompton) app ared to prosecute on behalf of the N.5.P.0.0 and stated that the prisoner's husband was at the front. The woman bad au Income of 18s 4d a week while be was away-45 Ild from the looal war relief fund, 9s lid from the War Office,and 31 6d, part of her husband', pay On Saturday last she re- ceived £2 13. Ed in relief and then wont away, leaving her ohildren playing about near home, and didn't ret•irn until Thursday. There you; no'hing to eat in the house except a little fish. She had been to Northwich, and had spent all the money in drink. Goodness know what would have become Of the children had it nor, been for VI • klndue,s of Inspector Hackett and two nelghbours named Mrs Smith and Mrs Bllison The women kept, the children until Tuesday, when the Inspector took them to the Workhouss, where they had since remained The woman returned to town on Thursday night, and admitted she had labindoned It he children —Mr Taylor (p dice court missionary) I stated that the assist- ant), from the local war relief fund hal been stoppei, so tar as the prisoner was concerned.—Prisoner was lent to gaol for a month.</p>
        <p>SHOOXING HoME-LlFit AT WARRINGTON. At the Warrington Police Court on Tue iday, John William Jackson and Annie Jackson man ,nd wife, residing at 2, liar y-street, appeared before the magistrates on t .e charge of neglecting their five children.—Mr A. Browne conducted the prosenstion on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. and said the male prisoner was in constant wore earning 2ts weekly. The oat, articles of furniture In th .1 house were a chair and a table, There was no bed whatever. the whole f .mily sleeping on some balding in the corner of the room. When the Inspector visited the place one of the boys had nothing but a shirt on. there was neither fire nor food in he house ang the children were in a shocking condition thr 'ugh want of care. A baby aged two years weighed only Bozs, whereas the nominal we.ght was Nibs 2ozs• —P S Bell that inspector Hackett gave evidence.—Seperlntendent Monaghan said the prisoners served three months in October. 190 for neglecting the Children' —The Bench decided to inflict a sentence of three months' imprisonment with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>MINOR OASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Messrs Birchall (iu the chair) and R T</p>
        <p>Fairclough. IMPRISONMENT Fla THEFT. —Thomas Martin a stranger to all toWn, was eharged with stealiog a shirt of the value of 2s 6d from outside the pawnshop ot Messrs Jackson. Church- steret. on F iday morning He pleadei guilty, and was sent to gaol fo. a ralnth.--Jmnes Leslie was also sent to prison for a similar period for stealing a number of empty bottle) from the yard of the Patten Arms Efotel.--auperintendent Mon.ghan stated the facts in each case. IMPUDENT THEFT—SMART tiENTENCRI.—WiIIians Mackie, 2, Ship-yard, was charged on rernaull with stealmg, by means of a trick, the sum of 30s,the moneys of William John Laird, on Saterday evening ; amid also with stealing a horse's bit, ef the value of 3s, the property of John Maginn.—Superintend- ent Monaghan said prisoner had no regular employment but did odd jobs for people incluil ig Mr oirilliam John Laird. Os Saturday night. at about seven o'clock. Mr Laird was about to go into Messrs Co per's stores to pay a bill when he ' heard an expresserain coming into the "tattoo. As he wished toot oh that train he gave the prisoner a bill and a sove- reign and a half sovereign to go and i av the account, Which amounted to 255, telling him to bring the change to the cab stand. That WAS the last se,n of him. Mr Laird went to Mr Cooper and found that the bill had not been paid and prisoner had never been there. A cab diver named Littlewood, hearing that pison.r had bolted with the money went on the platform about 7.50 when the trains for Manchester and Liverpool were due out. Ile saw the prisoner and ask-d him if he had paid Air Laird's bill. He replied Certainly I have paid it." Littlewood had his auspicious mad communicated hem to P.O. Monks. The coastal-, a went on the station and nt being satisfied, the man WAS taken to the Po ice Ration where it WAS found that he hKi not psi I the bill. 01 being searched 27s in money was found on the prisoner, tie having 'spent the rem sineler in drink and in buying a tieket to take him to Manchester. 1-fe had attempted to destroy the ticket, but it had been recovered. With regard to the second charge, the horse's bit which hal been identified. by Mr Magian as be- longing to him, WAS loan! o • the pri.soner. who said he had bougbt it in Manchester. Prisoner Was sent to gaol for two months.</p>
        <p>MONDAY —Before Mr F Monks (In the chair), Councillor Smethurst. Messrs Birchen, Hayward and Th-ruto A VIOLETT Wt art —Mary Thompson a native of Brad• ford, was charod with having been drunk and disorderly in Horsemarket-street aS. 10.55 on Sunday night and with assaulling P.C. Phillips at the Police Statioa—P.O. Phillips stated that the prisoner was very drunk and made use of obscene language to passers-by. tt • asked her t• desist, and she commenced to swear at him. He thereupon took her Into custody and when they reached the Police Station she I became vary violent and kiced witness on the shins. Sias had to be restrained from repeating the assault Williamson said tont the prisoner when broright in appeared to be quiet, but without any warning she rushed at the con— stable and made a severe attack upon htm—A fine of J.os, and costs was imposed. ViGRASCY.-it young woman named Bllarsbeth Barnes. living at 3. Pilkington's-yard. was charged with waving ben found sleepin , in an outbuilding early that morning —P.S. Williamson proved tilt. case —ft appeared that the prisoner bad for some time past been living a miserable life, having been dismissed from her sitnation owing to misconduct, and the Bench deaided that the best course would be to send bar to gaol for three months in the second division, where she would have proper care and attention.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY—Before Aldermen Sutton (in the chair). Hutchin- son, and Shaw Green. kir Thornton and Dr. Fox. A SOLDIER'S SIT.PICIoLTS CONDUCT. —John Allen, a soldier was charged with being R suspicious character, found fre- quenting Sootiand-road at Ave minutes past ten o'clock the previou• nigh, wth intent to commit a felony. He pleaded not guilty —Superintenient Mowighan said that at about 10 p.m. on Hon lay the prisoner came into the Alexandra Vaults and had three pints of beer. The landloid then went into the kitchen anl shortly afterwards he heard th bell of the till ringing He ran Into the bar, and found the prisoner leaning over the counter, with his bands near the till. He held prisoner for some time, and eyent,tally Police-constable Heap took him into custody. Prisoner alleged that he was looking for 6cl which he had lost.--He was sent to gaol for fourteen days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Warrington Occasional :Court. sATURDAT.—BeforI Mr. 3. Nelld</head>
        <p>Warrington Occasional :Court. sATURDAT.—BeforI Mr. 3. Nelld</p>
        <p>ALLEGED Tittrr Or FOWLS AND POTATiEN.—Lawrence Whit., Manse% White, John aiannion and Aichsel Dowd, were charged with stealing two fowls and a quantity of potatoes valued at 9&amp; the property of George Wilkinson. farmer. at Acton Grange, on the 3rd ltist—.Air Wilkinson said the skins of the cockerels (produced) were similar to those of the fowls on his premises, tie saw the fowls alive on the 3rd inst. Three of the me i worked for Ism, Between hair-past two an i three 69 saw the four men coming out of a shanty on the farm.—Dr Wilki.son's son said be also SAW ths men ceme out of the shanty. —The skins of the fowls and he potatoes were afterwards found th I.e.—Prisoners were remanded to Daresbury Petty SessiOns.l</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MoNDIY.-:Befora Mr, G. L. Davies awl Mr _J. Olkleston,</p>
        <p>A GUN LlCENOR.—iamuel Shalicross was fined R 1 -and costs for carrying a gun for whlah he had no licence. PRO7ANE LiN4SIYAGE,..JAMOS Roie was tined 21 6d and 8s 6d costs and Illizabeth Twigg 10. Including coots. Or In default seven days for using °biome ~language. _</p>
        <p>No LINT ---John Dale was fined 10s Including costs for being In charge of a vehicle which should have had a lighted lamp attached. _ . _</p>
        <p>STREET ORSTRECTION.—Joseph Wilkinson, aged 17, William Knook ton. 14. Joseph Spence , 16, Frederick Cliffa, 14, Richard Malone, 14 James Rafferty, 14, William John Cheaters, 13, David Bizley, 13. Robert Giavan, 14, John Gavan, 16. and Frederick Garner, 13, were all summoned for wilfully obstruotlng the footway in He,th-rcad. on the 2nd February, to the annoyance of passengers thereon. Wilkinson, Malone, Rills, Barley and Garner Weeded guilty, and the others not guilty.—P 0 Wilding proved the offence, and the defendants were each fined Es Bd or seven days' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Local Commissions.</head>
        <p>Local Commissions.</p>
        <p>The following military changes have been gazetted :—The Prince of Wales's Volunteers(South Lancashire Regiment) : Seconded for service with the Indian Staff Corps, Lieutenant Edmund W E Milman and Second Lieutent A F Inglis. St • Mary's Church, Latchford.—Annual Dance. One of the mo4t successful dances ever held in connection with St. Mary's Church, Latchford, took place in the Parr Hall on Mon lay evening. The attendance numbered about 360, and dancing was indulged in until the early hour-. of the mrrning to the strains of Mr Percy Randall's band, The duties of M.C.is were ably discharged by Messrs P Delaney and C Wells. Much of the success of the event was due to the admirable arrangements which hati been made by a Committee, of which Mr G Bibby was the energetic secretary. The refreshment depart- ment was in charge of a ladies' cstnmittee, whose efforts to cater for the requirements of the visitors gave every sarisfaction.</p>
        <p>Manchester-Liverpool Electric Express. To comp'y with thestandiug orders ot Parliament a special general meeting of the proprietors of the Manchester and Liverpool Electric Enpress Rail- way Company was held on Wednesday at the company's offices in London to consider a bill pro- posed to be introduced into Parliament in the pre- sent session for conferring further powers upon the company. The Chairman explained that the object Of the Bill was to deviate a small length of their ' line through Self trd a 5. required by the Salford Corporation. The rerlution apuroving the object of the Bill was csrried. The Board of Trade has decided to sanction the plans for the permanent way. This includes the triangular erection sup- porting the Mono railway and the four guide rails. The plans are to be signed in a short time, and. when that has been done the C)mpany will ‘te able to proceed at once with the project.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>THE COUNTY MATCH.</p>
        <p>LAHCASHIRH v. YORKSHIRR,</p>
        <p>After considerable delay, the Lancashire County Com- mittee met in Mancheder on Tuesday night and selected the following team to oppose Y .rkshire at Leeds to-lay Satur- day. If Lancashire win they will tie with Cheshire,ancl have to play off tor the championship L '1 homes (Oldham), full back ; J Fish (Warrington), R Valentine (Swinton), It Wilson (Broughton Rangers), and A Hogg (Broughton Rangers), three-quarter backs ; W James, and James (Brought in Rangers), half ba-Ms ; J Rheum', and P Tunney (Salford), F Trenech• (Barrow), (1 Ferguson (Oldham), Whitehead (Broughton Rangers), 3 livens, (Iwlnton), 0 Wilkinson (Oldham), and i Heath (Salford), forwards. The Yorkshire team is as follows :</p>
        <p>_ . Taylor (Hu'l), full back ; Davies (Batley) and Deere (Huddersfield), wing three-quarters ; T D Davies (Leeds), and }hirers (Bradford), :centres ; Grace (Leeds) and Marsden (Bradford), half backs ; Broadley, Laidlaw and Feather (Bilsdfoid), Sutcliffe (Huddersfield). Riley and Bartley (iialLax), Ritson (Hull), and Starks (Hull Kingston Rovers), forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>SWINTON V HULL.</p>
        <p>The return match between these clubs w as played at Swin- ton. before a pole atteti lance of spectato,s. Owing to the ground being thickly covered with snow, it was doulek,ful whether the match could be played but as Hull had made the journey to Swinton it was decide-I at the last moment to clear the grounl and plav the match This was quickly done, bit it V/ AS left in such a soft condition that it was impossible for either team to give anything like a good dis- play of football. Hull won the toss, and Swinton commenced the game up hill an i asalast the avlud. Iney were q Mokly forced back to their quarter, where Parry made s yard attempts to get over the home line but he found Ohorley too much for him. From a penalty kick, however, Jacques placed a goal for the visitors. Then Swinton played up strongly, and Oavies co-nmencpl it gooi pasting run between Valentine, 'Millwork and Harnpson. which ended in the last named player scoring an unimproved try to' Swia on. Jacques next dropped a goal for Hull, woo lei at half-time by four point ,Ito three With the Wind in their favour Swinton commenced th 3 second half in a vigorous fashion, and twice Harnpeon was brought. down by Taylor when he appeared certain to score. Several hard-fought scrurnmag s took place On the visitors' line, but nothing accrued from them. and Parry and Franks repeatedly re ieved th 3 pre“sure, Brans missed an easy penalty goal arid, try as they would, Swin- ton could not cross the Hull line until the last minute, when their forwards rushed the ball over th visitors' line and Barnett scored an unimproved try Final score •—Swinton, two tries (six points) ; Hull, two goals (four points).</p>
        <p>HEIADF3RD V HALIFAX</p>
        <p>At Park Avenue Tne ground was covered with fairly thick snow. rendering good fo 'than dieloait As a result of the visiting forw .rd s uain; their eat, Halifax got a good position and Ytorley tried to get through, but failed, Brad- ford lost Wright thr iugh an amident, and had to play with fourteen men. Bradford pressed hard, and Mosby kinked a penalty goal. The hal(-'ime score was :—Bradtord, one goal (two points) ; Halifax. nothing. Bradford had still to play with to rteen men, and being pressed Bigg go: over, but Little failed at goal. Pollard and fi)bertson bad a Clear opening for a try, but they nulial it Bradford again were pressed, and (rota a scrummage Brown sent the ball to Morley, who scored. Little adding the goal. Beault : Halifax, one goal and two trial (eight points) ; Bralford,one goal (two points). HIINELET V. LEIGH.</p>
        <p>This return match was p *yet at Rol:islet. on a snow-clad ground. before a small attendant's. Leigh were heavily handicapped through Bennett at three-quarters Kight and Dunbavin at half, and t`lelr two county forwards being absent. Hunelet wire much superior forward but at back Leigh more than held their own Leigh started with great dash and swarmed round the If unslet line, but for a time they were held at bay. Eventually Whi'ely mulled, and Arrowimith dribbled over and !morel near the posts for Leigh olyneue kicking a goal II unslet played up strongly after this, but by judicious kicking and clever dribbling Leigh again got dangerous. Eventually the Huncet forwards rushed the leather to the Leigh line but the ball rolled over the dead ball line before it eould be grounded. Shoitly before the% int rval. after a perslstant atta:k, Whitely scored a try, no goal resulting, and Leigh thus led at the interval by five points to three. Almost Immediately after the r start Moly- neux, the Leigh half, got his thigh damag -d and re ired for tile rest of the game rowers went from three-quarters to halt and Norbury went from forward to tier ..ry-quarter. Smith of L-Igh got away finely but was busload near the line. Hunslet forced Leigh back, ant A Goldthorpe kicked a penalty goal Leigh now deterlora.ed considerably. Hunslet smothering them forward( and Whitely at last scored an unimproved try. Subsequently a ter bad mulling by the Leigh backs, Wilson lc ,rd A Goldthoepe placing a goal. Result :—Hunslet two goals three tries (ii points); Leigh, one goal one try (five pointy). RUNCORN V. RRIGHOLSE RANGERS.</p>
        <p>The return match took place on the Irwell-lane enclosure on Saturday before a very soarse attendance. Snow had fallen heavily during the night but even under such ctn. cumstances a very good game was witnessed, rhe visit we started. Butterworth returning Jolley shortly after started a passing bout but Richardson mulled. After a smart forward movement in which Butte:worth tackled in the nick of time play mettle I down very tam ,Iy. Houghton came well to the trout with several dodgy runs whlob completeLy baffled his opponents and Runcorn, more than once had singularly hard lines in failing to score. Richardson Warder and Butterworth kept up a severe attack on the visitors linerand a capital combining movement was crowned by Flannigan scoring at the xotoer. Runcorn continued to press, but the defenoi was all that could be desired, half time arriving wit's Runcorn leading by one try to nil. Runcorn °poised the secmd half by forcing several scrummages dangerously near the visitors' line Brigbouse however. maintained • splendid defence and repeated y got possession and in 'turn made several incursions into the home teritory. Kicking and smart following up left the aivantage with Runcorn. and ,fter repeatedly failing to score at the critical moment Runcorn gained another point by Jones grounding the leather at the corner, the pal kick failing. The game was several times stopped on account of the rough element. The game finally ended with Runcorn leading by six points to two.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>SALFORD V. BRADFORD.</head>
        <p>SALFORD V. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>The return match between these clubs took place at Brad- ford on Tuesday before about 4.000 spectators. The grouu was in a soft condition, and it was with the greatest diffi- culty the players:could keep their feet dalford won the toss, and played with the wind in their favour. A line effort ow the part of Williams and Bone eaded in Lomas getting possession and dropping a capital goal for Salford. This was all the sewing up to half-time. whets Salford led by two points to not ilog. In the second half Bralford had con- siderably the best of matters, but they found the defence of the Salford backs too much for them. and the game ended in a win for Salford. Result r —Salford, one goal (two points,; Bradford. nothing. Salaam:ma Y. Orassrioi.</p>
        <p>At Brirrbouse,betore 1,000 snectators.There was earns doubt about this fixture during the morning, but both sides agreed to play. Halt-time :—Oldham ten Poi rite , Bri,rhouse two points. The visitors showed their superiority in the second portion, but Saville defended well Iluzzey scored and Bpottlswoodo added two tries lieergusm kicking two goals. ltesult:—Oldbam tour goals sad five tries (23 point.) ; Brighouse. one goal (two points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>HALM V. RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>HALM V. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Halifax on Tu.sda F. before 6,000 spe3tators. Langley started ter the visitors, and Kitson failing to reply Runcorn attacked. A strong forward buret. headed by alley and Bartle, took play to the visitors' 25, where Jolley was tackled in possession. deverat packs were foisghb out in close vicinity to the line, but Hou rhton eventually cleared by a strong kick. Jolley and Biota irdson worked together cleverly and forced Halifax to defend, but Little cleared. Half time No score. On resuming each pressed in turn, the play par- taking of a give-and-take character, neither side being able to score, and tbe game ended in a pointless draw.</p>
        <p>BRoUGHTON R&amp;NGEBS V HLTDDERIFIELD. The return match between these clubs took place on the Rangers' ground on Wednesday before a fair attendance of spectators The home club was rrongly represent.d,bnt on the Visitors si -te substitutes had to be found for five of their first team players The Hangers attaosed strongly, and the brothers James commenced a fine passing movement, which ended in Wilson scoring an unimproved try. After this early success Huddersfield were kept well on the defensive and Stead crossed their line, anti scored a try. which W Jame. improved. Thli was quickly followed by a fine try on the part of 8 James, which W James failed to Improve.</p>
