      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>IN THIS DEADLY WEATHER.</head>
        <p>IN THIS DEADLY WEATHER.</p>
        <p>On SaturJsy, D.cember 14th, Mr Horne, r6gistrar of the cemetery at accrineon, had notice of six funeralq, eh fixed for that day. Tuat made sixteen tar the week —a number very much above the average. The dread— ful weather did it. In no part of the country was the wild weather mo-e keenly felt than in the high-lying lands of East Lancashire and the adjoining districts on the borders of Yorkshire. i is one of the inr s'.l deadly winters that ever fell on England. Night and day.</p>
        <p>, THE DOCTORS' DOORBELLS JINGLE, the undertikers are busy, and the graveyards are filling up. We need protection and cure. The beer you realize that you have a cold on the chest apply a Ben- son's Porton,' Plaster where the pain or oppression is felt. If you think two would be better make it two. No harsn if you were covered with them. They act at once and break up the congestion in a few hours. Use them for coughs, rheumatiarn,inflaenza, pneumenia,and all similar complaints. They are the most warming, comforting and healing remedy over mad.. Don't hither with salves, liniments, lotions or rubbing oils, or with the stupid and useless old-style plasters. Clap oa a Benson's and naught else. It stops the pale as a brake stops an omnibus and will make you laugh for the very ease and good feelinz of it. So you will keep out of NO and at er'rk in suite of the weather. But get Benson's Player, Berisin's only, of any chemist. For sale in alarrinetsn by H M Ashton, 63, Sankey-street, Blots Limited, 5 and 35, Bridge-street ; Hill and Co., Bettermarke,-street ; H Miller, 77, Bridge-street ; and J R Young, 40, Sankey-street.</p>
