      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>LIBEL ON WARRINGTON THEATRES. – -- A Quick Settlement at the</head>
        <p>THE LIBEL ON WARRINGTON THEATRES. - -- A Quick Settlement at the</p>
        <p>Assizes.</p>
        <p>Considerable local interest was taken in the case at Manchester Assiz,s on Thursday, week before Mr Jurtice Buckuill and a jury, in which Messrs William James Lunt, William Johnson, and Joseph Potter. proprietors of the Royal Court Theatre and the rheatre Royal, Wvrington, sued the " Observer" Printing and Publishing Com- pany, Limited, and if. Isherwood, from whom they sought to recover damages for alleged libel contained in an articlo whico appeared in that paper on the 30-h November.—Mr Shee, , Mr Roodes and Mr Raby appeared for the plaintiffs, and Mr Taylor, K.C., and Mr NfacConkey (intructed by Messrs Wools and Son, of Warring- ton) for the defenthmts. Mr SECHE proceeded to state the facts of the case He said the two theatres were called the Court Theatre and the Royal. About the time of the libel a syndicate had been formed or was about to be formed for the purpose of raising £7,000 or .£lO.OOO wi h a view to establishinz a new theatre in War- rington, and it would appear that the idea was held that the best way to boom the new theatre was to rtyl'o damage the old ones. The article complained of was headed "What about the new theatre ?" and ran as follows :</p>
        <p>"It will be seen that the theatre-going public have good reason for feeling glad and for anticipat- ing the local dramatic future, so to speak, with pleasure. They have grown heartily sick of the fifth-rate fare provided for so many years in War- rington, both at the lesding place of amusement in the town and at the place familiarly known to its habitues as the blood tub ' and the flsa pit,' &amp;c They have grown sick of it, and are sick of it still. 'Trips to Plum-Turn ' and such singular pro- ductions as Why Women don't Love Men 'are not exactly all that could be desired, The more intelli- gent portion of the community crave for something better than these surprising offspring of the per- verted genius Of obscure inkslingers. Conse- quently those who can afford it go to Manchester or Liverpool,where they can get something better." Mr Shea said the article meant that the acting at these two thearres was bad, that the theatres— at all events one of them—were dirty, and that the playa were written by persons of obscurity and of perverted genius. The libel was published on the 30th November last, On the 2nd December Mr Isherwocd wrote a letter to Mr Potter, in which he said ;—"I regret to find that we have publiqhed a paragraph about your theatres which may give you same little annoyance. I did not see the para- graph in proof, or it would not have appeared. Fray. accept my assurance that in next Saturday's issue we shall give equal promin- ence to a paragraPh which will make you some compensation for the annoyance you have suffered," The plaintiffs asked for an apology, and they received an answer on the 5h December which contained the words'• It is no libel, but nevertheless we regret that it appeared." The next thing the defendants did was to publish a paragraph in their paper on the 7th December to this effect :—" In last week's paper we published some details in regard to the new theatre. The remarks we passed have been taken exception to. We now gladly give more public expression to our regret." The defendants thus expressed regret, but though statements had been made about fifth-rate acting and about a certein theatre being a flea-pit,- yet there was no public acknowledgment ihat the statements were untrue. All they asked for now was that the defendants should submit to a nominal judgment and ,laoe all they had said. If they did that, if they would' say that the Theatre Royal was not a " flea pit,' that the plays were not of an inferior character, that the acting was not inferior, and that the plain- tiffs had endeavoured to cater for the public in a proper way, the plaintiffs would be satisfied. After a•consultatton between counsel and clients, Mr Taylor said he had advised his clients to agree to Mr shee's proposal—that there should be julg- ment for the plaintiffs for a nominal sum and colts His Lordship said he was sure the arrangement was a wise and proper one, and they would hope that those concerned who had been friendly in the past would be friendly in the future. He thought the letter from Mr Isherwood was written in the best possible taste and he ha l no doubt whatever that Mr Isherwood meant the words ha wrote, He ;thelearnedjudge)thou2ht the agreement arrived at was a very proper termination to the case, ELASTIC SToCKING3, KNEst CAPS, &amp;c.—Fm• Varicose Veins an i all Cages of Weskness arid Swelling of the Legs. Sprains ; they are porous, light in texture, and inexoensi ve and and are drawn on like an ordinary stocking.—Prices 4s 64, 7,61, and 10564 each —Postages free.—Warrx AND %valour Surgical Instrument and Truss Mskers,93, Rsnshass,street tep 0111 Bold-street, Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. CHARLES J. HOLMES, J.P.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. CHARLES J. HOLMES, J.P.</p>
        <p>A highly-respected and au exceedingly useful citizen has passed away in the person of Mr Charles John Holmes, JP. His death occurred at his resi- dence, Rougemout, Great Sankey, at .10 on Mon- day morning, at the advanced age of 81 years. Since 1896:Mr-Holmes had been an invali 1 and was unable to walk owing to paralysis. Frequently, however, he was to be seen in the streets in a bath- chair and occasionally he went out for a drive. It was while he was returning a few days ago from a meeting of the directors of the Longford! Wire Works Company, Limited, that he met with the accident wh ch, unfortunately, accelerated his death. He was in a landau.and when he had been driven some distance on his way home he ex• pressed a wish to be removed to another part of the conveyance. 'I his was done by the deceased's personal attendant and the driver. After having had his dinner Mr Holmes made a remark to the effect that he believed his leg was broken. An ex amination proved that he had received some in- jury, and it was decid,d to send for Dr Murray, of Penketh, who found that a small bone of he left leg had been broken. Mr Holmes was at once put to bed,but was very uncomfortable throughout the night. The next day his condition became worse, he was una'ile to at, and he passed away at the time stated, the shock to the system having caus-d , failure of the action of the heart. It is a singular °incidence that the deceased met with an acci- I dent in 1898 while on his way to the same works and at the time the accident was regarded as very serious.</p>
        <p>For a long number of years he served the town faithfully,conscientiously and with great ability,giv ing freely of his time for the puodcweal,aud we feel sure that the sorrowing family will have the deep- est sympathy of the public in their bereavement. The decemed gentleman was born on October 7th 1823, at liarldocketone Grange, Markington, near R pley, in Yorkshire He received the earlier part of his education at Preston, but when about nine years of age he went to Ackvvorth School, the prin- cipal school in connection with the Society of Friends, of which he was a member. He, however.</p>
        <p>did not cotnplete his education there, but subse- quently speat a year and a half at Mr Josepn 1 liklmundsen's school, Penketh• In 1835 he obtained a situation in Warrington as apprentice to Nit- William Edward Milner, outfitter, who was at that time in business at the shop which Mr C Jackaen- Holmes at pre,ent occupies in Bridge-street in partnerahip with Mr Booth The late Mr Holmes entered public life in 1847, when he was appointed one of the Police Commissioners ot Warrington, and he had long been the only survivor among those who signed the transfer of the old Commissioners' powers to the Corporation. In 1852 he married Elinor,second daughter of Jonathan and Elizab 4h Jackson, of Cildar Vale, neer Gars- tang, and in the same year became a member of the Warrington Board of Guardians, of which body he was cneirman for two years. He first, entered the Town Council in 1852, being returned for the South West Ward at a by-el,ction. lie only remained in until November of the same year At the Novem- ber elections of 1856 Mr Holmes wa-s returned to the Town Council as a representative of the North- west Ward, and from that year to hie retirement in 1893 he had been continuously a member of the ruling body of the town. He had not been in the Council many years before he was elected to the aldermanic dignity. He first became Meteor in 1863, and was again elected to the office in 1869. In 1870 he was placed on the Commission of the Peace for the borough.</p>
        <p>On August 22nd, 1894, Alderman Holmes, along with Major Pickmere, was presented with the honorary freedom of the borough in recognition of eminent and valuable services rendered to the town. By reason of an unfortunate accident which betel the venerable Alderman a few weeks previously, causing a severe shock to the system, he was con- fined to his house. and totally precluded from attending the public meeting which had been con- vened at the Town Hall for tee purpose of making the prisentation. This accordingly took place at his residence the following morning, being carried out by a deputation of the Corporation, in a quiet manner, for it was deemed advisable that, owing to the delicate condition of the recipient's health,any- thing in the nature of excitement should be avoided The casket containing the certifteate of the freedom of the borough WAS handed to Alderman Holmes by the Mayor (slderman Oreenall). The worthy Alderman was much affected, and requested his son, NIrC Jackson Holmes, to respond on his behalf which he did very suitably. At a meeting of the Town Council on the 6th October, 1806, the Mayor (Alderman James Fair- clone) read the following letter from Mr Holmes, resigning his position as alderman of the borough ; —" Dear sir,—Fr some time past I had been in ihopes of being able to resume my place in the War- rington Town Council.hut as that now seems impos- sible, the only course I can adopt is to resign my I seat on the Town Council. I enclose Is. with that object. In separating myself from the members of the Council. with whom I have always had most friendly relations, I desire to thank them for the many acts of kindness I have received at their hands." It was moved by the Mayor and seconded by Alderman Harrison, " that this Council receives with regret the resigna ion of Charles John Holmes, Esq., hon, freeman of the borough, of the office of alderman, and de4re to place on recor 1 their warm appreciation of the eminent and valuable public services rendered by him during his connection with the Town Council for upwards of 40 years " rhe resolution wee carried In connection with the fitiances of the Borough and the acquirement of and management of the gas works by the Corpora- tintts the late II? Holmes rendered invaluable ser- vice. For 33 years he was a member of the Finance Committee. • In politics, Mr. Holmes was a consistent Liberal, but he did not play a leading part in political war- fare. He was a strong educationalist, and for 40 years was one of the managers of People's College school, lie was one of the managers of the Penketh friends school. He was also a member of the Old Mechanics' Trigg, utA—nn institution which did much by means of popular lectures to enlighten the general public on scientific and other topics.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>LIKE THE STAB OF A KNIFE.</head>
        <p>LIKE THE STAB OF A KNIFE.</p>
        <p>Pains in the back likq the stab of a knife ! 111,7 many thousands realize that sentence daily by agonising tor- ture, and never find a cure ? Thousands of people to whom it iS agony to stoop endure the pain and think, after buyinz many retnedim, that they will never be without it.</p>
        <p>Women are I:ocularly enhj•ct to aching backs, which m•ke the round of the household a dailv _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mrs. I'l 11117 hes, of D iulting, Sh.pton Mlllet, Somer. setshire, was one of the many sufferers who gratefully acknowledge the help derived from the use of Dodd's Kidney Pills. Writing on Julv sth, 1901, she states that for years she suffered from acorn plias in the back and the shoulders ; the pain being like the stab of a knife. The doctor treated her for rbearaatimm, bat neither his medicine nor other preparationn afflrded permanent. relief. " For days at a time," she says, " I could scarcely move_aty)ut."</p>
        <p>Such was Mrs. Hughes' sad lot. But there was a change, which she relates in her own wordS : "I got a box of Dodd'a Kidney Pills, and am grateful for the benefit derived. I feel like a different person, for they do all that is claimed for them. I also find them a grand tonic, and if I take one or two pills after a hard day's work, or when a little run down, they soon brace M 9 up." That is Mr. flughes7 experience. What is yours ? You have aching baelr, rheumatism, sciatica, neuralgia? You have tried doctors, and other remedies, and have obtained no relief? But you hare not tried Dodd's Kidney Pills—the great kidney cure? They are a cera tail cure for all diseases arising from the kidneys, because they make the kidneys well, and when the kid• neve are well, the body is healthy too. Avoid further suff 'ring. and save years of future ill-health by taking Dodd's Kidney Pill/.</p>
        <p>Dodd'a Kidcierlis are 24 91 per box. of all Chemists or ma, frqe on receipt of prio by The Dldde Medicine Co., 23, frarringdm-svenue, London, EC. Remember the name, D-o•d d-'s.</p>
        <p>Suaden Death at Stretton. On Saturday afternoon a man named Robert Kerr, 68 years of age, a roadman in the employ of the Che.hire County Council, and who lived at Primrose Hill, Stratton, wee seen to fall down suddenly near Stratton Church, and by the time assistance was to hand it was found that he had expired The body was removed to the Cat end Lion Inn Latchford Brittania A.F.C.</p>
        <p>A dance in connection with the above club was held in the Co-operative Hall on Monday evening, about 120 members and friends being present. The muic was supplied by Mr. A. Starkey's band, the duties of M.C.'s being discharzed by Messre. R. Todd and W Dykes. During an interval Mr Filling- hats rendered a song in a creditable Dinneen Excel- lent arrangements had been made by the committee , and secretary (Mr F Foulkes).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WARRINGTON SOCIETY. Annual Meeting and Dinner.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SOCIETY. Annual Meeting and Dinner.</p>
        <p>--- The annual meeting of the members of the Warn ringtoa Society was held at the Old Academy o Thursday laot week, Mr William Long, JP, tha president, occupying the chair, Toere were als present Messrs t` Monks.. JP. Alderman Monks," II Smethurst,ll Stuart Psge,Venn and Councillor A Bennett (one of the secretaries.) ANNUAL ItEPORT AND BALANCE SHEET.</p>
        <p>Councillor I3ENNETT submitted the annual report and balance eheet. At the outset the report lamented the death of air John Croefield, one of the founders of the society, a descendant of a famous Warrington family, and himself, in many ways, a Warringtonian of whom they were all proud. The loss of several members througn removal from the distact was referred to but the report stated that the leakage had been practically made good by the ne w elections, whilst the financial position of the Society (though not entirely satisfactory) showed marked improve- ment. In accordance with the scheme 'announced last year the executive instructed Messrs Josiah Wedgwood and Sons, Limited, of Etruria, to pre- pare a tablet for the Old Academy (specially de- signed by air Albert Warburton, FRIB A). It was coefidently hoped that this would arrive in time for actual erection in the first year of the new Century. but the firm already mentiened who, (no doubt in consequence of their old associations with the place) had taken the greatest trouble in the matter, had unfortunately not been able to com- plete their experiments nor to execute the order in the time they expected. The sum of £2O 4s hat been subscribed to date, and it was hoped that members who had not already con- tributed would see their way to do so, in order to enable the executive to procbed with the erectionof similar tablets on all the other lecal buildings which deserved e )mmemoration, as this was one of the principal objects for which tie Suciety was formed Its collection of local literature and other relics was steadily increasing and the efforts of its mem- bers, many of whom were interested in photo- graphy,were adding a new attraction to its ancient rooms in the shape of a collection of the prettiest products of their various cameras. The Polyglot and Philatelic Society had made their horns once more at the Academy, and the former had, indeed, shown vigorous signs of life, and carried on its meetings all the year. In conclusion the writer of the report wished to call the attention of local societies of a literary, artistic, or scientific char- acter to the accommodation which the Academy had to offer and to cordially invite all Warring- toeians who were interested in any phase of intel- lectual life to identify themselves with the Society and assist it to increasingly fulfil the lofty aims with which it started. rite b ilance sheet showed an income for the year of £ll2 12r 21, and af,;er meeting expenses there was a d ,ficit of £5 17s 8 ',which was deducted from the guaran e acceunt.Thie account consisted of donations from the guarant )ra and after allowing Po, varinus deductions there was a balance in hand of £27 43 101, which was carried to the credit of the capital account. The Cumaareer said that as far as he could judge they hal every re..s in to be eatisfied that the club was doing the work for which it was originally instituted. He expressed his pleasure that the Society was endeavour- ing to perpetuate the memory of some f the old build- ings in Warrington of which thev wee, in a sense costodianii,if they lived up to their name of Warriniitm Society. Si:mating of the Old Academy, Mr Lone said he hoped all the members would strive to make it the home of everything that tended to the intellectual welfare of the town and at the same time obtain an amount of innocent amusement from it which would be helpful in the weary round and stress of life. He moved the adoption of the report and balance sheet. Alderman MONKS seconded and the motion was agreed to. ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr Fairhurst was el-cted auditor fdr the ensuing year And the following General Cunmittee WAS elocted : Dr Gu ,et Grull, Id.csfs J Fairhurt, 11 S Page, T J Lre, itobrt Garnett, W ii J Jenkins, J A Ewart, B M Ashton, C Madeley, J Birtles, S Wilson and William Monks.</p>
        <p>Ahe trty vote of thanks to the officisls concluded the meeting. THE DINNER.</p>
        <p>The annual dinner was afterwards held, a goodly company sitting down. Mr W L-mg presided. An eso-llent repast was served, the catering of Mr and Mrs Atkinson giving complete satisf mtion. Alter dinner the usual toast list was submitted, The health of " The Kmg " was enthuliaatically honoured, in the proposition of the CHAIRMAN. Mr J if SMETHURST pi—posed " Tee Imperial Forces." _</p>
        <p>• - • Ia responding Mr VENN r-ferrel to the new volunteer regulations and said they would have a very great effect in reducing the number of volunteers and also on future recruiting. That was to be regretted b.cause the War Office might say that patriotism was on the wane and try to force conscription. Mr H M ASeToN then gave a humorous recitation in his usual happy style. The toast of " The Lancashire Witc'acs " was pro- posed by Mr LI Gandy and responded to b 7 Mr B Newcombe. _</p>
        <p>ldr TtlOstaS LONGDIN then proposed "The good old town" and gave sorr.e interesting reminis- cences of his early experiences in Warring- ton thirty-six years ago. He said he had been in almost every town in England and whatever peopla might say about the dirty town of Warrington, which he was not a native of, he did not think there was a cleaner town in England, nor a more progressive town. , Mr F HONKS, J.P, who was greeted with the singing of" He's a jolly trod fellow," and cheers, responded. He said he was a native of Warrington his father was a Warringtonian, as was also his grandfather. His great- grandfather came from Risley, so the family had long been settled in Warrington and neighbourhood. He was very much grievedor annoyed,he did not know which was ' the most, at the action which had been t ikon lately in reference to what was called municipal tiading. A good deal of mud had been thrown, some of which rnigt.t stick, but he thought in a most unwarrantable and unjustifiable manner. Fie was quite certain of one thing, that while there might be some things that had been undesirable, he was further cr.rtain that there had been nothing illegal or that should give rise to the agita- tion which they had been going through for the last few months. Witk reference to the town itself, he con fessed he might be prejudiced, but he areed with Mr Long. din that he did not see many towns that he would prefer more to live in than Warrington. The Cakiamet; said that with regard to the action which had been taken by certain parties en Warrington and which he had been so pained to see, what came to his mind were the beautiful words in which the Judge summed up the case that he had before him a few days ago. The Judge said it was a gr ,at pity that Mere should have ben so little of that " charity which thinketh no evil." Let them all take those words to heart and in all their intercourse, whether in Warrington or elsewhere, let them try to cultivate that feeling of charity even If they saw things that they did not quite agree with. . .</p>
        <p>Mr WingunToN proposed "Art and Literature," the toas; being heartily drunk. _ . .</p>
        <p>Alderman PUNKS responded and in the course of his remarks referred to the effort which the Corporation was making to promote a bill to confer the powers of a School Biard upon them. He said that if the bill were allowed to pass it would have such an effe3t on the education of the town generally that the petiple. be thought, had not realised, If they could bring the educational interests under the control of one commit- tee they would be far more likely to get better results from such an organization than from such a varied one as had hitherto existed. He would ask those pr 'sent to do what they could to help on bat movement and to overcome any factious opposition that might be offerpd by the people wao were taking part in the municipal trading ag tation. Alderman Monks made a ireference to the demand for a poll made at the meeting n connection with the Lancashire C ,uoty (Asylums and other Powers) Act on Tuesday evening and said that it had the effect of preventing their going on and help- ing forward the bill which was to save money. The person who demanded the poll wanted to pat the town to an exp masa of £250 when the Council was proposing to save money. . _ _</p>
        <p>61-I[lj6)-1:-ii-ENNETT, in reply said their fundamental idea should be to keep the good name of Warrmgton from reproach and make it even more famous than it was. He thought they were sick of hearing the epithets which had been flying about Lancashire during the last few months about Warrington. They had heard it spoken of as "wretched Warrington '2 and were almost sick of hearing the insult which they believed to be absolutely unfounded, or at any rate rested upon a slender foundation. The effen of the things which bad been said made it more incumbent upon them, even if there was no element of truth in the scandals, and he thought there was very, very little, to show that Warrington had after all a noble side and instead of " wretched Warrington " was illustrious Warrington. The toast of " the Chairman 13“1" been honoured the zathering terminated with the singing of " God save the King,"</p>
        <p>Brunner, Mond, and Co. and their Workpeople Another important czmcession has been made by Messrs Brunner, :timid, and Co. to those engaged at their works at Winnington,Lcstock Gralam,Middle- wich, Sandbach, &amp;e, namely, giving them a seven days' holiday and twelvA days' pay, instead of a like holiday with six days' remuneration. This is sure to meet with the appreciation of the several thousand workpeople employed by the firm,and will tend to encourage them to secure the advantage cffered by their regular attendance the works.</p>
      </div>
