      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WARRINGTON CREDITORS IN</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CREDITORS IN</p>
        <p>LUCK.</p>
        <p>20s. in the Pound on an Old Bankruptcy. _— John Garlick's Affairs.</p>
        <p>There was some good news for Warrington creditors at the Counts Court on Thursday last week.—Mr C J Dibb, official receiver in bankruptcy, made an applica- tion before his Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, for an order according to the terms of motion which he sub- mitted, in the case of John Garlick, or John Grant, who was adjudged bankrupt at Warrington in 1896, declar- ing that he (the official receiver) as entitled to all the property which belonged to Garlick at the time of his second bankruptcy at Hull, he then being an undis- charged bankrupt. Mr Dibb cited cases showing his position, and also his title to make the claim, and said that Garlick was in business at Warrington at one time, and was adjudged bankrupt. He was afterwards sent to penal servitue for five years, and it appeared that he escaped fram Stafford Gaol. He then went to Scotland, where he traded successfully in various parts, and amassed some money. Ile drifted to Hull where under the alias of John Brooks he started an important busi- as a box-maker, entering into a contract for the pur- chase of 121 acres of land for the purpose of carrying on his business. Proceedings were instituted in the Hull Court and Garlick having a notion that he was known to be a convict, absconded. In order to get rid of the difficulty the creditors at Hull instituted:prcceedings,and he (Mr Dibb) only became aware of the case through a paragraph in the newspapers. Garlick when he absconded from Hull lett 100 workmen unpaid, and they were so angry that some of them commenced to soil stuff at ridiculous prices in order to get their money. They even threat- ened to burn the place down, Mr Smailes took charge of the affairs, and managed to satisfy the 1 workpcople that their claims would be paid. He (Mr Hitt)) wrote to the official receiver at Hull that as he was trustee for the first bankruptcy he was entitled to claim the assets at Hull. Garlick started business at Hull in the name of Brooks, in the hope that it would not be known that be was an escaped convict. He asked for an order by consent according to the terms of the notice of motion. His H ,nour made an order accordinglY. Mr Dibb said that the assets had already realised £l,OOO, and there was a prospect of mr re. That W uld more than pay the Warrington creditors 20s iOthe pound, with interest, and something would be left for the Hull creditors. Mr Browe stated that the " boots " at tte Lion Hotel investedall his savings • (260) with Garlick in the business at Warrington, Ho (Mr Browne) had been able to trace this man, who would therefore, get his money returned in full.</p>
      </div>
