        <p>WARRINGTON, GEo. WRIGHT (SL SON, LTD., Are one of the oldest Firms of Dyers in the Kingdom, being established in MANCHESTER 1835. LIVERPOOL 1825. LONDON 1790. TRICE LISTS AND ALL INFORMATION CAN BE HAD BY APPLYINO TO OUR BRANCH OFFICE, MA, HORSEMARIKET STREET, goods left with our Agent, Arthur Davidson, 61, bridge Street. (late Lowe and Laithwaite) will receive prompt -attention and be charged the same prices se at our Branch.</p>
        <p>7LetterO to tbe TE,bitor.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr- Joseph Heron.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Kindly allow we through the medium of your paper to make a brief statement on behalf of myself and family. , It has come to my knowledge that a report has been circulated in the town to the effect that my father, the late Mr Joseph Heror, did not receive , prompt and due attention when discovered in an unconscious condition in the Warrington Cemetery on Saturday afternoon last. I hasten to state most emphatically that the said rumour did not gain currency with our approval and indeed we strongly disapprove of any statements made which might in the least degree cast reflection upon those who had the painful duty of ministering to my father's needs during his last hours. On the contrary we beg to thank publicly those who assisted in the manner named and especially do we feel under a deep debt of gratitude to Mr and Mrs Young for the extreme kindness displayed by them. It would hardly have been possible for more to have ben done and the doctor we called in held out not the slightest hope of recovery, the cause of death being ue to acute heart failure. Ia conclusion I can only assume that whoever was responsible for the adverse statements made was entirely misinformed. Your faithfully, JOSEPH HERON.</p>
        <p>February 6th, 3, Willis-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Ministers of Religion and the War.</p>
        <p>Sir,—There seems to be a deep and growing convic- tion in the minds of many Coriatian people that the attitude of most of the etipendiary ministers of religion in relation to the war in South Africa is disappointing and unsatisfactorY —May I ask you for a little space in whist to record some of my views in favour of that conviction ? It is well known that all the ministers to whom I have referred, from the lordly prelate down to the humblest pastor, claim to be the chosen and duly authorised teachers and preachers of the Chriitian religion, a.- though it must be admitted that the grounds for this claim are cowhere strikingly apparent. But, however this may be,it will not be denied that the religioo which these gentlemen are supposed to represent is largely made up of such precepts as the foilowingo—" Thou shalt love the Lord thy Gial with all thy hart, and thy neiehbourlas thyself " ; " Love your enemies, bless them that curse you, do good to them that hate you,and pray for them wtoiah despitefully use you and persecute you," and again, " Whatsoever ye would that men should do unto you, even so do ye unto them, for this is the law and the Prophete." These then are the vital and leading principles of Christianity, and are binding upon all who profess and call themselves Christians. Assuming this to be admitted does it net seem to follow as a logical necessity that the Lord's chosen servant,' , should be most energetic and 11 ft wearyime, in their efforts ' to press home these gracious truths upon the hearts and consciences of all to whom they minister. And surely no language could be more condemnatory of strife arid bloodshedding on the part of Christian men than these Simple and beautiful precepts are. But here, alas, we come to the patting of the ways. ' Whatever the nature of the preaching of our ministers may to under normal circumstances, certain it is that the ministrations of most of them in regard to the war present some of the most remarkable phenomena in the religious world, and God knows there are many remark- able things in that world. For much over two years past the armies of this great Christian country have been endeavouring, day and night, to subjugate or exterminate two small Republics of the same faith as ourselves; and that in the very sight of the heathen who know not God ! And the results, so far as we have been permitted to know thetn,are the loss of many thou- sands of lives, and the infliction of untold distress and sorrow upon multitudes of innocent yeople both here and in Scuth Africa. And how have the chosen and authorised preachers comforted themselves in the awful crisis ? Many of them have openly 'defended and blessed the war as just and righteous, and praised the soldiers for their patriotism in dying for their country, Conveniently forgetting that it was not for their own but for other people's country they made the sacrifice. How would the good preachers like to see such a form of patriotism developed in this country by foreign armies? Then again, some of the preachers have been lukewarm, neither hot nor cold, and have contented themselves by mildly protesting, not against the war itself but against some of its more inhuman methods. And, moreover, there are others who, if one may judge from their silence, are so little interested in the war that they seem to have no views upon it at all. But, so far as I know, no minister of any denomination has yet appeared in a pulpit or on a platform to deliver his message, without the fear or favour of men, that war is:an open and most cruel violation of the laws of Christ and a horrible outrage upon the sanctity of human life. And here a question of the gravest importance pre- sents itself for most serious consideration by Church- men and Nonconformists alike. The land is flo't d over with churches and chaperrathe ministers' name is legion, and the annual cost of maintaining both is simple enor- mous Under these presumably favourable circumstances surely it is no breach of charity to ask how it cemes to pass that the country is suffering as it now is frem the impoverishing and brutalising effects of war ? Can it be that this deplorable and antichriseian state of things is, in some measure, doe to an 'weer&amp; preach- ing of the Gospel ! That Gospel is something very different from forms and ceremonies and words of men's wisdom called Sermona. The fundamental principleate very essence of the Gospel, as taught and exemplified by the ;-.Jord himself, is love to God and man, and where that principle does not reign supreme there may be creeds and ritual but Christianity will be absent. When, therefore we see the chosen and authorised apostles of peace condoning and winkiag at such horrifying spec- tacles as are now being witne3sed by the heathen in South Africa we may, without breach of charity, con- elude that another Gespel than Christ's is their stand- , point. Yours truly. February 1902.</p>
        <p>lONA.</p>
      </div>
