        <p>VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC cures such ailments as indigestion, sluggish liver, con- stipation, wind, weak and painful back, kidney trouble and blood diseases. A genuine bank cheque for 2s 61 accompanies each 21 93 bottle which will be paid to anyone whom Veno's Seaweed Tonic fails to cure. It is a genuine gnat antee and means a cure or no nay. Ask at chemists and medicine vendors for Veno's Seaweed Tonic. Price is lid and 2s 91. Sold by Boots Limited, Warrington • Peake, Earles- town and Newton ; Weston and Co , Runcorn ; Wall. bridge, chemist, J W Jackson, chemist and druggist, St Helens ; and all chemists and medicine vendors every- where.</p>
        <p>Juvenile Temperance in Lancashire and</p>
        <p>Cheshire.</p>
        <p>The Committee of the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope Union, in their thirty-eighth annual report, state that in all departments of its work the Union has made progress, although its efforts were greatly crippled by lack of funds, to supply which an earnest appeal is made to the friends of the movement.—The work of giving science objet lessons on alcohol in the various colleges, high schools, elementary, poor-law,and industrial schools and institutions in Lancashire, Cheshire, and N W Derbyshire, has been most successfully carried on under the direction of the committee, a record year's work having been accomplished. The lectures given during 1901 numbered 1,244. The present strength of the Union is 72 town and district unions, with 1,524 societies, 6,350 voluntary peakers and workers, and 262,000 members. The Union does not contemplate a quiet time in 1902, for its energies are, in addition to the ordinary work, to be devoted : (a). To placing the finances of the general department in a healthier condition ; (b) to raising tbe New Quinquennial Fund for carrying on the science temperance teaching in schools; (c) to reviving (or better organising) the movement in the West Lancashire village districts, also in Mid and South-East Cheshire; and (d) to the erection and provision of new offices for the Union</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>MUNICIPAL</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL</p>
        <p>TRADING.</p>
        <p>The Corporation and an Inquiry.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of the Warrington " Examiner." Sir—The fact that neither of the two reso!utions in reference to an inquiry was adopted at the last meet- ing of the Council seems to indicate that the Council will not unless compelled have any satisfactory inquiry, and yet they complain of the agitation in the town. If there has been nothing wrong the agitation of which they complain might have been brought to an end long ago, be the Corporation agreeing to an outside inde- pendent impartial inquiry, This I contend is what they should have done. For after all that has occurred the ratepayers are justified in urging it. It is not likely that the Corporation will have the full confidence and cordial support of the rate- payers if it does not comply with their request in this matter. Does the Corporation desire by ignoring the claims of the ratepayers to provoke illegalities and slanders and libels, by men who only wish to promote the welfare of the town ? Is this their magnanimity ? A Corporation ought to act both legally and justly to be worthy of respect. It seems to me that the Corporation has acted as if it thought it could defy the rate- payers, but there is in the universe a power I which no men or Corporation or country can defy. It is simply a matter as to how long and to what extent that powEr ty,l permit resistance. The Deputy-Mayor.in a speech more abusive than logical, is reported to have said that he was prepared to submit to a thorough investigation bye art unbiassed inquiry; Will Mr Alder- ' man Roberts vote f,r and support a rEsolution for an outside independent impartial inquiry ? Mr Alderman Roberts' abuse of the non-trading agitators is, of course. no argument, but it seems to indicate that the Corpora- tion has corns that we should like to see removed. But alas I fear that " there is no physician there." H. , speaks of his pride in the place of his birth, but he does not say how much he has traded through &amp; Limited Company with the Corporation of his native to wn. Would not the good name and welfare of the town be as much promoted by a full and impartial inquiry as by some Umited Co:npaniEs trading legally directly or in- direc ly with the Corporation ? Words go with the wind . . .</p>
        <p>Actio IWstav behind.</p>
        <p>Cannot Mr. Alderman Roberts see that the agitators are taking their share of the vsponsibilities of citizens, though not exactly as he would dictate, and that they are performing an unpleasant public duty.Nlr Alderman Robert's statement that " personal vindictiveness and spleen is the whole Cause Of the agitation' is not correct and he has no right to say so. Let me point out that the question for the ratepayers is not the motive of the agitators, bat the procenlings of the Corporation. That is what the ratepayers have to deal with, for the Cor- poration is the responsible serva,t of the rate- payers. I am one of the agitators and I am , sure that his abusive statement is not true. Can he not see how foolish it would be, can he under- , stand no better motive ? If anyone in the Council had wronged or injured me. I would not on that account take part in this agitation, for without my doing so, the monster born of selfishness and sic, would follow him and overtake him and "clasp him in its arms of pain." If in my humble position as a privat3 individual I had not taken part in thia agitation I should have left undone what I have felt to be a public duty. I can expect no advantage to myself except the satisfaction of having played an inflaitely little part in helping to remove the evils of public life, that to me is dearer than any position which atown or a country can give. We could not withoat degradati on quietly submit to tI3I pro- ceedings of the Corporation of this town.—Yours truly, S. KENYON.</p>
        <p>IA Suggestion to end the Agitation.</p>
        <p>To THE EDITOR OF THE "WARRINGTON EXAMINER." have,like many other ratepayers, taken a deep interest in the agitation that is now proceeding in War- rington. ' Although I believe that the Warrington Corporation is to be congratulated on the enterprise it is showing in most of its undertakings and merits some gratitude from the rateparers and inhabitants of the town, yet I thmk over this question of trading it has faile i so far— may I say lamentably failed—to rise to the present occasion. I am prepared to admit it is a trying and , difficult time for the members of the Corporation, but still one expects tMae who are in " high places "to grapple and solve diffilu!t questions. If they cannot, then they are not fit for the positions they occupy. Bat the members of the Warrington Corporation can solve this question—if they only will ; it is not tooldifficult• The trend of public opinion in Eigland is against this question of trading.lt is patent to anyone who takes any interest in municipal and social questions. I need not mention the reasons,for the obi ,ctions have airs d been pointed out, except to emphasise the point that there is the temptation to advocate and run certain ' schemes, which though good in themselves, yet are detrimental and delay others, which are more needed for the bettering and brightening the lot of the dwellers incur towns. For example, in Warrington more is needed to be done in the housing, education, and , recreative question 4. These latter and chief questions affecting tne personal life of the people are, I venture respectfully to submit, too sadly neglected. I have not, so far, taken any part in the present agitation, and am nct in touch with anyone on this question. I can therefore claim to be impartial and free from bias. _ .</p>
        <p>I therefore hog to submit the following suggestion, which, if adopted, would I b.lieyo relieve the town from an inquiry, satiqf v the N-m -Municipal Trading Association(as their object would be thus attained) meet the wishes of the g.neral body of the ratepay r+, as expressed at the meetings, and cave the town from further agitation. "That the Corporation should at the earliest possible moment pass a resoluti m that ia future no contracts should be given to any Limited Company, or which any of its mem- bers are direc or. official, or hold a cu*tain</p>
        <p>numb&amp; or value of aharep." With regard to the certain number or value of ehareq a line can be drawn. - _</p>
        <p>Let us for peace saks and for common sense sake, have no further arguments about the " legality "of trading. Neithrr about the poor shareholders' sake, for I doubt whether they number 5 per cent, of the popula- tion. Besides shareholders are nct dependent upon Corporation bu,ineso, _ . . . . . _</p>
        <p>1 - In concluson it me say that the Warring,ton Corpora- tion have:: a fplendid opportunity of setting a bright example to other Corporations and it also gives certain members of it body the aime opportunity of showing they can mike a little slcri rice fcr the sake of the best interests of the town. And the town would honour them for it. Yours faithfully, E. ENGLAND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Mr Norbury Williams Apologises at Blackpool.</head>
        <p>Mr Norbury Williams Apologises at Blackpool.</p>
        <p>An Unfounded Statement.</p>
        <p>Blackpool has of late been in a ferment on the question of municipal trading, and at a recent meeting of ratepayers Mr. Norbury Williams, Man- chester's elective auditor was the principal speaker. It appears that in the course of his address Mr. Williams made a statement to the effect that a cer- tain alderman held shares in a company known as the Northern Quarries Company, which transacts business with the Corporation, and this turns out to be entirely incorrect. The matter culmina- ted last Wednesda3 in the serving of a writ upon Mr. Williams as he was leaving Parr Hall, Warrington, after addressing a rate- payers' meeting. This called forth an apology from Mr Norbury Williams who, in writing to the " Blackpool Times," said :—" In speaking at the public meeting held in Blackpool on Thursday last, the 30th January, I mentioned the name of the Northern Quarries Company,Limited,and stated that a member of the Blackpool Town Conseil was , a shareholder in that company. I find to-day from ; the reports of the proceedings of the meeting of ' the Town Council of Blackpool, held yesterday,that such is not the case. I had been informed that a member of that Council was the gentleman of the 'same name who is connected with the Northern Quarries Co., Ltd. I now find that this is incorrect and that no member of the Blackpool Council is connected with the Northern Quarries Company. I very greatly regret this mistake, and, as a man of honour, hasten to express my apology to the Northern Quarries Company, and trust that my error has not put them to any inconvenience." We understand that the matter has, in consideration of the apology been allowed to rest, and the case will therefore not come into court. The following leading article on the subject appeared in the " Blackpool Times "under the head- ing "Let us have facts ":—There is no necessity for panic about Mr Norbury Williams and his charges. He has damaged his case irretrievably by having to climb down abjectly over the Northern Quarries Co. If this is a sample of the way in which he flings his statements about, how can his word be taken over the rest of his tirade? He knows very little, about the town, and is evidently at the mercy of col the gossips and tittle-tattlers who, having so little to do in the winter, spend their time in the inventions of such fables as that about the Northern Quarries Co. What sort of knowledge of the town has be been inspired with when he can make the mistake of confusing Alder- man Ward, who has lived in Blackpool practically all his life, with Mr Ward, of the Quarries Company, who is a comparative stranger ? That the Corpora- I tion has been grossly slandered and abused we ad- I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>EXAMINER. FRIDAY FEBRUARY 14, 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. FRIDAY FEBRUARY 14, 1902</p>
        <p>mit, but as we have said before, it has nobody to blame but itself. The "scenes "in the Council de-</p>
        <p>stroy all public respect for it .as a body, while the</p>
        <p>ridiculous vote not only recalling Dr Kingsbury to the Council, but replacing him on the Land Purchase Sub-committee gives the very handle to our critics , which they are seeking for The condemnation of the action of the Council in the Kings- bury affair at the Ratepayers' Meeting on Tuesday night was decisive and emphatic. At the same time, we repeat there is no need for any sort of panic. As for those tales about mortgagees calling in their-money because of the attacks of Mr Nor- bury Williams they may safely be regarded as of the species told to the marines. We don't believe a word of them. As a matter of fact, we hear that the offers of loans to the Corporation have been larger than ever since Mr Williams' sensational attack. Let not our critic give. himself up to de- lusion. We rather like sensations in Blackpool; but we don't believe in them, all the same. There is a good deal of the old Athenians about mil Alderman Heap's defence of the town at Tuesday's Council meeting was complete. The Town Clerk defended the Corporation from the official point of view at the Trademen's Dinner effectively. To all sensible people these are enough. But it may be necessary to defend the ' honour and integrity " ominous words—of the town as well as of the Aldermanic Bench ! If so let us have an enquiry into municipal trading of all sorts, who does it, and how it is done. Let us know all there is to know, and above all, let these critics leave their vague denunciations and give us facts— actual cases of municipal corruption. Then let these instances be enquired into by an im- partial authority. It might be difficult to get such an authority in Blackpool. Then let us get one from outside—say, from the County Council, or from the Local Government Board. The mephi- tic atmdtphere of suspicion and inuendo may drive the best men out of the Town Council. It may be said that we could fill their places. In this con- nection we may say frankly that there are none among Mr Norbury Williams' local sponsors whom the ratepayers would be likely to prefer to the great majority of the present members of the Town Council. We know them all pretty well ! They are posit.vely the most unpopular advocates a can- didate can have at a contested election, It may be sad, bat it is true. Mr Norbury Williams fell into queer hands when he came to Blackpool. Let us hope he will seek batter and more accurate com- pany nest time!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Blackpool's "Corruptness."</head>
        <p>Blackpool's "Corruptness."</p>
        <p>Mr. Norbury Williams' " Foul Aspersion "</p>
        <p>In the course of its COMMenti OR th statement of Mr Norbury Williams that Blackpool is the most corrupt borough lathe United Kingdom, the hlackpuoliiGsCt News ' says :—And so, on the authority of the M in- chester Municipal expert,Blackpool"is the most corrupt borough in the United Kingdom." We 1, we certainly do not feel disposed to take up a brief on behalf of a sot of men who have been fooled and befooled, and have brought the town into such ridicule and conternp: as have the Council over the "Kingsbury affair." Any ordinary words of opprobrium would be fully deserved. But this wholesale charge of corruption, however, is another thing altogether. If Mr Norbury Williams had said th most of the members of the Btackpool Town Council had vertebrae not more substantial than jelly fish, that they were sumvii hie to hypnotic and other influences which showed palpable lack of brain power, that there was a strong taint of cowardice running through them, and that 'toadyism was one of their worst features' we should have saidl"Hear, hear ; go a head." But Mr Williams' speech, while being rather remarkable for what it left unsaid, had " corruption " for its only text and the re- iterated charge that " Blackpool is the most corrupt borough in the United Kingdom certainly cannot be ' allowed to pass unchallengroil. This sweeping condem- nation of the Blackpool Town Council and all its works would have been ever so much more effective had it been accompanied by any attempt at proof. Mr N ir- bury Williams reeled off his charges glibly enough, but no attempt to justify the statements was made. 'Toe fact of the matter is that Mr Norbury Williams or any members of the local purity party will find it an ex- ceedingly difficult matter to prove one single piece of corruption in connection with the Blackp,ol Town Council for a long time past. Indis- cretions, mistakes, foolery, there have been in plenty, but "corruption "is a n.sty w ird that sticks in one's "gizzard." Lot the evidence be forthcoming and the " Augiean Stable of the Cirporation can quickly be cleansed. If Mr Norbury Williame or anyone else know any fact discreiitable to any member of the Corporati let him make it public and end these miserable innuen- doesiand unworthy ass,rtions unsupported by a title of evidence. If Mr Norbury Williams will qualify as a ratepayer of Blackpool we shall have no obj iction to him lo,ing appointed an elective auditorin fae,',wl will undertake to support his candidature. We have n ) inceeests to serve (tier than trlo9l Of the ratepayers generally, and if Mr Norbury Williams csu'd produce s nine solid facts upon wOich to w irk thu action could be taken. If no proof is forthcorninz, thrn Mr N orbury Williams' absurd statement that Black o iol is "the most corrupt borough in the United Kingdom' falls to the ground as being utterly uareliab,e and un- true. As an honourable man, Mr Norbary VVillittni must produce some proof or with ira w his foul asper- sion upon the good name of the Borough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Blackpool Alderman ;Takes up the _ . .</head>
        <p>A Blackpool Alderman ;Takes up the _ . .</p>
        <p>Cudgels.</p>
        <p>Mr. Norbury Williams' designation of Blackpool as "the most corrup: borough in the United Kmgdom " has called forth the strong resentmmt of Alderman J. Heap, Chairman of the Finance Committee of the Cor- poration, who addressed a letter to Mr Williams asking for the particulars upon which he based his conclusion. —Mr Williams replied, declining to accept the accuracy of the newspaper reports, and asking for further infor- mAtion.This provoked Alderman Heap to write a second letter to Mr Williams the otherday, in which he says : Surely you cannot expect that a request for proof of such serious and sweeping assertions is 141.9,; by quibbling over the accuracy of newspaper reports, or . by asking for further information. You say"l have been in Blackpool incognito, dodging round corners, afraid of anyone seeing me, and addressinit inquiries to several members of the Council and private citizens." Alter all these inquiries, attar having drawn your in- ferences th.refore, aril stated them in public, you answer a letter for particular from those aggrieved with a request for still more information _ . . . . . . .</p>
        <p>" I do not wish," says-A—ld—er.Man Eleap in conclusion, " to write a diF.courteous later, but it certainly borders on impudence for you, a non-resident, to make claims for information from tho meinoers of the Blackpool Town Council, who owe you no obligation."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>Praise for the Lancashire Brigade.</p>
        <p>How the Volunteers Worked in South Africa. Warrington people will be gratified to know that the work which Captain Appleton's company of Volunteers performed in South Africa was such as tolhave merited praise from General Sir Redvers Buller. It appears that when Captain Appleton was about to publish his book, " The Volunteer Service Company (lst South Lancashire Regiment) in South Africa," he wrote to General Sir Redvers Buller asking if he might dedicate the volume to him, Sir Redvers was very much from home at that time, seeking the relaxation which he had so well earned in shooting Stu, and in ignoring all Correspondence. Captain Appleton. however, took the liberty of dedicating his work to the head of the Natal Field Force in the following words : 'Dedicated to General Sir Redvers Buller, V.C, G.C.B, soder whom the Company had the,.: honour of serving as 'I ' C,)mpany, Ist South Lances- s ire Regiment, 11th (Lancashire) Brigade, sth Division, Natal Field Force:' In due course Captain Appleton's letter to General 'Buller was answered in the following terms "Downes, Crediton, Devonshire, January 19th, 1902. Dear Captain Appleton,—l am very glad to hear that you are going to record the good work I your Volunteer Company did in South Africa. You I joined that excellent Lancashire Brigade, which it was my good fortune to have under my command, after, it is true, the relief of Ladysmith,but in time to put in as good, as hard, and as varied work as tbe greediest of soldiers could well desire—the operations in the Biggarsberg,the forcing of Botha's Pass, the repairs of the damaged Natal Railway— what better work could be done? opinions differ, and will, I fear always differ, as to how we can most economically maintain as large an Army as the Empire requires. I believe you showed us the best way—namely, to keep up good and strong Volunteer regiments at home ready to fight en masse for the defence of their homes and willing to send out strong companies when wanted as rein- forcements to the Field Army abroad. This is what your Regiment has done, and it is because I know bow well your Company fulfilled its share that I am glad to accept the dedication of your record of its work.—Yours truly, REDTERS BtrLnan.” A copy of this letter is to be placed in all unsold opies of the book,and those who have already pur- chased may,have a copy for insertion on application o C aptain Appleton, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>[Br A CORRESPONDENT]</p>
        <p>Frost, snow, and fog is the order of things in this dis- trict just at present. Old people grumble at it, and wish it would take its departure, whilst the youngsters are hoping for a continuance of the frost at any. rate. They see looming in the distance lots of fun on the ice, and many who are neither old nor young are watching the progress of events at Newton lake. Hera there is plenty of room for all kinds, profes3ionals and novices, and it is some years since the lake was frozen over to allow of safe skating to the full extent of its surface. Thus, by many, it is hoped the frost will continue sufficiently keen to allow of a good measure of enjoy- ment being indulged in on the lake.</p>
        <p>An attempt is on foot to resuscitate the May Queen demonstration and fe9tival in Earlestown, bat this time with a different object in view.</p>
        <p>There is an agitation afloat to try and complete St John's Church, in accordanoe with the original plan,and it is said something like £6,000 will be required. To get such a Wm as this in a working claps district means plenty of hard work, and the May Queen festival is one of the ideas to this end. Such a thing as this needs first of all, fine weather, and a big crowd in the field- after the necessary procession. Already a preliminary° committee has been formed, but if it is intended toe make it a success,the committee should include the ncosto influential of the townsmen who are willing to work for love,and without any idea of securing any pecuniary benefit. Thus there should be no haphazard selections and only the best men invited to ace. It is common knowledge that the squabble amongst the committee was to 90M0 extent responsible for the failure of the last festival. The object for which the affair is to be inaugurated is an exc-Ilent one, and it is hoped i will be attended with success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>ic-Z.ATAL COL:LIER-Cf-ACCIDENT A'</head>
        <p>ic-Z.ATAL COL:LIER-Cf-ACCIDENT A'</p>
        <p>The Coroner and Manager at Loggerheads.</p>
        <p>Mr Brighouse hell an inquest at the Griffin Arms, Bold, on Thursday, on the body of J M'Grath, aged forty-two years, a thy wage-man, of Jackson-street, Buttonwood, who was killed on Monday night last in the Bold Colliery. Samuel Davies said he was work- ing with deceased, arid they were timbering the old road. The roof was about 51t high. They were preparing to put up a spray, when the roof suddenly came down, bringing the sides of the place with it. Witness was knocked away, but deceased was buried in the fall and killed on the spot. The foreman said blth the men were Experienced work- men, and were doing the work properly. The Coroner stated that on Tuesday he received a telegram, which said, " Please give instructions for the removal of the body. Am afraid of trouble with the men if not re- moved." It appears that the body had been laid in the room at the colliery, which is in the township of Bold. The inquest was held at the nearest hotel, which is some miles distant. The coroner complained of the telegram, and asked who sent it.</p>
        <p>Mr Thompson, manager, said the telegram was sent by his instructions. The Coroner said that he had a great number of colliery managers in his district, and never had any trouble with any of them exeept with Mr Thompson. He asked Mr Thompson for the names of any of the men who had complained. Mr Thomps,m replied that his foreman had complained. _ . _ . .</p>
        <p>The Coroner called in two witnesses, and asked them f they had heard of any complaints, or if they had made any. The witnesses replied in the negative. After the coroner had commented on the conduct of Mr Thompson, it was arranged that in future the bodies should remain at the colliery,and the inquest should bo held at the Boundary Vaults, St Helens Junction.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. The Penrhyn Choir Concert The Secretary of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council (Mr F 6tott) informed the members of that body at their monthly meeting on Tuesday evening that the amount received in donations and admission charges in connection with the above concert would be sufficient to defray all expenses, The sum derived from the sale of tickets would therefore po to the Bethesda relief fund. All the money had not yet been sent in by the ticket- sellers but the amount be had received up to date was about XII. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S lIIGH-OLASSMIN BRA L WATB/18.—Advt PING PONG, Latest society game. All prlee GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge-street.—Advt. Mission to the People. The second of a series of Evangelistic Services was held on Sunday evening last in the Parr Hall, and, considering the inclemency of the weather, it was again a decided success. Mr Corbett took for his subject,"The Black Man, Abed Malek and Jeremiah," and gave a very in terestinc, and effective address. Mrs Williams sang with great taste and expression a song out of Sankey's hymn book,which was much appreciated by the congregation. Mr Fred Pod- more, M.l.U,M.,again presided at the organ,and Mr Spruce conducted the choir. The services will be resumed on Sunday next at 8 o'clock in the even- lng.when several good artistes have promised their assistance.</p>
        <p>H H. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC as VIE XING 0?</p>
        <p>TONICS —63, SANKEY-STREET, Warringtoo.—(Advt.) SpT ANGB'S Al CRYSTAL on, the best &amp; safest lampoil.—J, S. CI ultimo. 51 to 55 Bridge St, —Advt</p>
        <p>Warrin2,ton Polyglot Society. The meeting ot the "Polyglots" on Wednesday evening last was of a convivial nature. Mr Thomas May presided and there was a very good attend- ance of members. The programme comprised music trench and German speeches, readings, &amp;c. The musical part found able exponents in Miss May, Miss Forbes and Mrs Ashton augmented by Miss Bouchier's exquisite contributions on the violin. Mr Deßatti gave one or two excellent French read- ings, and selections in French and German 103 Messrs Gerrard. Kertland and Ockel, were very well received. Mr. Ashton's contributions were as usual, of an entertaining' and humorous character and added much to the evoning's enjoyment.</p>
        <p>PAROCHIAL TEA AND ENTERTAINMENT AT NEWTON. . . . _ _</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the annual parochial tea in connection with St Peter's Church, Newton tBok place in the schoolroom. About 150 persons were present and the repast was much enjoyed, the honours of the tables being efficiently carried out by lady members of the congregation. After tea an entertainment was given in the Town Hall,New- ton, the hall being about half filled. The pro- gramme, it appears, had been hurriedly arranged, which may, perhaps, be some excuse for its In- sipidness. The only items which were rendered in anything like a presentable manner, were the glees "Comrades in Arms" and "Roman Soldiers," given by members of the Wigan Male Voice Choir. The other artistes were Miss Lottie Jones and I Adams, Messrs: T Owen, John Sephton,l7 Wagg, J Harris )n, and J Mather. At the close, the Rev J Ryder,vicar, delivered a few remarks in moving a vote of thanks to the artistes and announced the appointment of Mr E Leyland as organist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>GO-OD TEMPLARY.</head>
        <p>GO-OD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The Hope of Earlestown Lodge meeting was held in the Co-operative Hall,NYargrave-road on Monday, Bro Cutler C.T. presiding. Bro Woods read the District Lodge Digest. Bro Cutler C.T., appointed his committees as follows :—Finance Committee: Bros Clarke and Williamson; sick, Sister Williams and Hatton, Bros Newman and: Woods. A special committee was gelected to arrange the quarter's programme and for the visit of the Wesley Hall Lodge. Toe Lodge. was formally closed for Bro and Sister Hatton's "At Home," when a number of friends were invited and a splendid programme was gone through, Bro. Cutler presiding. Items: mandoline solo, "Day dawo," Nir Baxter; song, Come back to me," Miss Stronach ; recitation, "The gambler's wife," Mr J Warburton; song, " A summer shower," Mrs Refreshments provided by Bro and Sister Hatton were then handed round. On resuming Mr Baxter contributed a mandoline solo song, "Joyous life," Miss L Stronach ; recitation, "The station master's story," Mr J Warburton; song, "I cannot sing the old songs," Mrs Williams; a vote of thanks to Bro and Sister Hatton, those taking part in the programme, and to Miss Toombs who nad accompanied, proposed by Mr Woods and seconded by Mr Williamson was carried unani- mously and lthe meeting closed by singing the Doxology.</p>
        <p>THE GLEN SPEY DISTILLERY, situate in the finest ikky-produclng District of Scotland, is tho property of W and A Gilbey. This Whisky Is made entirely from Home-grown Barley and is kept absolutely unblended in. His :Majesty's Bonded Warehouses te mature,and sold by W a d A failbey'o agents in every town at 36 6d per botti . LAMES BEST _GIME_ _BOOTS AND MON</p>
        <p>UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STMT.—Advt. ASR for CUNNINNHAM'S HlGur-oLess MINERAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>H. M ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is THE KINGIOF TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET Warrington.—(Advt UNDBR THE CLOCK FOR ROOD BOOTS.—Adv</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The annual social p.i-o-iii-o-ted-1-3Y—tlie -"teachers of the above Sunday School, took place in the school- room on Thursday evening. There was a fair number present,and after tea,tbe evening was very pleasantly spent in games and singing. ST. JOHN'S CHURCH, EARLESTOWN. The annual Shrove Tuesday social in connection with this church took place in the Misiion flail, on Tuesday evening. The room was well &amp;led, and the refresh- ments consisted of hot-pot of which there was a plentful supply. Dancing and games interspersed with singing cpmprised the evening's enjoyment. _ EXPLOSION AT THE WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning about tialt-pasl ten an explo- sLon occurred in the Wesleyan Chapel at Earlestown in consequence of one of the tubes of the heating appara- tus in the boiler house below the Chapel, bursting. Toe brickwork was considerably damaged, and the window was shattered. Thi-re is no safety valve on the boiler, and the water must have frozen in the pipes. Fortun- ately no one was hurt. - ST. PETER'S CHURCH, NEWTON. . -</p>
        <p>Mr. Elwin Leyland, who f(')r several years has officiated as organist aC Wargrave Church, has been appointed to a simikr position at St. Pettr's Church, Newton, cr.ving to the retirement through ill-health of Dr Middld)o. He will take up his duties on the 9th March neat. It is intsnded to open a Mission at the Crow-lane end of the parish, and Mr P. Fairclough, who has acted as deputy-organist will be appointed organist of the Mission.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>CATHOLIC BILL AT BARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>CATHOLIC BILL AT BARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The sixth annual ball, held under the auspices of the United Irish League (Earlestown branch) took place on Friday evening in the Town flail, Earlestown,and was of a most successful character. The room was very prettily decorated, a number of plants being lent by Mr Randall, Newton. About 240 persons were present, and dancing was spirit- edly kept up until late on Saturday morning, to music sunlied by the L'arlestown Orchestral Society. THE KEEN FROST AT EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The districts of Earlestown and Newton are frost bound this week. All the country round bears a mantle of white, and nearly all the ponds in the neigh- bourhood are frozen over. On the shallow places sliding aod skating were indulged in early on, and towards the week-end the deeper ponds were bearable, and some good sport was available. If the exceptionally keen weather continues, there is no doubt the large sheet of water at Newton will be thronged with skaters. There have been some minor accidents in consequence of the slippery state of the roads although the Dis;rict Council have done what they could to make tne sidewalks safe for pedestrians. On Monday Mrs Lirnond, wife a Mr J Lirnond, of Wargrave-road,fell opposite her residence when crossing the road and fractured her knee cap. The injury was attended by Dr Valentine, but the injured lady will be 801110 considerable time before she can get about again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN DIY SouooLs.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN DIY SouooLs.</p>
        <p>Special services in connection with the above schools were held in the Wesleyan Chapel on Sun- day. Although the weather was most inclemont, there were large congregations. Several items were sung by the scholars, who had been specially trained by Mr Bradley. The morning preacher was the Rev CE Dove,and in the course of his sermon hesaid there were 823 scholars on the books, with an average attendance of 713. They had 0 certificated teachers, 5 assistant and 4 pupil teachers, with 2 special teachers for cookery and needlework. During the year they had been successful in obtaining a Win- wick Foundation Scholarship value £l5 tenable for 2 years. They had three boys at the Warrington and Manchester Grammar Schools, who had won scholarahips under the Lancashire County Council and the Winwick Educational Foundation—The evening preacher was the Rev E C Bocock.—ln the afternoon a service of sacred song entitled " Charles Garrett" was given by the .scholars, assisted by the members of the choir, the connective readings being given by Mr J H Profit. Collections in aid of the schools were made at each service. RAILWAY ACCIDENT NEAR NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Monday night both lines of rails between Manchester and Liverpool were b!ocked for about three hours in consequence of three waggons,loaded with coal, leaving the metals at Parkside near Newton-le-Willows. A coal train from Sanderson's siding to Widnes had to draw across the lines into a siding at Parkside in order to allow the 5 15 p m express Manchester to Liverpool to pass. After its passage the coal train backed out, and when pro- pelling the waggons on to the main line, three waggons:came off the rails, from some unexplained cause. Serious dislocation of the passenger service was caused, no trains from Liverpool getting fur- ther than Nowtont whiisF, no train from Mannhester passed through before 8 30. Several of the import- ant express t,ains had to be diverted via St Helens and Wigan, and much delay was cauerl by working others over the triangle at Lowton. The fog came on very thickly, and this added considerably ts the difficulties and to the inconvenience of the trivel- ling public. The breakdown gang from Warrington arrived on the scene shortly atter seven o'clock,but it was nearly two hours later before they got the waggons out of the way.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S UHIFECH KARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S UHIFECH KARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The annual parochial tea and concert in connec- tion with the above church took place on Saturday evening in the Mission Hall, Earlestown. About 200 persons sat down to an excellently served tea, several lady members of the congregation presiding at toe tables. The subsequent proceedings were presided over by the. Vicar, Rev T R Matthews,who said they were not sleeping in St John's Parish, although they might be much more alive than they were. Still, he was thankful to say they were making progress. In the Sunday school they had some 1470 scholars and it had been said it. was one of the largest schools in the Liverpool diocese. There was one detriment to the bringing up of children in this place, and that was the selling of newspapers on Sunday. He did not think parents derived very much benefit from the few pence earned in this way. A meeting would shortly be held in connection with the com- pletion of the Church, for which about £6,000 would be required. The entertainment consisted of songs, quartettes, sketches, tableaux and instru- mental items. The tableaux were very well arranged, the illustrations being " Eugland,lreland, Scotland and Wales," "The Gipsy's Warning," "Seasons," and "Greek Scene." A plantation scene by several members of the congregation in nigger costume was capitally presented and heartily applauded. Mr J Harrison presided at the piano. WARGRAVE CHURCIT PARCCIIIAL TEA AND</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>CONCERT.</head>
        <p>CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The annual Shrove Tuesday tea and concert in connection with the Wargrave Church, took place on Tuesday evening. The inclement weather mili- tated against the attendance, and consequently only abou&amp; 100 people put in an appearance at tea A very tattefully verved and substantial meal was provided, the honours of the tables being carried out by Mesdames, Carron, Cave, Sweetlove, Mols- neux (Market-street), Molyneux (Wargrave-road), Parnell, Lawson, Blackburn, John,on, and Miss Carson. The decorating of the room was performed by Mrs Cbandley and ),liss Molyneuy. After tea a concert was given by the Wargrave Amateur troupe of minstrels, who gave in capital style the following programme:—duet mandoline and piano, song" ulroonys dog " song "Tell her I love her so," song "The Picaninny's prayer," bone solo, "The express,' song, " The Yeller Coon ;" violin solo; Historical Sketch, " How Bill Adams won the Battle of Waterloo;" song, "The Mulligan Guards song, "Dolly Grey ;" song, "John Bull's letter bag;'' song, "My old Kentucky home;" song. " Marguerite ;" chorus " Good Night." The re- mainder of the evenino. until 12 o'clock was spent in dancing,the affair being most enjoyable through- out.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN POACIIR a THREATENS A KE HPER</p>
        <p>At Newton Petty Sessions, on Thursday, before Messrs. T J Gillespie and R Stone. James Good- win, of 9, Vitriol-square, Earlestown, was charged with game trespass at Winwick. Mx A E White appeared for complainant, George Robert Lee, and said that on the 20th ult., the defendant and an- other man, each carrying a gun, were on a field in 3 e broad daylight at Winwick, belonging t Elln Fairclaugh. Lee; the keeper, concealed him:elf, and saw the men fire three shots at a hare, and afterwards go to it and pick it up. The keeper went up to the men who said they had a game licence. Wisen questioned as to the hare, defendant pulled it out •f his pocket,. Both men gave wrong names and addresses, and threatened the keeper. Igyilence was given by Lee and Constable Schofield, who went with Lee to defendant's house on January 24.—The defendant said he was not there on the day in question, and the keeper never taw him. He was away at Wigan from eleven o'clock in the morning until hall-past three.—The bench said that it was a very bad case—as bad a case as had been brought before them. He (defendant) first went poaching stole a hare, took it away, gave a wrong name, and threatened tee keeper. He would be fined 20s and costs, and if he bad been up before they would have given him a much heavier fine. They allowed the advocate's fee</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>LATE MR. C. J. HOLMES.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. C. J. HOLMES.</p>
        <p>THE FUNERAL. With a quiet simplicity characteristic of the Society of Friends, the remains of the late Mr. Charles John Holmes were interred in the Friends' Burial Ground, Penketh, at noon on Thursday. The country around looked picturesque in its white mantle of snow, but the keenness of the frost was felt in all its severity on the bleak burial ground and the service at the graveside was, wisely, brief. The ground adjoins the Friends' Meeting House, and the uniform pattern and size of all the headstones distinguishes it from ,oiher burial grounds. At the invitation of the Mayor, the members of the Council, magiitrates and officials of the Corpora- tion, attended tne funeral with the object of paying a fitting tribute of respect to the memory of the deceased gentleman. The Mayor was unable to be present, but the following responded to the invitation :—The Ex-Mayor (Alderman Robert), Alderman Faircloug.h, Alderman Hutchin ' son Councillors Smethurst, Bennett, Rogers, W iForshaw,B Davies, T B Malkin, Mr lhomas May, Mr John Fairclough, J.P., with Messrs J Lyon Whittle (town clerk), T Longdin (borough surveyor), W S Haddock (gas engineer), G F F White (magistrate's clerk.) Toe following gentlemen attended as a deputa- tion from the People's College School :—Mr James F Dutton, Mr JR Young and Mr Edward Wallington. The relatives and friends who gathered round the grave were Mr and Mrs C Jackson Holmes (son and daughter-in-law), Mr Joshua Smitason (nephew), Miss Hoegkinson (niece), Mr J Foster Jackson (nephew), Mr and Mrs James Arthur Jackson (nephew and niece), Mrs Sutton (Manchester), Mr John Ashworth (Manchester), Mr Alfred Bradshaw (Manchester), Mr W E Brown, B A. and Mrs Brown(headmaster and headmistress of Penketh Friends' School), Miss Wane (PeOketil School), Mr Walker Unsworth (Manchester), Mr Jame: Turner (Manchester), Mr Dyke A Fox (Birkeninad), Mr ft Garnett, JP,Mr C E Parker and Mr William Daven port (Peniteth), Mr W A Jeans (Sankey), Mr end Mrs James Rawlinson (Warrington), Mrs William Derbyshire (:iouthport), Mr and Mrs Robert Nightingale (Warring- ton). De Murray, Mr John Collinson, Mr Booth (South- port), Mr F J Whittle, and Mr A J Willett. The service at the graveside was conducted by Mr Dyko A Fox and was brief and impressive. The coffin was of fumed oak with brass mountings. There was no plate or inscription on the lid and by request, there were no flowers. As soon as the coffin had been lowered into the grave the mourners and friends:pro- ceeded to the Meeting House where a service was con- ducted. The pupils from the Penketh School were also present. Like the exterior of the building, the inside is remarkably plain. There is no attempt at ornamenta- tion and a raised platform at the end of the building serves as the pulpit or rostrum for the conductors of the meeting. , After an impressive silence for a few minutes, prayer was offered by Mr John Asaworth. An address was then delivered by Mr Dyke A Fox, based upon the text: " I am the way the truth and the Life." It was a privi- lege of the highest kind, he said, to be permitted to in- vite their fellowmen in His name to know the truta and to be partakers of the life to which He was and is and ever will be the way. After referring to the necess- ity of everyone knowing Christ and beginning now that MO of the soul in Christ which knows no end, the preacher said they had seen their old friend, Mr Holmes laid in his grave that morning and close to him was the grave of that young lad who was so suddenly cut off at the railway ststion a few weeks ago. There the young and the old were laid to rest. There was one Saviour for young and old and Only one. There was only one way, one truth, one life. It was a blessed thing to think of their friends who had laid hold of the simple message of Christ and were now receiving the blessings of so doing. He hoped there would not be one in the company present who on that great day would be left out of the kingdom. . .</p>
        <p>Mr John Ashworth, of Manchester, prefaced an address:with a quotation from Longfellow's " Psalm of Life."</p>
        <p>" Lives of great men all remind us We can make our lives sublime, And, departing, leave behind us Footprints on the Bands of time' They had, he said, seen the last of their friend, Charles John Holmes, and in looking back upon his life there wa3 much that many of them could learn lessons from and receive encouragement. They know he was quiet in his demeanour ; he had not a great deal to say ; yet those of them who had, even for a short time,come into contact with him had been benefited by such contact. He had not onl3 faithfully done his duty in private life and in connection with the school with which he took a special interest, but he had also been a useful citizen of Warrington. There were such men who were willing not only to serve their follow-men in connection with the church with which they belonged, but also willing to servo the town in which they lived to the honour and glory of God. They could look back upon the life of their departed friend with satisfaction. It was an incentive to those who were left behind to be willing to do their duty and to servo in what- ever capacity they might be called „" upon, either in public life or private life, and to be faithful as unto God. He thought they had a good example in their late friend Charles John Holmes. When they looked back upon his life it reminded them that they should do their duty not only to 'their fellow-men but also to their God. He hoped the example that they had in the life of their late friend would encourage them to be as faithtul in whatever calling they followed as he had boon upon earth. Then they would also feel that they could go from their work to their Rest, feeling that God had been with them and that His peace would rest upon each one of them. After prayer had been offered, the proceedings ter- minated. The funeral arrangements were made by Messrs Holmes and Booth, the duties largely devolving upon Mr T. Greaves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>DRAUGHTS.</head>
        <p>DRAUGHTS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON D.C. v. RICHMOND'S D.C. A draughts match between the above clubs was played at the Pelican Hotel on fuesday evening, which ended in a draw. The members of the Warrington Draughts Club entertained their visitors with a smoking concert, which was thoroughly el jlped.—aongs were rendered by Mr F Warburton, Mr J Burke, Mr W O'Brien and Mr Butterworth. Mr Pemberton accompanied in a very effective manner. Result :</p>
