        <p>WARRINGTON AND THE CORONATION. Some Suggestions.—Others Invited? A meeting of the Parliamentary C)mmittee of the Warrin.ton Corporation was held uoder the presidency of the Mayor at the Town Hall on Monday and several suggestions were considered as to the way in which the coronation Of MS msjeAy the king should be celebrated in Warrington. It was decided that an illuminated address should be presented to His majesty and the further consideration of the followine matter. was adjourned to a meeting to be held on the 28th instant. (1). A public pr cession in which the Friendly cieties and the Trades and Labour Cou,icil and others be ask- d.to c.,-operate on the clay of the Coronation. (2). A procession of children to take place on the following day. (3). A treat to the ageil poor. (4). A treat to children. (8). That commemoration nr.idals be struck. (6). That all public buildings in the town be decor- ated and illuminated. (7). That a public dinner be held. (8). Trial a statue of Her late Majesty be obtained. (9). That scholarships be formed in the technical schools for elementary scholars. (10 ) That a public meeting be held at which thc ab ive and other proposals might be considered. We are requested to state that the Committee will be glad to:receive the views of the ratepayers on the above proposals and any further sugge tons they may choose to make. " Charles Garrett" Temperance Union Ellesmere-street. A tea party and concert were held in the Elles- mere-street Wesleyan Metho list Mission on Satur- day in aid of the sale of work funds. Over 100 persons were present. After tea the room was cleared for the concert which was presided over by Mr A Perkins. A capital programme was gone through, an i several encores were demanded, the artistes being Mr Albert Spruce's concert party, which included Miss Crompton, Miss Sumner, Mr A Spruce, Mr J Corrin, Mr A E Burdett, Mr S Row- lands, and jr Greenough.</p>
